// Полнотекстовый поисковый индекс — генерируется скриптом scripts/build_search_index.py
window.SEARCH_INDEX = [
  {t:'About — International Logistics', u:'about.html', text:'Home → About About My name is Vadim Alexandrovich Chirkov — an international logistics specialist with over 30 years of experience. I graduated from the Ural Electromechanical Institute of Railway Transport Engineers in 1994 with a degree in "Organisation and Management in Railway Transport". In 2006 I obtained additional education in "Management" at the management training courses for the national economy at Perm State University. Experience My operational experience in logistics is over 30 years: railway transport management (my core speciality), international supply, supply chains and customs clearance. I have direct hands-on practice with all modes of transport — both in Russia and in the countries of Africa and the Middle East. International projects I have participated in a number of international projects: Libya — construction of a high-speed railway; Nigeria — the ALSCON aluminium production; Dubai/Iraq — LUKOIL Overseas; DR Congo — a trading company supplying mines "turnkey"; Guinea — a unique project to restore the FRIGUIA SA alumina plant; Sierra Leone — a mining enterprise. Digital tools and analytics In addition to operational work, I have implemented digital tools: a logistics module based on 1C (not an out-of-the-box version); a transport module based on Microsoft Dynamics ERP — a working tool for expediting; direct participation in the preparation of the implementation of a SAP TMS. For data analysis I work fluently with Power Query and Power BI: I built daily KPI monitoring, a report on transit time and the share of logistics in the cost of delivery, and automated the forecast of payments to contractors 12 weeks ahead. AI in my work I actively use AI in my work: I have created and maintain two of my own AI agents (on a Raspberry Pi and a laptop, with memory synchronisation), and work with several language models. With their help I have automated routine tasks — document parsers (PDF→Excel, upload to MSD365), automatic calculation of the spot logistics budget, mailbox processing and others.'},
  {t:'Air freight — International Logistics', u:'air-freight.html', text:'Home → Air freight Air freight Air transport is the fastest way to deliver cargo, used for urgent and high-value shipments. Below are materials on consignment documents and the regulatory framework for air carriage. Consignment documents Air Waybill (AWB): MAWB and HAWB, issuance specifics, chargeable weight. Regulatory framework Warsaw and Montreal Conventions, IATA rules, ICAO (Chicago Convention).'},
  {t:'Chartering abbreviations — International Logistics', u:'chartering-abbreviations.html', text:'Home → Chartering abbreviations Chartering abbreviations A dictionary of abbreviations and terms used in ship chartering and international maritime logistics. Сокращение Расшифровка / значение AA Always Afloat AAAA Always Accessible Always Afloat AAOSA Always Afloat or Safe Aground. Condition for a vessel whilst in port AARA Amsterdam-Antwerp-Rotterdam Area ABAFT Toward the rear (stern) of the ship. Behind. ABOARD On or within the ship ABOVE DECK On the deck (not over it - see ALOFT) ABT About ADCOM Address Commission ADDENDUM Additional chartering terms at the end of a charter party AFSPS Arrival First Sea Pilot Station (Norway) AFFREIGHTMENT The hiring of a ship in whole or part AFT At or towards the stern or rear of a ship AGROUND Touching or fast to the bottom AGW All Going Well AHL Australian Hold Ladders AIDS TO NAVIGATION Artificial objects to supplement natural landmarks indicating safe and unsafe waters ALOFT Above the deck of the ship AMIDSHIPS In or toward the centre of the ship ANCHORAGE A place suitable for anchorage in relation to the wind, seas and bottom ANTHAM Antwerp-Hamburg Range APS Arrival Pilot Station ARAG Amsterdam-Rotterdam--Antwerp-Gent Range ARBITRATION Method of settling disputes which is usually binding on parties. A clause usually in a charter party A/S Alongside ASBA American Shipbrokers Association ASPW Any Safe Port in the World ASTERN In the back of the ship, opposite of ahead ATDNSHINC Any Time Day/Night Sundays and Holidays Included ATHWARTSHIPS At right angles to the centreline of the ship ATUTC Actual Times Used to Count BACKLETTER Where a seller/shipper issues a &#x27;letter of indemnity&#x27; in favour of the carrier in exchange for a clean bill of lading BAF Bunker Adjustment Factor. A Fuel Surcharge expressed as a percentage added or subtracted from the freight amount, reflecting the movement in the market place price for bunkers. BALE CAP. Cubic capacity of a vessels holds to carry packaged dry cargo such as bales/pallets BALLAST Heavy weight, often sea water, necessary for the stability and safety of a ship which is not carrying cargo BALLAST BONUS Compensation for relatively long ballast voyage BAREBOAT CHTR. Bareboat Charter - Owners lease a specific ship and control its technical management and commercial operations only. Charterers take over all responsibility for the operation of the vessel and expenses for the duration. BBB Before Breaking Bulk. Refers to freight payments that must be received before discharge of a vessel commences BDI Both Dates Inclusive BEAM The maximum breadth or the greatest width of a ship BELOW Beneath the deck BENDS Both Ends (Load & Discharge Ports) BI Both Inclusive BIMCO The Baltic and International Maritime Council BL1 Bale BL2 (Bill of Lading) A document signed by the carrier which acts as a Contract of Affreightment, a receipt and evidence of title to the cargo. BM Beam BN Booking Note BOB Bunker on Board BOFFER Best Offer BOW The forward part of a ship BROB Bunkers Remaining on Board BROKERAGE Percentage of freight payable to broker (by owners in c/p&#x27;s) or applicable to sale or purchase BSS Basis BSS 1/1 Basis 1 Port to 1 Port BT Berth Terms BULKHEAD A vertical partition separating compartments BUNDLING This is the assembly of pieces of cargo, secured into one manageable unit. This is relevant to items such as Structural Steel, Handrails, Stairways etc. Whilst this is a very flexible description, a rule of thumb is to present cargo at a size easily handled by a large (20 tonne) fork lift. BUNKERS Name given for vessels Fuel and Diesel Oil supplies (Originates from coal bunkers) BUOY An anchored float used for marking a position on the water or a hazard or a shoal and for mooring BWAD Brackish Water Arrival Draft CAF Currency Adjustment Factor CBM Cubic Metres CBFT (or CFT) Cubic Feet CFR (or C&F) Cost and Freight CHART A map used by navigators CHOPT Charterers Option CHTRS Charterers CIF Cost, Insurance & Freight. Seller pays all these costs to a nominated port or place of discharge. CKD Completely knocked down COA Contract of Affreightment - Owners agree to accept a cost per revenue tonne for cargo carried on a specific number of voyages. CIP Carriage and Insurance paid to... COACP Contract of Affreightment Charter Party COB Closing of Business COBLDN Closing of Business London COD Cash On Delivery COGSA Carriage of Goods by Sea Act CONGESTION Port/berth delays CONS Consumption C/SNEE CONSIGNEE. Name of agent, company or person receiving consignment COP Custom Of Port CP (or C/P) Charter Party CPD Charterers Pay Dues CPT Carriage Paid To CQD Customary Quick Despatch CR Current Rate CROB Cargo Remaining on Board CRN Crane CRT Cargo Retention Clauses, introduced by charterers based on shortage of delivered cargo because of increased oil prices CST Centistoke CTR Container Fitted DA Disbursement Account DAF Deliver At Frontier DAPS Days all Purposes (Total days for loading & discharging) DAMFORDET Damages for Detention. Penalty if cargo is not ready when ship arrives for working (1st day of Laycan). This is not detention which is charged for ships time on delay. If the cargo is ready there is no DAMFORDET. DDU Delivered Duty unpaid. DDP Delivered Duty Paid. DECK A permanent covering over a compartment, hull or any part thereof DEM Demurrage (Quay Rent). Money paid by the shipper for the occupying port space beyond a specified &quot;Free Time&quot; period. DEQ Delivered Ex Quay DES Delivered Ex Ship DESP Despatch. Time saved, reward for quick turnaround- in dry cargo only DET Detention (See DAMFORDET) DEV Deviation. Vessel departure from specified voyage course DFRT Deadfreight. Space booked by shipper or charterer on a vessel but not used DHDATSBE Despatch Half Demurrage on All Time Saved Both Ends DHDWTSBE Despatch Half Demurrage on Working Time Saved Both Ends DISCH Discharge DK Deck DLOSP Dropping Last Outwards Sea Pilot (Norway) DO Diesel Oil DOLSP Dropping Off Last Sea Pilot (Norway) DOP Dropping Outward Pilot DOT Department of Transport DNRCAOSLONL Discountless and Non-Returnable Cargo and/or Ship Lost or Not Lost DRAUGHT (or DRAFT) Depth to which a ship is immersed in water. The depth varies according to the design of the ship and will be greater or lesser depending not only on the weight of the ship and everything on board, but also on the density of the water in which the ship is lying. DRK Derrick DUNNAGE Materials of various types, often timber or matting, placed among the cargo for separation, and hence protection from damage, for ventilation and, in the case of certain cargoes, to provide space in which the tynes of a fork lift truck may be inserted. DWAT (or DWT) Deadweight. Weight of cargo, stores and water, i.e. the difference between lightship and loaded displacement. EBB A receeding current EC East Coast EIU Even If Used ELVENT Electric Ventilation ETA Estimated Time of Arrival ETC Estimated Time of Completion ETD Estimated Time of Departure ETS Estimated Time of Sailing EXW Ex Works FAC Fast as can FAS Free Alongside Ship. Seller delivers goods to appropriate dock or terminal at port of embarkation and buyer covers costs and risks of loading FCA Free to Carrier. A modern equivalent of FAS used in intermodal transport where goods are transferred at a nominated forwarders premises, depot or terminal but not actually on board vessel. FD (FDIS) Free Discharge FDD Freight Demurrage Deadfreight FDESP Free Despatch FDEDANRSAOCLONL Freight Deemed Earned, Discountless And Non-Returnable (Refundable) Ship And Or Cargo Lost Or Not Lost FENDER A cushion, placed between ships, or between a ship and a pier, to prevent damage FEU Standard 40’ Container FHEX Fridays/Holidays Excluded FHINC Fridays/Holidays Included FILO Free In/Liner Out. Seafreight with which the shipper pays load costs and the carrier pays for discharge costs. FIO Free In/Out. Freight booked FIO includes the seafreight, but no loading/discharging costs, i.e. the charterer pays for cost of loading/discharging cargo. FIOS Free In/Out Stowed. As per FIO, but excludes stowage costs. FIOST Free In/Out and Trimmed. Charterer pays for cost of loading/discharging cargo, including stowage and trimming. FIOT Free In/Out and Trimmed. As per FIOS but includes trimming, e.g. the levelling of bulk cargoes. FIOS includes seafreight, but excludes loading/discharging and stowage costs. FIT Free In Trimmed FIW Free In Wagon FIXING Chartering a Vessel FIXTURE Conclusion of shipbrokers negotiations to charter a ship - an agreement FLATPACKING Cargo to be presented stacked and secured as an integral unit. FLT Full Liner Terms FMC Federal Maritime Commission FME Force Majeure Excepted FMS Fathoms FO1 For Orders FO2 (IFO) Fuel Oil/Intermediate FO FO3 Free Out FOB Free on Board. Seller sees the goods &quot;over the ship’s rail&quot; on to the ship which is arranged and paid for by the buyer FOFFER Firm Offer FOG For Our Guidance FOQ Free On Quay FOR Free On Rail FORCE MAJEURE Clause limiting responsibilities of the charterers, shippers and receivers of cargo. FORE-AND-AFT In a line parallel to the keel FORWARD Toward the bow of the ship FOT Free On Truck FOW1 First Open Water FOW2 Free On Wharf FP Free Pratique. Clearance by the Health Authorities FR First Refusal. First attempt at best offer that can be matched FREEBOARD The minimum vertical distance from the surface of the water to the gunwale FRT Freight. Money payable on delivery of cargo in a mercantile condition FREE DESPATCH If loading/discharging achieved sooner than agreed, there will be no freight money returned. FREE EXINS Free of any Extra Insurance (Owners) FREE OUT Free of discharge costs to owners. Includes seafreight only. FRUSTRATION Charterers when cancelling agreement sometimes quote &#x27;doctrine of frustration&#x27; i.e. vessel is lost, extensive delays. FWAD Fresh Water Arrival Draft FWDD Fresh Water Departure Draft FYG For Your Guidance FYI For Your Information GA General Average GEAR A general term for ropes, blocks, tackle and other equipment GLS (GLESS) Gearless GNCN Gencon (GENERAL CONDITIONS) GN (or GR) Grain (Capacity) GO Gas Oil GP Grain Capacity. Cubic capacity in &#x27;grain&#x27; GR Geographical Rotation. Ports in order of calling GRD Geared GRT Gross Registered Tonnage GSB Good, Safe Berth GSP Good, Safe Port GTEE Guarantee GUNWALE The upper edge of a ship&#x27;s sides 2H Second Half HA Hatch HAGUE RULES Code of minimum conditions for the carriage of cargo under a Bill of Lading HATCH An opening in a ship&#x27;s deck fitted with a watertight cover HBF Harmless Bulk Fertilizer HDLTSBENDS Half Despatch Lay Time Saved Both Ends HDWTS Half Despatch Working (or Weather) Time Saved HHDW Handy Heavy d.w. (Scrap) HIRE T/C Remuneration HMS Heavy Metal Scraps HO Hold HOLD A compartment below deck in a large vessel, used solely for carrying cargo HULL The main body of a ship HW High Water ICW Intercoastal Waterway : bays, rivers, and canals along the coasts (such as the Atlantic and Gulf of Mexico coasts), connected so that vessels may travel without going into the sea IMDG International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code IMO International Maritime Organisation IN &/OR OVER Goods carried below and/or on deck IND Indication INTERMODAL Carriage of a commodity by different modes of transport, i.e. sea, road, rail and air within a single journey INCOTERMS (Refer to comments in covering statement on front page A-F) ITF International Transport Workers Federation (Trade Unions). Complies on crewing ITINERARY Route.Schedule IU If Used IUHTAUTC If Used, Half Time Actually To Count IWL Institute Warranty Limits KEEL The centreline of a ship running fore and aft; the backbone of a vessel KNOT A measurement of speed equal to one nautical mile (6,076 feet) per hour LANE METER A method of measuring the space capacity of Ro/Ro ships whereby each unit of space (Linear Meter) is represented by an area of deck 1.0 meter in length x 2.0 meters in width. LASH To hold goods in position by use of Ropes, Wires, Chains or Straps etc. LAT Latitude. The distance north or south of the equator measured and expressed in degrees. LAYCAN Laycan (Layday Cancelling Date) LAYTIME Time at Charterers disposal for purpose of loading/discharging L/C Letter of Credit LCR Lowest Current Rate LEE The side sheltered from the wind LEEWARD The direction away from the wind. Opposite of windward LEEWAY The sideways movement of the ship caused by either wind or current LF Load Factor. Percentage of cargo or passengers carries e.g. 4,000 tons carried on a vessel of 10,000 capacity has a load factor of 40% LIEN Retention of property until outstanding debt is paid LNG Liquefied Natural Gas LOA Length Overall of the vessel LOAD LINE SEE PLIMSOLL LINE LOF Lloyds Open Form LOG A record of courses or operation. Also, a device to measure speed LOI Letter of Indemnity LONGITUDE The distance in degrees east or west of the meridian at Greenwich, England LOW Last Open Water LS (or LUMPS) Lumpsum Freight. Money paid to Shipper for a charter of a ship (or portion) up to stated limit irrespective of quantity of cargo LSD Lashed Secured Dunnaged LT1 Liner Terms LT2 Long Ton = 1,016.05 kilogram (2,240 lbs) LTHH Liner Terms Hook/Hook LW Low Water LYCN Laycan (Layday Cancelling Date) MANIFEST Inventory of cargo on board MB Merchant Broker MDO (DO) Marine Diesel Oil MIDSHIP Approximately in the location equally distant from the bow and stern MIN/MAX Minimum/Maximum (cargo quantity) MOA Memorandum of Agreement MOLCHOPT More or Less Charterers Option MOLOO More or Less Owners Option MOORING An arrangement for securing a ship to a mooring buoy or pier MT Mertic Tonne (i.e. 1,000 kilos) M/V Motor Vessel / Merchant Vessel NAABSA Not Always Afloat But Safely Aground NM Nautical Mile. One minute of latitude; approximately 6,076 feet - about 1/8 longer than the statute mile of 5,280 feet NAVIGATION The art and science of conducting a ship safely from one point to another NCB National Cargo Bureau NESTING Implies that cargo is presented stacked in the contour of similarly shaped cargo, it may be likened to a stack of plates. This is particularly relevant in the presentation of tankage strakes for transport NON-REVERSIBLE (Detention). If loading completed sooner than expected, then saved days will not be added to discharge time allowed. NOR Notice of Readiness NRT Net Restricted Tonnage NYPE New York Produce Exchange OO Owners Option OBO Ore/Bulk/Oil Vessel OSH Open Shelter Deck OVERBOARD Over the side or out of the ship OWS Owners P&I Protection and Indemnity Insurance PASTUS Past Us PC Period of Charter PCGO Part Cargo PCT Percent PDPR Per Day Pro Rata PERDIEM By the Day PER SE By Itself PHPD Per Hatch Per Day PLIMSOLL MARK / PLIMSOLL LINE / LOAD LINE An internationally recognised line painted on the side of merchant ships. When a ship is loaded, the water level is not supposed to go above the line. Water can reach different parts of the line as its temperature and saltiness varies with the season and location. From where Plimsoll Shipping derived its name. PORT The left side of a ship looking forward. A harbour. PRATIQUE Licence or permission to use a port PREAMBLE Introduction to a charter party PROFORMA Estimated Account PUS Plus Us PWWD Per Weather Working Day RCVR Receiver RECAP Recapitulation of the terms and conditions agreed REVERSIBLE (Detention). If loading completed sooner than expected at load port, then days saved can be added to discharge operations. ROB Remaining On Board RT Revenue Tonne (i.e. 1.0 metric tonne or 1.0 cubic meter, whichever greater). The overall RT is calculated on a line by line basis of the Packing List using the largest amount. The overall freight liability is calculated on the total RT amount, multiplied by the freight rate. SATPM Saturday P.M. SB Safe Berth SD (or SID) Single Decker SEAFREIGHT Costs charged for transporting goods over the sea. This does not cover haulage or loading/discharging costs but the sea transport only SEAWORTHINESS Statement of condition of the vessel (valid certificates, fully equipped and manned etc.) SELFD Self Discharging SEMI-TRAILERS Are usually 12.0 meter flat bed road trailers SF Stowage Factor. Cubic space (measurement tonne) occupied by one tonne (2,240 lbs/1,000 kgs) of cargo SHINC Sundays/Holidays Included SHEX Sundays/Holidays Excluded SKIDS Are bearers (timber or steel) positioned under the cargo to enable forklift handling at port, and for ease of rigging and lashing on board ship. SN Satellite Navigation - A form of position finding using radio transmissions from satellites with sophisticated on-board automatic equipment SOC Shipper Owned Container SOF Statement of Facts SP Safe Port SPIDERING Is the strengthening of circular tanks for transport, this prevents the tanks from becoming warped. The tanks are strengthened with steel or wood crossbeams giving a &quot;spider&quot; appearance SRBL Signing and Releasing Bill of Lading SSHEX Saturdays, Sundays, Holidays Excluded SSHINC (or SATSHINC) Saturdays, Sundays, Holidays Included STABILITY It is paramount that a vessel is stable in all aspects at all times. When cargo is loaded/discharged, the stability is monitored by a computer, which takes into account the weight and position of cargo within the vessel. STARBOARD Right side of a ship when facing the front or forward end. STEM Subject to Enough Merchandise (Availability of cargo). Also, the forward most part of the bow. STERN The aformost or after part of a ship SUB Subject (to). Depending upon as a condition SUPERCARGO Person employed by a ship owner, shipping company, charterer of a ship or shipper of goods to supervise cargo handling operations. Often called a port captain. SWAD Salt Water Arrival Draft SWDD Salt Water Departure Draft THWARTSHIPS At right angles to the centreline of the ship TIDE The periodic rise and fall of water level in the oceans TIME BAR Time after which legal claims will not be entered TBN To Be Named / To Be Nominated TC Time Charter - Owners agree to hire a particular ship for a set length of time and provide technical management, crewing etc. TCP Time Charter Party TEU Standard 20&#x27; Container TOPSIDES The sides of a ship between the waterline and the deck; sometimes referring to onto or above the deck TRIM Fore and aft balance of a ship TTL Total TW Tween Decker USC Unless Sooner Commenced UU Unless Used UUIWCTAUTC Unless Used In Which Case Time Actually Used To Count VPD Vessel Pays Dues WATERLINE A line painted on a hull which shows the point to which a ship sinks when it is properly trimmed WAY Movement of a ship through water such as headway, sternway or leeway WCCON Whether Customs Cleared Or Not WIBON Whether In Berth Or Not WIFPON Whether In Free Pratique Or Not WINDWARD Toward the direction from which the wind is coming WIPON Whether In Port Or Not WLTOHC Water Line-To-Hatch Coaming WOG Without Guarantee WP Weather Permitting. That time during which weather prevents working shall not count as laytime WPD Weather Permitting Day WWD Weather Working Day WRIC Wire Rods In Collis WWR When, Where Ready WWWW Wibon, Wccon, Wifpon, Wipon YAR York Antwerp Rules YAW To swing or steer off course, as when running with a quartering sea Z UTC = GMT'},
  {t:'Chartering operations technique — International Logistics', u:'chartering-technique.html', text:'Home → Chartering operations technique Chartering operations technique Stages of a chartering deal The chartering process passes through seven consecutive stages — from the offer to the final settlement. Offer — the shipowner or broker offers a vessel: its characteristics (type, tonnage, speed, deadweight), the route (ports of loading/discharge) and the freight rate. Negotiations — the charterer clarifies the terms: price, laytime, demurrage, despatch; rates may be adjusted and special conditions discussed. Recap / Confirmation — written confirmation of the main terms, exchange of recaps between brokers, agreement of the details and the final consent of the parties. Charter Party Execution — signing of the contract, use of standard terms (GENCON, NYPE, etc.), calculation of freight and advances, determination of the date the vessel is ready. Mobilization — the vessel proceeds to the port of loading, cargo is prepared, documents are completed, port services are notified. Operations — loading and discharging of the cargo, laytime control, issuance of bills of lading. Settlement — invoicing, assessment of demurrage/despatch, final freight calculation, payments by all parties. Typical stages of chartering correspondence Stage 1: Offer FROM: [Shipowner / Broker] TO: [Charterer / Charterer\'s Broker] OFFERING FOR PROMPT FIXTURE: Vessel Name: MV EXAMPLE Type: General Cargo / Container / Tanker Class: [Classification Society] DWT: [Deadweight Tonnage] Bale Capacity: [cubic metres] Speed: [knots] Built: [year of build] Flag: [flag] TRADING: [trading area] LOAD PORT(S): [ports of loading with dates] DISCHARGE PORT(S): [ports of discharge] FREIGHT: $[amount] per metric ton / per container / Lumpsum PAYABLE: [method of payment] LAYTIME: [days allowed for operations] DEMURRAGE: $[amount] per day per port DISPATCH: [% of freight for saving laytime] CAN LOAD: [date the vessel is ready] EARLIEST: [date of readiness] GENERAL CONDITIONS: [GENCON / BALTIME / NYPE / etc.] Validity: [period of validity of the offer, usually 24–48 hours] Stage 2: Recap RECAP — CONFIRMATION OF FIXTURE FROM: [Charterer\'s Broker] TO: [Shipowner\'s Broker] REF: [offer number] DATE: [recap date] FIXTURE DETAILS: VESSEL: MV EXAMPLE (as above) CARGO: [description of cargo] Quantity: [quantity in metric tonnes] Package: [packaging] LOAD PORT(S): [ports and dates] DISCHARGE: [ports] FREIGHT: $[amount] all-in [method of calculation] PAYMENT TERMS: Bill of Lading terms / Prepaid / 1/2 on B/L, 1/2 on delivery LAYTIME PROVISIONS: - Laytime: [number of days] - Time begins: [from what moment] - Time stops: [when it stops] - Demurrage: $[amount] per day per port or pro-rata - Dispatch money: [% for saving] GENERAL CONDITIONS: [standard conditions] SPECIAL CLAUSES: - [any additional conditions] BOOKING REFERENCE: [booking reference] OWNER\'S CONFIRMATION: [date of the shipowner\'s confirmation] CHARTERER\'S CONFIRMATION: [date of the charterer\'s confirmation] BROKERS: [brokers of the parties] Stage 3: Recap Correction RECAP CORRECTION / RELETTERING TO AMEND RECAP DTD [date of the original recap] PLEASE NOTE THE FOLLOWING AMENDMENTS: ITEM 1: [what it was] SHOULD READ [how it should be] ITEM 2: [what it was] SHOULD READ [how it should be] ALL OTHER TERMS AS IN RECAP DATED [date] PLEASE CONFIRM YOUR ACCEPTANCE OF ABOVE AMENDED TERMS Key parameters of a chartering deal Freight Rate Type of rate Description Example Ad valorem % of the value of the cargo 5% of the cargo value Per metric ton For each tonne of cargo $50/MT Per ton Per tonne of the vessel\'s capacity $100/ton of the vessel Lumpsum A fixed sum per voyage regardless of the cargo $500,000 per voyage Per TEU/FEU Per container unit $2,000 per TEU Sliding scale The more cargo, the lower the rate Volume discount Per diem Hire rate per day (time charter) $50,000 per day Laytime Kind of days Definition Running days Calendar days (consecutive). Working days Working days, excluding weekends. Weather-working days Excluding days of bad weather. Excepted days Excluded days: holidays, strikes. Examples of formulations: "10 running days" — 10 calendar days. "7 working days, Sundays and holidays excepted" — working days only. "Weather working days" — days of bad weather are excluded. "Laytime shall commence when vessel is ready, berth or no berth" — the days begin from the readiness of the vessel, even if there is no berth. "Laytime shall commence upon receipt of notice of readiness (NOR)" — the days begin from the moment the notice of readiness is received. Demurrage and Despatch Laytime is included in the cost of freight. Exceeding the limit entails demurrage, and early completion entails despatch (a bonus for saving time, usually 50% of the demurrage rate). FORMULAS: DEMURRAGE = Number of excess days × Daily demurrage rate DISPATCH = Number of days saved × 50% × Demurrage rate EXAMPLE (overrun): - Laytime: 10 days - Actually worked: 12 days - Demurrage rate: $5,000/day - DEMURRAGE = 2 days × $5,000 = $10,000 EXAMPLE (saving): - Laytime: 10 days - Actually worked: 8 days - DISPATCH = 2 days × 50% × $5,000 = $5,000 (saving for the shipowner) Typical charter-party terms (GENCON 94) Parties — the shipowner (Owner) and the charterer (Charterer). Vessel — name, type, class, deadweight (DWT), permitted ports. Cargo — description and quantity, packaging and marking, prohibited cargoes. Freight — amount and unit of calculation, method of payment (Bill of Lading terms, Prepaid), currency of payment. Laytime — number of days, kinds of days (running, working, weather-working), start of the count (ready, NOR), excluded days. Demurrage & Despatch — the rate for overrun, the percentage of freight for early completion. Port charges — who pays (Owner, Charterer, split), the amounts of the charges. Insurance — who insures the cargo, the amount of cover (usually 110% of the value). General Average — joint participation in the compensation of losses. Liability — exclusions from liability, limits of liability. Force Majeure — kinds of circumstances and their consequences. Dispute and arbitration — choice of law, arbitration (London, New York, etc.). Examples of recaps with explanations Example 1. Voyage charter for dry cargo RECAP CONFIRMATION DATE: 30 July 2026 REFERENCE: 2026/DC/001 VESSEL: MV NORTHERN STAR TYPE: General Cargo Ship DWT: 35,000 MT FLAG: Panama CLASS: Lloyd\'s Register + DNV FROM: SHIPPER\'S BROKER (Exporter) TO: OWNER\'S BROKER (Shipowner) CARGO DESCRIPTION: - Steel coils - Quantity: 32,000 metric tons - In 40 x 40 ft containers (800 containers total) - Shipper: ABC Steel Mills, South Korea LOAD PORT: Busan, South Korea ETA: 10–15 August 2026 DISCHARGE PORT: Rotterdam, The Netherlands ETA: 15–20 September 2026 (via Suez Canal) FREIGHT RATE: Base Freight: $18.50 per metric ton Total Freight: 32,000 × $18.50 = $592,000 Currency: USD PAYMENT TERMS: - 50% on B/L signing: $296,000 - 50% on arrival: $296,000 - Payment method: Bank transfer LAYTIME PROVISIONS: - Loading laytime: 5 running days - Discharging laytime: 5 running days - Time commences: Upon notice of readiness (NOR) - Time ceases: Upon completion of all cargo work DEMURRAGE: - $3,500 per day per port - Pro-rata for part of day DISPATCH MONEY: - 50% of demurrage rate for each day saved - Applicable if operations completed before laytime expires - Example: 1 day saved = $1,750 dispatch GENERAL CONDITIONS: - GENCON 94 - Hague-Visby Rules apply - All bills of lading to be clean SPECIAL CLAUSES: 1. Both ports will revert at Owner\'s/Charterer\'s option 2. Cargo to be worked 24 hours per day if both parties agree 3. Gears to be used if available at no extra cost to Charterer 4. Owner\'s agent at load port to be nominated by shippers 5. All stevedoring to be in accordance with local regulations INSURANCE: - Charterer to arrange insurance at 110% of cargo value - B/L to be marked "Freight prepaid" VALIDITY OF OFFER: 48 hours from this date OWNER\'S BROKER: Nordic Shipping AS Tel: +47 2223 4444, Email: broker@nordicship.no CHARTERER\'S BROKER: Global Trade Partners Tel: +82 2 1234 5678, Email: bp@globalpartners.kr Item What it means Why it matters ETA Busan The vessel will be ready to load on 10–15 August The charterer must have the cargo ready by that time Freight $18.50/MT The standard rate for Korean steel exports If the cargo does not fit the tonnage, the freight may change 50/50 payment Half on signing, half on arrival The shipowner is guaranteed at least half before delivery 5 days laytime 5 days each for loading and discharge Demurrage is paid from the 6th day $3,500/day demurrage $3,500 is paid for each day of overrun Expensive — the charterer strives to finish on time GENCON 94 The standard terms of the international association The parties know their obligations without a detailed description Gears to be used The ship\'s cranes are used free of charge A saving for the charterer (usually $200–300/day) Example 2. Tanker time charter RECAP — TIME CHARTER DATE: 5 August 2026 REFERENCE: 2026/TC/TANK/042 VESSEL: MT OCEAN PIONEER Type: Crude Oil Tanker Capacity: 130,000 metric tons (DWT) Age: 5 years, Class: ABS + Nippon Kaiji Kyokai Flag: Liberia Speed: 14.5 knots loaded, 15 knots ballast PERIOD: 36 months EARLIEST DELIVERY: 15 September 2026 PLACE OF DELIVERY: Singapore (Seller\'s option between Singapore/Rotterdam) PLACE OF REDELIVERY: Worldwide, normal trading limits HIRE (DAILY RATE): Base Rate: $48,000 USD per calendar day Delivery to 25,000 bbls basis Suez passing = $48,000 - Full period, all risks for Charterer PAYMENT: - Monthly in advance on a fixed date - Account payable: [Bank details] - Currency: USD CREW COSTS: - Owner pays all crew costs - Charterer reimburses Owner monthly for crew changes - Estimated: $800/month per crew member FUEL/BUNKERS: - Charterer to pay all fuel and bunker costs - Owner to provide clean ship; Charterer to settle all bunkering invoices - Bunker sampling at load port and discharge port MAINTENANCE: - Owner responsible for hull and machinery maintenance - Charterer responsible for cargo-related maintenance - Vessel in "good and efficient" condition INSURANCE: - Owner to maintain P&I insurance (Protection & Indemnity) - Charterer to arrange cargo insurance - Owner\'s All Risks coverage at Charterer\'s expense REDELIVERY: - Vessel to be redelivered "in same good condition as delivered" - With fuel (if time permits, seller to top off bunkers) - Normal wear and tear excepted OFF-HIRE CLAUSE: - $900/day deduction from hire for each day of breakdown exceeding 24 hours - Examples of off-hire events: * Machinery breakdown * Hull repairs * Defects affecting cargo operations GENERAL CONDITIONS: - SHELLTIME 4 (Tanker Time Charter Uniform Conditions) - BIMCO Standard Form - Hague-Visby Rules SPECIAL PROVISIONS: 1. Charterer has options to extend for additional 12, 24, or 36 months 2. Early redelivery possible with 90 days\' notice 3. Vessel capable of all ports worldwide except those restricted by draft 4. Charterer has right to substitute similar vessel with Owner\'s approval 5. War risk insurance to be arranged at Charterer\'s cost if required PERFORMANCE BONDS: - Charterer to provide performance bond = 3 months hire - Amount: $144,000 (3 × $48,000) DISPUTES: - London Arbitration - Two arbitrators + umpire - LCIA Rules apply VALIDITY: 48 hours from this date Owner\'s Broker: Tanker Solutions Inc. Tel: +1 212 555 0123 Charterer\'s Broker: Global Energy Logistics Tel: +44 20 7123 4567 Item What it means Why it matters 36 months The vessel is chartered for 3 years A long-term commitment, stable income for the shipowner $48,000/day Payment of $48,000 every day for 3 years Total: 48,000 × 365 × 3 ≈ $52.6m for the period Delivery Singapore The vessel is delivered in Singapore (or Rotterdam) The charterer starts paying from that moment Owner pays crew The shipowner pays the crew\'s wages The charterer reimburses the costs on crew changes Charterer pays fuel The charterer pays for fuel The shipowner saves on consumables Off-hire clause $900 is deducted for each day of breakdown over 24 hours Motivates keeping the vessel in good condition Performance bond The charterer deposits a $144,000 guarantee Insurance for the shipowner against non-payment SHELLTIME 4 The standard terms of Shell tanker charters The parties know their rights and obligations precisely Example 3. Container voyage charter RECAP — CONTAINER VOYAGE CHARTER DATE: 12 August 2026 REFERENCE: 2026/CONT/SH/089 VESSEL: CONTAINER STAR EXPRESS Type: Container Ship (Cellular) Capacity: 5,400 TEU (20-foot equivalent units) Speed: 23 knots Gear: Gearless vessel Flag: Hong Kong TRADING: Route: Asia — Europe Load Port: Shanghai, China Discharge Port: Rotterdam, The Netherlands LOADING: CW 36-37, 2026 (Week 36-37 of 2026) ETA Load: 2–8 September 2026 ETA Discharge: 2–8 October 2026 (via Suez) FREIGHT RATE: Base: $1,200 per TEU for full containers Empty Container Cost: $200 per TEU (if repositioning required) Reefer Surcharge: +15% (for refrigerated containers) Total Estimated Freight: - 4,000 Loaded TEU × $1,200 = $4,800,000 - 400 Empty TEU × $200 = $80,000 - 200 Reefer TEU (within the loaded) × 15% × $1,200 = $36,000 TOTAL: ~$4,916,000 PAYMENT TERMS: - Payment due within 10 days of B/L date - Advance: 25% before loading starts = $1,229,000 - Balance: 75% within 10 days of B/L = $3,687,000 LAYTIME: - Loading: 5 days (included in freight) - Discharging: 5 days (included in freight) - Free time includes ship-to-shore (STS) cranes - Additional days: $8,500 per day per port DEMURRAGE: - Port demurrage: $8,500 per day - Container demurrage (after discharge): * 20-ft: $5 per day * 40-ft: $8 per day - Free time: 7 days after discharge DISPATCH BONUS: - 50% of daily detention rate if loading/discharging completed early GENERAL CONDITIONS: - CONTINOLOAD terms (Container Voyage Charter Standard) - Hague-Visby Rules - Bills of Lading to be clean WEIGHT LIMITATIONS: - Maximum container weight: 30 MT per container - Overweight surcharge: $200 per MT in excess of 20 MT - Dangerous goods: IMDG Code compliance mandatory SPECIAL CLAUSES: 1. Containers must be inspected and weighed at load port 2. Shipper responsible for accurate weight declaration 3. Heavy containers to be declared 14 days before loading 4. Reefer containers: Owner to provide power connection and monitoring 5. SOC (Shipper Owned Container) allowed: $75 per TEU additional fee 6. Vessel to call at Suez Canal pilots\' station (on-hire) CARGO INSURANCE: - Shipper to arrange insurance at 110% CIF value - B/L must state "Freight prepaid" or "Freight to pay" as per agreement DOCUMENTATION: - Shipper to provide shipping instructions 7 days before loading - Container number, seal numbers, weights must be accurate - Shipper liable for errors in documentation STRIKES/FORCE MAJEURE: - Strikes and lockouts: time on demurrage + hire continues - War: time suspended, no demurrage/hire accrual - Deviation: not payable unless forced by circumstances REDELIVERY/RETURN: - Empty containers must be returned within free time (7 days) - Storage charge: $10 per container per day after free time - Damaged containers: repaired at Owner\'s expense or charged to charterer COMPLAINTS: - Load port: must be filed within 72 hours of operations - Discharge port: must be filed within 48 hours of discharge - Later claims not accepted VALIDITY: 36 hours from this date, then null and void unless extended Owner\'s Broker: Coastal Shipping & Logistics Tel: +852 2299 1234 Charterer\'s Broker: Sunrise Forwarding Ltd. Tel: +8610 5555 6666 Item What it means Why it matters 5,400 TEU The vessel carries 5,400 twenty-foot containers Maximum capacity with 20-foot containers $1,200/TEU The standard Shanghai–Rotterdam rate Depends on demand, season and capacity 5 days laytime 5 days each for loading and discharge Container operations are usually faster — 2–3 days Empty container $200 Payment for the carriage of empty containers Typical for routes with a cargo flow imbalance Reefer surcharge +15% Refrigerated containers are more expensive Payment for higher energy consumption $8,500/day additional For additional days beyond 5 days laytime Motivates fast port work Weight limitations 30 MT Maximum 30 tonnes per container Protection against overloading, stability of the vessel SOC containers $75 The shipper\'s container instead of the vessel\'s Permitted for an additional fee Complaints 72/48 hours Claims are filed within tight time limits Otherwise the cargo is considered delivered in proper condition Validity 36 hours The offer is valid for 36 hours A fast-moving market requires promptness Real example: a Contract of Affreightment (Gencon 94) Below is a real example of a voyage charter party under the GENCON 94 form for the carriage of a consignment of steel by several bulk carriers. This is a classic Contract of Affreightment (COA) — a contract for a series of scheduled shipments, where several vessels and several lots of cargo fall under a single charter. Part I — the completed "header" of the deal RECOMMENDED UNIVERSAL CHARTER PARTY OF THE BALTIC AND INTERNATIONAL MARITIME COUNCIL (REVISED IN 1922, 1976 AND 1994) CODE NAME: "GENCON" Place and date: Moscow, 24.05.2026 Owners: Shipping Ltd., Edinburgh, United Kingdom Charterers: First class Name of vessel / Gross-Net tonnage: m/v "BALTIC SKY" or substitute — 2061/1178 m/v "SONATA" or substitute — 5184/3046 Total deadweight on summer load line (about): 3,085 t — m/v "BALTIC SKY" 7,909 t — m/v "SONATA" Present position: in the course of a voyage Expected readiness to load: see boxes 21 and 22 Port or place of loading / discharge: 1 SP Rotterdam or Dunkirk → 1 SP Vyborg or St. Petersburg 1 SP Vyborg or St. Petersburg or Ust-Luga → 1 SP Dordrecht 1 SP Vyborg or St. Petersburg or Ust-Luga → 1 SP Rotterdam Cargo: about 150,000 t of steel in total, stowage by weight, in lots of 2,800 – 6,000 t each, jointly agreed Freight rate (FIOS L/S/D terms): Euro 12/t — Rotterdam Euro 13/t — Dunkirk Euro 36/t — Rotterdam Euro 25/t — Rotterdam Payment of freight: 100% within 10 banking days of the date of signing and release of the bill of lading, to the owners\' account (see box 23) Loading/discharging gear: see box 24 Laytime for loading: 1,500 t WWD SHINC Laytime for discharging: 1,500 t WWD SSHEX UU Shippers: to be nominated on the charterers\' instruction Agents (loading/discharge): see box 34 Demurrage rate (per day, pro rata): Euro 3,000 — m/v "Baltic Sky" Euro 6,000 — m/v "Sonata" (see also box 26) Cancelling date: see boxes 21 and 22 General average to be adjusted in: London Tax on freight: for the charterers\' account Brokerage commission: no commission Law and arbitration: London (see clause 19) It is mutually agreed that this contract shall be performed subject to the conditions of the charter party, which includes both Part I and Part II. In the event of a conflict, the provisions of Part I prevail over those of Part II. Part II — key terms of GENCON 94 The full standard terms of "Gencon 94" (Part II) include 19 clauses. The key ones are: Clause Essence 1. Presentation of the vessel The vessel shall proceed to the port of loading after completing its previous obligations, load and deliver the cargo to the port of discharge. 2. Owners\' liability Liability only in the absence of due diligence in making the vessel seaworthy; a list of exceptions (nautical fault, etc.). 3. Deviation The vessel may call at any ports, tow, and deviate to save life and property. 4. Payment of freight Freight is paid on the loaded quantity; prepaid freight is deemed earned and non-returnable, even if the vessel/cargo is lost. 5. Loading/discharging The cargo is worked by the charterers free of the risk and expense of the owners (corresponds to FIOS); the provision of the vessel\'s cargo gear. 6. Laytime Laytime runs from 13:00/14:00 if the notice is given before 12:00, otherwise from 06:00/08:00 the following working day; shifting to the berth and customs formalities are not counted as laytime. 7. Demurrage Paid per day or pro rata; on non-payment — a 96-hour notice, then the right to cancel the charter. 8. Lien The owners have a lien on the cargo and sub-freight as security for freight, dead freight, demurrage and losses. 9. Cancelling If the vessel is not ready by the cancelling date, the charterer may cancel the charter; the owners must give notice of the delay and offer a new date. 10. Bills of lading Signed in the "Congenbill 1994" form with the note "Without prejudice to this charter party". 12. General average Adjusted in London under the York-Antwerp Rules 1994. 15. Brokerage commission Charged on earned freight, dead freight and demurrage; on non-performance — 1/3 of the commission in favour of the brokers. 16. General strike clause The rights of the parties in strikes/lockouts affecting loading or discharge. 17. War risks ("Voywar 1993") The owners\' right to cancel the contract or deviate from the route in the event of war risks (war, mines, piracy, terrorism, blockade). 18. General ice clause The master\'s right to leave the port in case of ice danger; the charterer\'s rights when the port of discharge is inaccessible. 19. Law and arbitration Subject to English law, arbitration in London (or another option — New York / an agreed place). What is important in this example: this is a COA (Contract of Affreightment) — a contract for a series of shipments of steel (~150,000 t) by several substitute vessels ("Baltic Sky", "Sonata") with flexible ports of loading/discharge and separate freight rates by direction. The FIOS L/S/D terms mean that loading/discharging and stowage are performed at the charterer\'s expense and risk — typical for bulk cargo. Laytime is set at a rate of 1,500 t/day with different counting regimes: for loading — WWD SHINC, for discharge — WWD SSHEX UU. Peculiarities of correspondence and standard expressions Phrases in chartering correspondence Situation English expression Translation On offer We are offering… We are offering… For your prompt fixture… For your prompt fixture… Subject to owner\'s approval… Subject to owner\'s approval… Validity of 48 hours… Validity of 48 hours… In negotiations We are a bit high at this level… This is a bit expensive… Can you reduce the rate? Can you reduce the rate? We are more at… We are closer to [an amount]… This is our rock bottom… This is our rock bottom… Let us come back to you… We will get back to you… On recap As per recap dated… As per recap dated… Please confirm… Please confirm… We hereby confirm… We hereby confirm… Subject to Subject to… All other terms as previously agreed All other terms as previously agreed On reference to conditions Under the General Conditions… Under the General Conditions… According to the Charter Party… According to the Charter Party… As per Clause… As per Clause… Typical sequence of correspondence DAY 1, 09:00 — OFFER from the shipowner From: Owner\'s Broker To: Charterer\'s Broker Subject: OFFERING MV EXAMPLE Dear Sirs, We are offering for your prompt fixture: VESSEL: MV EXAMPLE DWT: 35,000 MT CAN LOAD: 15–20 August LOAD: Shanghai DISCHARGE: Rotterdam FREIGHT: $18.50/MT LAYTIME: 5 running days each port DEMURRAGE: $3,500 per day DISPATCH: 50% GENERAL CONDITIONS: GENCON 94 Please advise your interest. Best regards, Owner\'s Broker ──────────────────────────────── DAY 1, 10:30 — CLARIFYING QUESTION From: Charterer\'s Broker To: Owner\'s Broker Subject: RE: OFFERING MV EXAMPLE Thanks for the offer. We are interested but have some questions: 1. What\'s the earliest you can adjust your freight level? 2. Can you accept 7 days laying days? 3. Who pays stevedoring? Please advise. ──────────────────────────────── DAY 1, 11:45 — REPLY TO QUESTIONS From: Owner\'s Broker To: Charterer\'s Broker Subject: RE: RE: OFFERING MV EXAMPLE Regarding your questions: 1. Freight: We can consider $18.00/MT but no further reduction. 2. Laytime: 5 days is our limit as per other commitments. 3. Stevedoring: All in cost, included in freight. Please confirm if acceptable. ──────────────────────────────── DAY 1, 14:00 — CONSENT From: Charterer\'s Broker To: Owner\'s Broker Subject: RECAP — MV EXAMPLE FIXED We confirm fixture on the following terms: VESSEL: MV EXAMPLE LOAD: Shanghai, CW 33-34, 2026 DISCHARGE: Rotterdam CARGO: 32,000 MT steel coils FREIGHT: $18.00/MT = $576,000 PAYMENT: 50% on B/L, 50% on arrival LAYTIME: 5 running days loading, 5 running days discharging DEMURRAGE: $3,500 per day DISPATCH: 50% GENERAL CONDITIONS: GENCON 94 Please confirm your acceptance. ──────────────────────────────── DAY 1, 15:30 — CONFIRMATION OF THE RECAP From: Owner\'s Broker To: Charterer\'s Broker Subject: RECAP CONFIRMATION We confirm your recap dated 01 August 2026 with reference MV EXAMPLE. All terms as per your recap accepted. Fixture confirmed. ──────────────────────────────── DAY 2, 09:00 — SIGNING OF THE CHARTER PARTY [Both parties receive the signed document] Practical advice for a chartering specialist What to do Be prompt — the freight market moves fast. State the terms clearly — avoid ambiguity. Check the documents — bills of lading, invoices, recaps. Know the market — follow quotations, rates and demand. Communicate through brokers — they are professionals in negotiation. Document everything — confirm the terms in writing. Meet the deadlines — especially laytime and payments. Check the deadweight — do not exceed the vessel\'s DWT. What not to do Do not make promises without approval — first agree with management. Do not ignore special conditions — they are important. Do not be late with payments — it damages your reputation. Do not sign a charter party without reading it carefully. Do not neglect insurance — it costs dear. Do not forget about laytime — it is money. Do not economise on documentation — you will save on problems. Freight calculation: examples Example 1. Voyage charter GIVEN: - Vessel: General Cargo, 35,000 MT DWT - Freight rate: $50/MT - Cargo: Steel products, 28,000 MT - Laytime: 5 days at 1,000 MT/day = 28 days - Actual work: 35 days - Demurrage: $3,500/day CALCULATION: 1. Base freight = 28,000 MT × $50 = $1,400,000 2. Demurrage: - Excess days: 35 − 28 = 7 days - Demurrage = 7 × $3,500 = $24,500 3. Final freight: Total = $1,400,000 + $24,500 = $1,424,500 PAYMENT TERMS: - 50% = $712,250 (on signing B/L) - 50% = $712,250 (on arrival at the port) Example 2. Time charter GIVEN: - Vessel: Tanker, 130,000 MT - Daily rate: $48,000/day - Period: 6 months ≈ 180 days - Delivery: 1 September 2026 - Redelivery: 28 February 2027 - Off-hire events: 8 days of breakdown CALCULATION: 1. Base hire: - Number of days: 1 September — 28 February = 180 days - Daily rate: $48,000 - Base hire = 180 × $48,000 = $8,640,000 2. Off-hire (deductible): - Days without payment: 8 days × $48,000 = $384,000 3. Final hire: Total = $8,640,000 − $384,000 = $8,256,000 PAYMENT TERMS: - Monthly in advance: 30 × $48,000 = $1,440,000/month - For 6 months: 6 × $1,440,000 = $8,640,000 - After deducting off-hire: $8,256,000 Example 3. Container charter GIVEN: - Vessel: 5,400 TEU - Rate: $1,200/TEU - Cargo: 4,000 full containers (20-ft) + 200 reefer containers (within the 4,000) + 400 empty containers for repositioning - Reefer surcharge: +15% CALCULATION: 1. Standard containers: - (4,000 − 200 reefer) × $1,200 = 3,800 × $1,200 = $4,560,000 2. Reefer containers: - 200 × $1,200 × 1.15 = 200 × $1,380 = $276,000 3. Empty containers: - 400 × $200 = $80,000 4. Final freight: Total = $4,560,000 + $276,000 + $80,000 = $4,916,000 PAYMENT TERMS: - Advance payment (25%): $1,229,000 - Balance (75%): $3,687,000 - Payment within 10 days of B/L'},
  {t:'Ship chartering — International Logistics', u:'chartering.html', text:'Home → Ship chartering Ship chartering Types of contract of carriage by sea Under a contract of carriage of goods, the carrier undertakes to deliver the goods entrusted to it by the shipper to the port of destination and to hand them over to the person entitled to receive them (the consignee), and the shipper undertakes to pay the agreed freight for the carriage of the goods. Under a contract of affreightment (charter party), one party (the carrier, the shipowner) undertakes, for payment, to place at the disposal of the other party (the charterer) all or part of the cargo capacity of one or more vessels for one or more voyages for the carriage of goods or baggage. Either the shipowner or another legal entity having the right to manage the operation of the vessel for the whole term of the contract of carriage may act as the sea carrier. In the standard forms of voyage charter parties, the term "shipowner" (or "owner") is used in all such cases. The charterer may be the seller or buyer of the goods, a multimodal transport operator, a broker or a freight forwarder. The conclusion of a contract of carriage by sea is evidenced by the issue to the shipper of a bill of lading, while a contract of affreightment is documented by a voyage charter party or a fixture recap with reference to a standard charter-party form. Bill of Lading (B/L) The bill of lading is used as a contract of carriage by sea, usually in liner shipping. The shipowner acts as a common carrier. It independently determines the composition of the fleet on the line, the ports of call, the frequency of voyages, the schedule, develops the commercial terms of the B/L and the tariffs, and offers the transport service system it has organised to all shippers. Individual consignments of cargo are stowed on board by the carrier (master) at its discretion, in the general interest of the voyage. Only the carriage of cargo on deck must be agreed with the cargo owner, and a corresponding note ("on deck") is made on the face of the bill of lading. After accepting a given consignment, the carrier is entitled to load or discharge any other cargo in this or another port of the line, to perform various auxiliary operations and repairs, and to deliver the cargo with transshipment at an intermediate port onto another vessel or another mode of transport. The terms of carriage and the tariffs apply to all shippers for a long period — until changed. In certain cases (a regular shipper, a large consignment, under-loading of the vessel, or the presence of empty containers in port) the line may agree significant discounts on the published tariff rates with the customer. Voyage Charter Party (C/P) The voyage charter party is used in liner and tramp shipping for the carriage of bulk cargo. The contract is concluded on the basis of the charterer\'s offer, which defines all the main conditions of the forthcoming voyage: the kind and quantity of cargo, the ports of loading and discharge, the date the cargo will be ready for carriage, the rates of cargo work, and the requirements for the vessel. In general, under a C/P the shipowner acts as a contract carrier. The charterer is provided with the whole vessel, or specific cargo spaces, or a specific part of the vessel\'s capacity for the stowage of cargo. The carrier has no right to use the chartered vessel (or spaces) for other, even incidental, carriage, unless this is agreed with the charterer. If such cargo has been loaded on board, the charterer may demand its discharge, and if the carriage has already taken place — compensation for its proven losses (due to delay in the delivery of its cargo, damage to the cargo, mixing of cargoes). The carrier must provide, by the agreed date, a vessel fully prepared for the forthcoming voyage and, after completion of loading, immediately send it to the port of discharge by the usual route and at the usual speed. Unless the contract provides for liner terms of vessel handling, the charterer is responsible for providing a berth at the ports of loading and/or discharge and for performing cargo work within the time (or at the rates) provided for in the charter, and also pays the cost of the cargo work. The charter sets out in detail the rights and obligations of the parties in the event of any possible deviations of the actual conditions of the voyage from those agreed in the charter, such as: the absence of the required quantity of cargo, the unreadiness of the vessel or its arrival at the port of loading earlier than the agreed date, demurrage of the vessel awaiting a berth, delay of the vessel or early completion of cargo work, and various force-majeure circumstances. All the terms of the contract, as well as the freight rate, are agreed in detail by the carrier and the charterer and are valid only for the contracting parties and only for the period of the given transaction. Three types of ship chartering under a voyage charter party Chartering for a single voyage (one voyage C/P). After the cargo is delivered to the consignee and all settlements for the voyage are completed, both parties are released from mutual obligations. Chartering a vessel for several consecutive voyages (consecutive voyage C/P). Under this contract the operator must, after completion of the first voyage, immediately send the vessel on the second, then the third, and so on until the whole agreed number of voyages is performed. The freight is paid separately for each voyage at pre-agreed rates. Throughout the term of the contract, cargo of other owners may not be carried on the vessel without the charterer\'s agreement, even in the same or the reverse ballast direction. The substitution of the vessel stipulated in the contract by a substitute in the first or any subsequent voyage must also be agreed with the charterer. Long-term or general freight contract (contract of affreightment) — see a real COA example in the section "Chartering operations technique" . It is an agreement on the carriage, by regular voyages, of a specified quantity of a given charterer\'s cargo during an agreed calendar period; to perform its obligations the carrier is entitled to use not only its own but also chartered tonnage and to substitute vessels during the term of the contract. The main terms of a freight contract: the term of the contract with the specific dates of its beginning and end; the ports (ranges) of loading and discharge; the kind and total quantity of cargo and the distribution of carriage by month (in tonnes or by number of voyages); the type or deadweight of the vessels the carrier may use to perform the contract; and the minimum interval for the presentation of vessels for loading. The contract may set restrictions on the age and dimensions of the vessels (length, draught when fully laden), requirements for suitability for cargo operations, for the carriage of palletised cargo, for an ice class, etc. The charterer enters these conditions into the contract: they guarantee that the shipowner will not present two or more vessels for loading at the same time, that their deadweight will correspond to the sizes of the cargo consignments, and that any vessel presented to it will meet the characteristics of the port, the sailing conditions and the cargo transshipment technology. In turn, the shipowner stipulates the maximum number of ports of call per voyage, the minimum estimated loading of the vessel for the carriage of the last consignment of cargo, and other conditions. The contract is drawn up on the basis of one of the standard charter-party forms and defines uniform conditions of carriage for the whole term of the contract. Short forms of contract of carriage by sea The liner bill of lading and the voyage charter party are the full form of a contract of carriage by sea, since they contain all the necessary commercial and legal clauses defining the relationship of the parties to the given transaction. In addition, there are short forms of contract. In them only the main characteristics of the transaction are agreed, while the remaining conditions are governed by reference to a charter party or bill of lading. Such forms include: Booking note — a preliminary application by the cargo owner to reserve space on a vessel for a specific consignment of cargo. It is used usually in liner shipping. After it is signed by the carrier or its agent, the booking note acquires the character of a contract of carriage by sea. It sets out the specific characteristics of the transaction: the quantity and kind of cargo, the ports of loading and discharge, the date the cargo is ready, the name of the vessel, and the freight rate. As to the remaining conditions of carriage, reference is made to the bill of lading of the line. Berth note — a contract for the carriage of incidental bulk cargo. It is used in both liner and tramp shipping, usually for loading at the same port (at the same berth) where the main cargo is accepted. Unlike the booking note, it contains a number of clauses typical of charter carriage: the procedure for calculating laytime, the allocation of cargo-work costs, and the freight rate. As to the remaining commercial and legal conditions, the berth note refers to one of the standard charter-party forms. All types of contract of carriage by sea define the rights, liability and risks of the shipowner (carrier). Standard voyage charter-party forms When concluding a voyage charter party, the shipowner and the charterer must agree in detail their mutual obligations as to the vessel, the cargo, the ports of loading and discharge, the procedure for calculating laytime and paying freight, the allocation of stevedoring costs, etc. For each of these items it is necessary to provide for the various situations that may arise in the forthcoming voyage and the liability of the parties for breach of obligations. The wording of the contract must guarantee its unambiguous interpretation and take into account the practice of arbitration and judicial bodies. The limited time available to the parties for negotiations makes it practically impossible to formulate afresh all the conditions of the contract on concluding each specific transaction. Therefore the use of standard charter-party forms is generally accepted in modern merchant shipping. These are standard charter conditions developed on the basis of long international experience, which the partners may use as a starting basis for negotiations when concluding a transaction. The standard forms are developed by international organisations of shipowners and brokers or by large charterers with the involvement of specialists in maritime law, which ensures a high professional level of these documents. Their use does not deprive the parties of the possibility of making individual amendments to the charter, entered in the text or included as special additions. The introduction of amendments or additions to the charter automatically nullifies all conditions contradicting them that are contained in the printed text of the charter, even if this was not specifically stipulated. As a rule, the clauses of the charter and the lines of the printed text are numbered, which simplifies the agreement of the necessary changes to the printed text ( see a real COA example in the section "Chartering operations technique" ), especially when negotiations are conducted by means of communication (e-mail, telephone). The main task of the charterer and the shipowner in this case is to resolve the questions determining the specific content of the contract: the vessel, the time of its presentation for loading, the rates of cargo work, the freight rate and the demurrage rate. As to the commercial and legal conditions of carriage, it is sufficient for the parties to choose a standard charter-party form and agree the changes and additions to be made to its printed text. Structure of a charter party Modern charter parties consist of three sections: the preamble, which states the parties and the place and date of conclusion of the contract; part one, which contains all the variable conditions characterising the content of the given specific transaction (the vessel, the cargo, the date the vessel is ready, the calculation of laytime, the payment of freight); and part two, which contains the procedure for applying the commercial conditions of the transaction and the standard clauses of a legal nature: on general average, on mutual liability in the event of collision of vessels, etc. Usually, on concluding a charter party, the legal clauses are accepted by the partners in full according to the printed text of the given charter-party form, or with minimal changes. The modern form of a charter party facilitates its use in operational conditions, since all information on the content of the transaction is brought together on a single first page. In addition, the preparation of copies of the charter is facilitated, since the whole constant part may be reproduced in advance by printing, while on concluding each specific contract it is sufficient to complete only the single first page, in which the variable conditions are set out. In recent years a special form of charter party has been used, where special boxes (the so-called box form) are provided on the first page for recording each kind of variable information. This kind of layout is the most convenient from the point of view of the clarity of the material, and also for completing and processing charter parties. Addendum and rider When agreeing the commercial conditions of a charter party, especially if the negotiations are conducted on the basis of an outdated form, it becomes necessary to change a large number of standard formulations. Making numerous additions and corrections to the printed text complicates the completion and study of the charter. Therefore the accepted practice is that the relevant conditions of the standard form are simply struck out and new ones are formulated instead. All newly formulated or additional clauses of the contract are combined into a single supplement to the charter party (an addendum or rider). The clauses included in the addendum are numbered consecutively, continuing the numbering of the clauses of the standard form. On the last page of the standard form (in the box form — in a special box on the first page of the standard charter) the numbers of the clauses of the addendum are given and it is stated that they are an integral part of the charter party. Most often the addendum formulates the following conditions of the contract: a full description of the vessel; the terms of payment of freight; the allocation of cargo-work costs; the calculation of laytime; the inspection of the readiness of the holds by the charterer\'s surveyor; the allocation of the costs of dues and charges on the cargo, freight and vessel; the payment of extra insurance; the relationship of the parties in the event of damage to the vessel by the charterer\'s stevedores, etc. Riders are usually used to formalise private additions to the charter party for a given specific voyage, as a rule confidential, for example the terms of payment of freight. Commercial conditions of charter parties 1. Seaworthiness and characteristics of vessels The carrier must, in good time before the voyage, put the vessel in a seaworthy condition: ensure the technical fitness of the vessel for the voyage, properly equip the vessel, man it with a crew and supply it with everything necessary, and also bring the holds and other spaces of the vessel in which the cargo is carried into a condition ensuring the proper receipt, carriage and preservation of the cargo. The carrier is not liable for the unseaworthiness of the vessel if it proves that the unseaworthiness was caused by defects that could not be discovered by the exercise of due diligence (latent defects). 2. The concept of "seaworthiness" is composite, consisting of the following elements Preparation of the vessel as to hull and machinery — ensuring the stability, strength and watertightness of the hull, the soundness of the machinery, mechanisms, fuel, ballast and other systems of the vessel, the supply of the necessary provisions, water, fuel and equipment (apparatus, tools, inventory, replacement and spare parts, instructions, etc.), i.e. the requirements for the elements of the vessel that ensure its technical fitness for the voyage and safe navigation. Confirmation of the seaworthiness of the vessel is the valid documents of the classification society for the equipment and outfit, reports on repairs carried out, etc. Preparation of the cargo spaces, cargo gear and hatch covers for the carriage of the planned cargo. In other words, the possibility of carrying the given cargo in the given vessel. The readiness of the vessel to receive cargo is confirmed by a surveyor\'s certificate, and the readiness of the cargo gear by valid certificates. Manning of the vessel with a qualified crew , preparation of the crew and of the conditions for the crew. Compliance with the requirements of international organisations and national authorities. Here it is also necessary to take into account such characteristics as the flag, class and age of the vessel. Thus, the impossibility of the vessel\'s entry into a port because of a boycott of the vessel\'s flag, or a prohibition on the entry into port of vessels without an ice class, may be regarded as a case of unseaworthiness, i.e. unreadiness for the given voyage. The existing forms for the carriage of cargo or the hire of a vessel solve the problem of formulating the provisions on the seaworthiness of the vessel by entering the necessary requirements for the vessel into the form. A tendency has been noted: the newer the form, the more clauses in it are devoted to the requirements for the seaworthiness of the vessel. Thus, questions of seaworthiness are regulated in parallel by international conventions, regulatory legal acts and customs, on the one hand, and by the contract, on the other. 3. Date the vessel is ready to load In a charter party, the time the vessel is ready to load is set by the shipowner not as a specific date but as an interval between two calendar dates. The first date is briefly called laydays (the full text in the charter-party form is "laydays no commenced before…"). If the vessel arrives at the port earlier, the charterer has the right not to accept it, and laytime will begin to run only after the laydays date. The second date is called the cancelling date (i.e. the date for termination of the contract). If the vessel is not ready to load by that date, the charterer has the right to cancel the charter party. Usually the charter states that laydays begin at the start of the official working hours of the port (e.g. from 8 a.m.) and that the cancelling date expires at the end of the official working hours of the port (e.g. at 4 p.m.). In the course of the negotiations on concluding the charter, the whole interval is called laycan (l/c) and is set as a single whole (e.g. 10–25.06.2026). To avoid disputes, the charter should clearly stipulate the procedure for changing the laycan dates. If the vessel arrived at the port before the laydays date and was placed under loading by the charterer, the contract may provide for one of three options for the calculation of laytime: a condition in the shipowner\'s interest: all time actually used for loading before the laydays date is included in laytime; a condition in the charterer\'s interest: the time before the laydays date is not counted, regardless of whether it was used or not; a compromise: half of the time used before the start of laydays is included in laytime. If necessary, the charterer should include in the addendum to the charter the condition: "no NOR to be tendered before laydays". 4. Cargo As a rule, the contract is concluded for the carriage of a specific cargo. The shipowner is interested in the charter stating not only the full and precise name of the cargo but also all its transport characteristics, i.e. the physical and chemical properties of the cargo that determine the possible loading of the vessel, the duration and cost of the cargo work, and the risks associated with the carriage of that cargo for the given cargo, vessel, crew or other cargoes. First of all the method of carriage and the kind of packing are stated: in bulk, in separate packages, in pallets, big bags or containers. When carrying measurement cargo (stowage factor (SF) over 1.3 m³/t) the charter must state the estimated SF; for round timber — its length and diameter and whether the logs are debarked or not; for sawn timber in packages — the type of package (squared or the so-called tractor package), with dunnage between each row of boards (on stickers) or not, and the size and mass of the package; for metal and other general cargo the type (packages, coils, bundles, bales, bags), dimensions and mass of one piece are stated; for bulk cargo — the SF, the permissible angle of repose, the percentage of moisture; for heavy lifts and long lengths — the method of carriage (crated or not), the dimensions and mass of each piece, and the admissibility of carriage on deck. Special attention must be paid to a full description of the cargo when carrying dangerous goods. Sometimes the charter stipulates that the shipper has the right to present any lawful cargo for carriage. This means that the cargo must pass customs (and, if necessary, quarantine) control both at the port of loading and at the port of destination. As a rule, the charter stipulates a specific quantity of cargo — in metric tonnes, cubic metres (timber cargoes) or in units (containers, vehicles). For general cargo the precise mass is stated, or the qualification "about" is used. In that case a deviation from the stated quantity of ±1–3% is implied, depending on the kind of cargo. Bulk cargoes (dry bulk, timber, liquid, scrap metal) are sold and bought under large, long-term contracts. Therefore the charter usually stipulates a margin — the permissible deviation from the basic quantity of cargo agreed between the shipowner and the charterer for the given voyage. The margin is ±5–10%. Sometimes the charter stipulates that the quantity of cargo within the margin is chosen by the charterer (the condition MOLCHOPT — more or less charterer\'s option). If the shipowner cannot determine in advance how much of a given cargo it will stow on the vessel (unnamed cargo, equipment, etc.), it enters into the charter the condition "full and complete cargo (f+c)". This means that it undertakes to accept as much cargo as is permissible by the deadweight or cargo capacity of the vessel. The stowage of part of the cargo on deck is permitted only with the charterer\'s consent. A corresponding reservation must be entered in the charter (sometimes stating the percentage of the total quantity of cargo). In the bill of lading a stamp "On deck" is made. If the charterer has not presented the whole quantity of cargo declared by the master within the margin, the shipowner is entitled to dead freight. It is calculated as freight at the rate agreed in the charter for the whole quantity of cargo not loaded, less the expenses that the shipowner would have incurred in such carriage. 5. Ports of loading and discharge The ports of loading and discharge may be set in the charter in one of four ways: Direct ports — specific ports. Option of the port — the charter lists several possible ports of loading/discharge, and during the voyage the charterer has the right to send the vessel to any of them. For example, Hamburg or Bremen; Antwerp, Rotterdam, Amsterdam (ARA). Range — the charter states a section of the sea coast bounded by two ports, or a sea basin. During the voyage the charterer has the right to send the vessel to any port of that section, including both bounding ports. For example, the Hamburg–Le Havre range. Broad c/p (wide charter) — an option of ranges. The charter states several ranges, and during the voyages the charterer may send the vessel to any port of any of those ranges. If the possible ports of loading/discharge are not specifically named in the charter — a range or a wide charter — the shipowner must clearly stipulate the requirements for the port. At present it is generally accepted in such cases to include in the charter the clause "good, safe, always afloat, always accessible port" (gsaaaap — good, safe, always afloat, always accessible port). This means that the port nominated by the charterer must have transshipment equipment; be safe in the military, navigational and quarantine senses; be accessible in any weather and at any time of day; and the depths at the berth must permit cargo operations always afloat. 6. Laytime Laytime is the time which, in accordance with the terms of the charter, is allowed to the charterer for the performance of stevedoring operations at the ports of loading and discharge, for which the shipowner receives no additional remuneration other than the agreed freight rate. Preliminary notices. In order for the running of laytime to begin in accordance with the charter, the master must give, at the port of loading (discharge), to the addresses stated in the charter, preliminary notices of the expected date of arrival of the vessel at the port. The number and timing of these notices are stipulated in the contract. Most often the giving of 2–3 notices is provided for, e.g. 15, 10 and 5 days in advance. In that case the last of them is called the final notice (or definite notice). When the voyage is short, the giving of only one preliminary notice may be agreed — after leaving the last previous port. The charter specifically stipulates the master\'s obligation to declare the expected time of arrival of the vessel at the port (ETA). This notice must be given 24 and/or 48 hours before the vessel\'s approach and, as a rule, is not subject to correction. If the vessel is late against the declared time, the charterer is often entitled to demand payment of proven losses for the idle time of gangs, lighters, wagons and the storage of cargo. Notice of readiness (NOR). The second important condition for the beginning of laytime is the giving by the master of a notice of readiness of the vessel for cargo operations, which must be accepted by the charterer. Depending on the agreement of the parties and the customs of the port, the charter may provide for the following alternative conditions as to the place of giving the notice of readiness: after obtaining free pratique and mooring the vessel to the berth; regardless of whether the vessel is at the berth or not, i.e. from an anchorage in the port waters (the WIBON condition); regardless of whether the vessel is in the port or not, i.e. from an open roadstead within sight of the pilot station (the WIPON condition); whether it has obtained free pratique or not (WIFPON); whether it has passed customs control or not (WICCON). In recent years shipowners have most often entered the conditions WIPON, WIBON, WIFPON, WICCON (abbreviated WWWW) into charters or addenda. They give the master the right to tender notice of readiness of the vessel for cargo operations even if the vessel is at an inner or outer roadstead and has not passed sanitary and customs control. Period of grace. Under the conditions of the standard charter-party forms, the charterer is allowed a certain period of grace after the acceptance of the notice of readiness and before the commencement of laytime, which is connected with the practice of ports in booking work gangs in advance. Most often the period of grace is formulated as follows: "if the notice of readiness is accepted before noon, laytime begins to run from 1 (2) p.m.; if the notice is accepted after noon, laytime begins to run from 6 (8) a.m. on the following working day". However, in some specialised charter-party forms other formulations are used, e.g. from 8 a.m. on the following working day, or 24 working hours after the acceptance of the notice. The printed text of the Gencon form states that all time spent waiting for a berth is counted as loading/discharging time. It is in the charterer\'s interest to change this formulation to "laytime…" (further in the text). In that case all stoppages excluded from laytime (bad weather, weekends) will not be counted during the vessel\'s stay at the roadstead either. Calculation of laytime. The calculation of the duration of laytime may be stipulated in the charter at a rate per hatch per day (hour); at a rate per vessel per day (hour); as a number of days (hours); according to the customs of the port; or at customary speed. Usually only working weather days (WWD — weather working day) are counted as laytime, i.e. the charterer is released from liability for the idle time of the vessel on weekends, holidays and days of bad weather. Recently a more precise formulation is used: "weather permitting". Where the charter provides for the loading or discharging of the vessel at several ports, laytime is set as a whole for the relevant kind of cargo operation, and the running of laytime at the second and subsequent ports of loading (discharge) usually begins immediately after the acceptance of the notice of readiness, without a period of grace. Sometimes laytime is calculated as a whole for the entire voyage. This method of calculation is called "reversible". Excluded periods. Days of bad weather, as well as Sundays and holidays, are excluded from laytime (Sundays, holidays excepted — SHEX). If, under the contract, Sundays and holidays are included in laytime, the clause SHINC (Sundays, holidays included) is entered in the charter. If the running of laytime began during the vessel\'s stay at the roadstead, the time of the passage from the anchorage to the berth, as well as the time of customs and sanitary formalities and the time of acceptance of the holds by the charterer\'s surveyors, etc., are excluded from laytime. If the charter provides for the charterer\'s right to load (discharge) the vessel at several berths, the addendum to the charter should state whether the time of shifting berths is included in laytime or not. In all cases any delays or interruptions in cargo work caused by the vessel are excluded from laytime: due to a defect of the vessel\'s cargo gear or hatch covers, or the absence of electric power. Also charged to the shipowner is the time for shifting along the berth, lightering, and trimming of cargo, if this is caused by the non-conformity of the vessel\'s characteristics with the terms of the charter (as to length, draught, suitability for grab discharge). Interruptions in cargo operations caused by the charterer, such as the unreadiness or absence of cargo, the absence of labour or vehicles, etc., are not excluded from laytime. When laytime is calculated in working days, the charterer may, if necessary, perform cargo work also on Sundays and holidays. In these cases the question arises whether the time used for work should be included in laytime. It may be stipulated that Sundays and holidays are excluded from laytime regardless of whether they were used for work or not (whether used or not). The other, opposite approach is expressed in the condition that Sundays and holidays are excluded from laytime only if they were not used for work (unless used). In that case, if the charterer performed work on Sundays and holidays, all time actually used for work is included in laytime. Usually, to remove possible disputes, such a clause is entered in the charter in expanded form: "Unless used, if used, all time actually used shall count". The detailed accounting of the vessel\'s time in port from the moment of mooring to the berth (dropping anchor) until the end of cargo operations and the completion of the cargo documents is recorded in a special document — the statement of facts. On its basis a time sheet is drawn up, which, in addition to information on the actual duration of the vessel\'s stay, gives the calculation of laytime and the amount of despatch (demurrage). Regardless of the form of this document, which differs among shipowners, the time sheet must contain the following data: the time, i.e. the date, hours and minutes of the vessel\'s mooring to the berth or anchoring at the roadstead (in the latter case the time of the vessel\'s mooring to the berth is stated separately); the time of the master\'s tendering the notice of readiness and of its acceptance by the consignor\'s (consignee\'s) agent; the charter rate of cargo work, the total quantity of cargo and the duration of laytime; the time of the actual beginning and end and the total duration of the cargo work; daily data on the duration of laytime and of the periods excluded from laytime; the calculation of the amount of demurrage or despatch on the results of the handling of the vessel. The time sheet is signed by the shipowner (its broker) and sent to the charterer for agreement and payment. 7. Payment of despatch/demurrage If the vessel is delayed under loading or discharging beyond the agreed laytime, the so-called time on demurrage begins, for which the charterer pays the shipowner demurrage, which is an additional payment to the agreed freight for the delay of the vessel. The amount of demurrage is stated in the charter as a specific sum per vessel per day, or less often as a rate per 1 gross registered tonne of capacity, and is paid for each day of idle time and proportionally for part of a day. In a number of charters for the carriage of cargo with high rates of cargo work (ore, coal), an hourly rather than a daily demurrage rate is set. Time on demurrage is always counted in running days, so from the moment laytime expires demurrage is payable for all days without exception — Sundays, holidays, days of bad weather, etc. It is understood that if the vessel had been handled on time it would not have been in port during those weekends or days of bad weather. The only thing excluded from time on demurrage is stoppages caused by the vessel. Some charter-party forms stipulate a certain maximum number of days on demurrage, i.e. days during which the charterer has the right to detain the vessel against payment of demurrage, e.g. under the Gencon-76 charter — 10 running days at the ports of loading and discharge together. On the expiry of the stipulated number of days on demurrage the shipowner is entitled, having warned the charterer, to send the vessel out of the port of loading with the quantity of cargo already loaded and to demand payment of dead freight for the short loading. However, the parties usually agree on the amount of compensation payable by the charterer for the detention of the vessel beyond the time on demurrage. Such detention is called detention beyond the time on demurrage (detention), and for each day of such delay payment is provided at a rate exceeding the demurrage rate by a certain percentage, or in the form of a specific additional daily rate paid on top of the demurrage rate. The charterer is interested in removing from the printed text of the charter-party form the indication of the maximum number of days on demurrage. Gencon-94 provides that demurrage is paid immediately upon receipt of the shipowner\'s invoice. Otherwise the shipowner must give the charterer written notice, and on the expiry of 96 hours, if the vessel is at the port of loading, it has the right to cancel the transaction and present all its losses to the charterer. Despatch money is remuneration paid by the shipowner to the charterer for completing the loading or discharging of the vessel before the expiry of the laytime stipulated by the charter, i.e. for the so-called "saved time". The amount of despatch is usually set at half the demurrage rate, on the consideration that the shipowner should share with the charterer the additional benefit obtained as a result of the early handling of the vessel. The basis for receiving despatch must be the existence of a corresponding condition in the charter. Shipowners have recently been seeking a clause in the charter abolishing despatch (free despatch), or at least reducing its amount. Instead of the previously common condition that despatch is paid for all time saved (all time saved), charters now stipulate its payment only for working time saved (working time saved), i.e. excluding non-working days. 8. Payment of cargo work The variants for allocating the costs of loading and discharging cargo between the shipowner and the charterer used in voyage charter parties may be reduced to the following six standard schemes: the shipowner bears the costs of cargo work both at the port of loading and at the port of discharge (gross terms); the shipowner is free of the cost of loading, but bears the costs of stowing the cargo and of discharging it (free in); the shipowner is free of the cost of loading and stowing the cargo, but bears the costs of discharging (free in and stowed); the shipowner is free of the cost of discharging, but pays for loading and stowing the cargo (free out); the shipowner is free of the costs of loading and discharging the cargo, but pays for its stowage — free in and out (fio); the shipowner is free of the cost of loading, discharging and stowing the cargo — free in and out and stowed (fios) — most frequently used. The charter specifically stipulates the allocation of the costs of separation, dunnage and securing (unsecuring) materials. In most standard charter-party forms these costs are charged to the charterer (FIOS L/S/D). In performing cargo operations it is often necessary to resort to overtime. The shipowner may need this to avoid, for example, the vessel\'s being delayed in port over Saturday and Sunday or other holidays, in connection with a long wait for a spring tide to leave the port, or the threat of the vessel\'s being late for the cancelling date of the next voyage, etc. The charterer may resort to overtime in order to keep within the allowed laytime and to ship the goods within the time provided for by the sale contract. The usual condition contained in charters is the payment of overtime by the party ordering it (overtime for account of the party ordering same). Such practice occurs even in the absence of a corresponding condition in the charter. 9. Freight In voyage chartering, the freight is set by agreement of the parties most often as a rate of freight per unit of cargo. The freight units are usually units of mass, volume or a piece of cargo. In liner shipping, for the carriage of especially valuable cargoes, freight is also calculated as a percentage of the value of the cargo (ad valorem). Since, for a number of reasons, the quantity of cargo received at the port of destination does not always exactly correspond to the quantity of cargo delivered at the port of loading, the charter stipulates for what quantity of cargo the freight is paid: for the loaded (intaken) quantity, i.e. the bill-of-lading quantity of cargo, or for the delivered quantity at the port of destination. Where it is impossible to establish in advance how much cargo the vessel can take, or it is clear in advance that neither the deadweight nor the cargo capacity of the vessel can be properly used, the freight is stipulated as a total sum for the voyage, which is called "lumpsum". The time of payment of freight may be stipulated differently in the charter. The shipowner is interested in receiving payment of freight at the port of loading immediately after the signing of the bills of lading, or within a stipulated number of days after the signing of the bills of lading for the shipped cargo. Usually the formula "freight payable w/i 3 (5) days after signing B/L but in any case BBB (before breaking bulk)" is used, i.e. in any case before the start of discharge. This clause gives the shipowner the right not to permit discharge until the freight is paid, with all the vessel\'s idle time for that reason being included in laytime. The addendum to the charter must stipulate that the B/L should bear the note "Freight payable as per charterparty". The charterer\'s obligation to pay freight cannot be linked to its right to receive compensation for damaged cargo or other losses caused to it. Freight is paid in full even if the charterer has justified claims against the shipowner. The shipowner may insure its risks of loss of freight in the event of the loss of all or part of the cargo, but such insurance relates only to the case of loss of the cargo in the course of carriage and does not protect against dishonest or insolvent charterers. Where freight is paid at the port of loading, shipowners usually enter the following condition into the charter (addendum): "Freight is deemed earned upon the loading of the cargo and is not subject to deductions and is not returnable, whether the vessel and/or cargo are lost or not lost" (in correspondence the abbreviation DEDANRVAOLONL — deemed earned, discountless and non-returnable, vessel and/or cargo lost or not lost). In addition to the payment of freight for the cargo, the charter provides for the payment by the charterer to the shipowner of the so-called dead freight, if it has not provided the vessel with the full cargo stipulated by the charter, taking into account the margin. If the charterer has not provided the whole quantity of cargo provided for by the charter, then in order to obtain dead freight the agent and/or the master of the vessel must take the following measures: Obtain a written declaration from the charterer that no more cargo will be delivered to the vessel. After the end of loading, call in an independent surveyor and obtain from it an official report on the extent of the unused deadweight and cargo capacity of the vessel. Enter into the bill of lading a reservation on the shipowner\'s claim for dead freight in connection with the short loading of the vessel. Such a reservation in the bill of lading enables the shipowner to exercise its lien on the cargo if the charterer fails to pay the dead freight in time and in full. Enter into the charter a clause that dead freight and/or demurrage at the port of loading are paid by the charterer within a stipulated number of days after the signing of the bill of lading, but in any case before the opening of the holds at the port of discharge (the BBB condition — before breaking bulk). 10. Other conditions of the charter Agents servicing the vessel at the ports of loading and discharge. The shipowner is interested in the vessel\'s being serviced by an agency company with which it has a corresponding long-term contract. But the usual practice is that the port agents are appointed by the charterer. The forwarders of the consignor and consignee can competently recommend a reliable agent, respectively at the ports of loading and discharge, or take upon themselves the agency of the vessel. Large consignors usually insist that their agency companies be appointed under the charter to service the vessel in the ports. As a result of negotiations any possible variant may be entered into the charter by agreement of the parties: the shipowner\'s agent at the ports of loading and discharge (owners agent both ends — abbreviated oabends or oabe); the charterer\'s agent at both ports (charterers agent both ends — abbreviated chabends or chabe); the shipowner\'s agent at one of the ports (loading or discharge), the charterer\'s agent at the other. Taxes and charges. A standard condition is entered into the charter (addendum) that all charges and dues on the vessel are paid by the shipowner, and all charges and dues on the cargo and freight are paid by the charterer. Sometimes the charterer stipulates that the dues on freight are paid by the shipowner. In that case the shipowner may demand a corresponding increase in the freight rate. Under the Gencon-94 form, box 23 of part 1 of the charter must state whether the tax on freight is levied on the shipowner. Extra insurance of cargo. The question of the extra insurance of cargo for the age, class and/or flag of the vessel is specifically stipulated. The addendum states at whose expense it is paid: the shipowner\'s, the charterer\'s, or at the shipowner\'s expense but not more than a stipulated sum (a compromise). Also, as a compromise, the clause "extra insurance charterer\'s account but owners to contribute USD…" may be entered into the addendum to the charter. Brokerage commission. On signing the charter, the broker through whom the transaction was concluded is stated in it. The brokerage commission is set as a specific percentage charged on all payments due to the shipowner — freight, dead freight and demurrage. Often two brokers take part in concluding a transaction — one on the shipowner\'s side and one on the charterer\'s side. The charter may stipulate that the brokerage commission is paid directly by the charterer, which transfers to the shipowner only the remaining part of the freight (less commission). Shipowners\' liability. Shipowners are liable for the loss of or damage to cargo, or for delay in the delivery of cargo, only if such damage or delay was caused by a lack of due diligence on their part to make the vessel in all respects seaworthy, properly equipped, manned and supplied before the voyage, or if the damage and delay were caused by the personal acts or omissions of the shipowners or their manager in ensuring the preservation of the cargo. Deviation. Unless otherwise agreed by the partners, the vessel has the right to call at ports in any order, to sail without pilots, to tow or render other assistance to vessels, and to deviate from the route for the purpose of saving life or property. Sometimes, in the course of negotiations, after the words "saving life" the shipowner adds "including the life of crew members", meaning that such deviation should not be regarded as a breach of the condition of the contract on the seaworthiness of the vessel before the voyage. In addition, after the word "property" the shipowner may write in "and also for bunkering", especially if at the port of loading there is a shortage of the necessary grades of fuel or high fuel prices are quoted. Lien. The shipowner has a lien on the cargo as security for the payment of freight, dead freight, demurrage and claims for damage to the vessel, including all expenses for obtaining those payments. To exercise the lien the shipowner may either withhold the discharge of all or part of the cargo, or deliver such cargo at the port of discharge to the warehouses of the agent, the stevedoring company or the port with an instruction not to release the cargo to the consignee until the charterer has paid the whole amount of its debt. Bills of lading. Bills of lading are issued on the basis of the standard form provided for by the given charter-party form. They are signed by the master of the vessel or by the shipowner\'s agent. In the latter case the shipowner must issue a written power of attorney to the agent, a copy of which the agent must present to the charterer. The charterers indemnify the shipowner for any losses that may arise because, under the bill of lading, more onerous obligations than those assumed by it under the charter may be imposed on the shipowner. Mutual liability in collision. The clause establishes the principle of the carriers\' liability to the cargo owners for damage to and loss of cargo on the colliding vessels in the amount of the established fault. General average and the Jason clause. A standard clause making reference to the York-Antwerp Rules. Charters, as a rule, refer to the latest revision of the rules. Often, in addition to the reference to the York-Antwerp Rules, this clause also states the place where the adjustment is to be made, e.g. London. Besides London, specialists (average adjusters) who can conduct general average adjustments are available in Moscow, New York and other places. In the absence of an agreement of the parties, the relations arising from general average are governed by the law of the state in whose port the vessel ended its voyage after the occurrence giving rise to the general average. When the vessel operates to US ports, the Jason clause must be included. Later it became known as the Jason clause. Subsequent decisions required certain changes to the text of the clause. It is now usually called the "new Jason clause". General strike clause. As to a strike or lockout that may affect the duration of the voyage, three cases are considered. If a strike at the port of loading began at the time the vessel is ready to leave the previous port, or during the passage to the port of loading, or on the vessel\'s arrival at that port, the shipowners request the charterers\' consent to count laytime as if there had been no strike (lockout). If the charterers do not give their consent in writing or by telegraph within 24 hours, the shipowners have the right to cancel the charter (i.e., if the charterer consents, the first 24 hours of the vessel\'s idle time are for the shipowner\'s account, the rest for the charterer\'s account). If part of the cargo has been loaded, the shipowners have the right to send the vessel on the voyage with that cargo (freight is paid only for the quantity actually loaded) and to complete the loading with incidental cargo in their own interest. If a strike or lockout takes place at the port of discharge and is not settled within 48 hours, the charterers have the right to hold the vessel awaiting the end of the strike (lockout), paying half demurrage on the expiry of the time allowed for discharge until the end of the strike (lockout). After that, for the whole time until the complete end of discharge, demurrage is paid at the full rate. But the charterer has the right to send the vessel to another, safe port where it can discharge without the risk of delay because of the strike (lockout). Such instruction must be given to the vessel within 48 hours after the master or the shipowners notified the charterers that the strike is preventing discharge. If the cargo is delivered at the alternative port, all the conditions of the charter and bill of lading remain in force. However, if the sea passage to the new port exceeds 100 miles, the freight is increased in proportion to the increase in the total length of the voyage. Apart from the above obligations, neither the charterer nor the shipowner bears liability for any other consequences of the strike (lockout). War risks (Voywar 1993). War risks include war or the threat of war, hostilities, civil war, acts of war, revolution, mutiny, civil unrest, the laying of mines, piracy, acts of terror, hostile actions and blockade — against all vessels or vessels of a specific flag, specific cargoes or crews, imposed by any person, body, terrorist or political group or government, which in the reasonable judgement of the master and/or the shipowner may be dangerous or may become dangerous to the vessel, its cargo, crew or other persons on board. If war risk in a specific port or group of ports becomes known before the vessel begins loading, the shipowner may demand that the charterer replace those ports with other safe ones within the same range. If the charterer does not nominate such safe port or ports within 48 hours of the shipowner\'s notice, the latter has the right to cancel the contract or that part of it connected with the war risks. If war risks become known after the start of loading, or during the passage to the port of discharge, or in the course of discharge, the shipowners are not obliged to continue loading (discharging), to sign bills of lading for an unsafe port, or to instruct the master to remain in an unsafe port or to continue to a place where the vessel, its cargo, crew or other persons on board will be exposed to war risks. The shipowners have the right to tender notice to the charterers demanding the nomination of a safe port for the discharge of the cargo or part of the cargo. If within 48 hours after the receipt of such notice the charterers do not nominate a safe port, the shipowners may discharge the accepted cargo or part of the cargo in any safe port of their choice (including the port of loading) with all the other conditions of the charter remaining in force. If the discharge is made at any port other than the port of loading, the shipowner is entitled to the full amount of freight. All additional expenses incurred in discharging the cargo at the port of loading or in delivering or discharging the cargo at any port must be paid by the charterer or the cargo owner. The shipowner has a lien on the cargo as security for all payments due to it. The shipowner also has the right to reimbursement of its additional expenses of discharge. As security for its additional expenses and freight, the shipowner may use the lien on the cargo. If war risks in a specific region require the taking of a new, unusual route for the vessel, the shipowners must notify the charterer that such route will be taken. If the length of the voyage increases by more than 100 miles, the shipowners have the right to a proportional increase in the amount of freight. On the occurrence of war risks, the vessel is entitled to comply with any orders and recommendations of the authorities, of war-risk insurers, of the UN Security Council, the European Union, or any body or group empowered to compel compliance with their orders or directions. The shipowner has the right to discharge in any port cargo that may lead to the confiscation of the vessel as a carrier of contraband goods. The vessel may also call at any port to change the crew or part of it, or to put ashore other persons on board, if there are grounds to suppose that those persons may be interned, arrested or subjected to other sanctions. General ice clause. If the port of loading has become inaccessible because of ice conditions at the time the vessel is ready to leave the last previous port, during the sea passage or on arrival at that port, the shipowner, because of the danger of being frozen in the ice, has the right to cancel the charter. If the danger of the vessel\'s being frozen in arose in the course of loading, the master has the right to take the vessel out of the port with the quantity of cargo on board and to complete the loading at any port in the shipowner\'s interest. The cargo loaded under the charter must be delivered to its destination without additional expense to the charterer, against payment of freight for the whole quantity of cargo delivered. If loading under the charter is to be made at two or more ports, some of which are closed by ice, the shipowner has the right to load only that part of the cargo that comes from the open port and then to complete the loading in its own interest at other ports, or to cancel the charter if the charterer does not agree to load the full cargo at the open port. If ice prevents the vessel from reaching the port of discharge, the charterers have the right either to detain the vessel until navigation resumes, paying demurrage, or to send the vessel to another, safe and immediately accessible port where it can discharge without the risk of being detained by ice. The corresponding instruction must be given within 48 hours after the shipowners notified the charterers of the impossibility of reaching the original port of destination. If, in the course of discharge, because of the danger of being frozen in, the master considers it necessary to leave the port, it has the right to do so and to discharge the remaining cargo at the nearest safe port. After the delivery of the cargo at such port, all the conditions of the bill of lading remain in force, and the vessel must receive all the freight due. If the distance to the safe port exceeds 100 miles, the freight for the cargo delivered to that port is increased proportionally. The ice clause is provided for cases where ice conditions at the agreed ports of loading and discharge are an unusual force-majeure circumstance. If the ice conditions in the given region and at the given time of year are the usual conditions of navigation, the ice clause is replaced by the icebreaker clause. This clause defines the vessel\'s obligation to follow an icebreaker in ice, establishes whether the vessel may tender notice of readiness from the ice edge, whether waiting for an icebreaker is included in laytime, who bears the risks when the vessel follows an icebreaker, etc. Law and arbitration. This clause determines under the law of which country and in which city disputes between the partners under the charter are to be considered. The Gencon-94 charter provides for three variants. The dispute may be considered in London under English law, or in New York under American law, or in any other place by agreement of the parties. The chosen variant is stated on the face of the charter in the corresponding box. Under English law it is provided that if the parties have not agreed to appoint a single arbitrator, each party appoints one arbitrator and both of them appoint a third. The arbitration tribunal thus constituted reaches a decision unanimously or by a majority, and such decision is final. On receipt by one party of written notice of the appointment of an arbitrator by the other party, it must state its arbitrator within 14 days, otherwise the decision taken by the single arbitrator is final. Under American law the appointment of three arbitrators is mandatory, and a court order may be obtained to enforce the arbitrators\' decision.'},
  {t:'CMR Convention (1956) — International Logistics', u:'cmr-convention.html', text:'Home → Road freight → CMR Convention CMR Convention (1956) The CMR Convention (from French Convention relative au contrat de transport international de marchandises par route ) is the Convention on the Contract for the International Carriage of Goods by Road, signed in Geneva on 19 May 1956. It is the principal international instrument governing the carriage of goods by road between contracting states: it establishes a uniform consignment note, the terms of the contract of carriage, and the carrier\'s liability. The Russian Federation is a party to the Convention (as the legal successor to the USSR, which ratified it in 1983). Scope of application (art. 1) The Convention applies to the carriage of goods by road for reward where two conditions are met simultaneously: the place of taking over the goods and the place of delivery are situated in two different countries ; at least one of those countries is a contracting party to the CMR Convention . The Convention applies regardless of the place of residence and nationality of the parties to the contract (sender, carrier, consignee). This means that if goods move, for example, from Russia (a contracting party) to a third country, the CMR applies even where the carrier is a company from a state that has not acceded to the Convention. What falls OUTSIDE the CMR (art. 1, para. 4) The Convention does not apply to: carriage of postal consignments under international postal conventions; carriage of the deceased ; carriage of furniture removals (household effects in the course of a move). The CMR consignment note The contract of carriage is confirmed by the CMR consignment note . Key rules: Three copies (art. 5) — signed by the sender and the carrier: the first for the sender, the second accompanying the goods, the third for the carrier. In practice, additional copies are often made (for customs, settlement, the freight forwarder). Particulars (art. 6) — the date and place of issue, the names and addresses of the sender, carrier and consignee, a description of the goods (nature, packing, number of packages), gross weight, places of loading and unloading, related charges, customs instructions, etc. The absence or irregularity of the consignment note does not affect the validity of the contract of carriage itself (art. 4): the contract exists even without a note, and the CMR remains the evidential document. Important: the CMR note is not a document of title (unlike a bill of lading). It cannot be "transferred" by endorsement as a right to the goods; it merely records the contract of carriage and the taking over of the goods. Carrier\'s liability (art. 17–29) Period of liability The carrier is liable for the goods from the moment of taking them over until the moment of delivery to the consignee (art. 17, para. 1), and for delay in delivery. Presumption of fault The CMR operates a presumption of fault of the carrier: loss, damage or delay are presumed to have been caused by the carrier unless he proves otherwise. The burden of proof lies with the carrier. Grounds for exoneration (art. 17, paras. 2, 4) The carrier is relieved of liability if he proves that the loss or damage was caused by: the fault of the person entitled, or an instruction of his (sender/consignee); defective packing or containers; the special inherent nature of the goods; circumstances which the carrier could not avoid and the consequences of which he was unable to prevent (force majeure). "Special risks" are singled out separately (art. 17, para. 4): carriage in open vehicles, absence or damage to packing, loading/stowage by the sender, the nature of perishable goods, etc. — for these there is a presumption of exoneration of the carrier where the goods were carried under the agreed conditions. Limit of liability — 8.33 SDR per kilogram For loss of or damage to goods, compensation is limited to 8.33 units of account (SDR) per kilogram of gross weight of the lost or damaged goods (art. 23, para. 3). Carriage charges, customs duties and other costs connected with the carriage are refunded in addition. For delay in delivery, compensation does not exceed the amount of the carriage charges (art. 23, para. 5). SDR (Special Drawing Rights) — what they are and how they work The SDR (Special Drawing Rights) is an international unit of account created by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) in 1969 as a supplement to the official reserves of member countries. It is not a currency and not a means of payment — the SDR has no banknotes or coins. The currency basket The value of the SDR is determined on the basis of a basket of five world currencies (the composition is reviewed by the IMF every five years): the US dollar; the euro; the Chinese yuan (added to the basket in 2016); the Japanese yen; the pound sterling. How the rate is determined The IMF calculates and publishes the SDR exchange rate against the major currencies daily , based on market quotes of the basket currencies. To convert a liability limit into a national currency, one takes the current SDR rate (published by the IMF and central banks) and multiplies it by the number of kilograms. Why SDRs are used in conventions SDRs are used so that liability limits in international transport conventions are not eroded by the inflation of a single currency and do not depend on exchange-rate fluctuations. Besides the CMR, the SDR is used in the Montreal Convention (aviation) and the Hague-Visby Rules (sea carriage); within the CMR itself, the 1978 Protocol replaced the former unit (the Poincaré franc) with the SDR. Example: goods weighing 1,000 kg gross are lost. The limit = 1,000 × 8.33 = 8,330 SDR. If 1 SDR ≈ USD 1.35, the maximum compensation ≈ USD 11,245 (the exact figure is calculated at the rate on the date of settlement). Reservations on taking over and delivering the goods (art. 8–9, 30) On taking over the goods the carrier checks the apparent condition of the goods and their packing, the number of packages and the marks (art. 8). Where a check is impossible, he enters a reasoned reservation in the note. A note containing no reservations is prima facie evidence that the goods and their packing were in apparent good condition on taking over (art. 9). Where the goods are received without reservations, they are deemed to have been delivered in accordance with the note (art. 30) — therefore damage must be recorded before signing the delivery documents. Limitation periods (art. 32) The general period is one year from the date of delivery of the goods (or the date on which they should have been delivered). In the case of the carrier\'s wilful misconduct , or default considered equivalent to wilful misconduct, the period is three years . Successive carriers (art. 34–40) Where carriage is performed by several carriers successively under a single CMR note, each carrier is liable for the whole of the journey , and their liability is joint and several . A claim may be brought against the first carrier, the last carrier, or the carrier on whose leg the loss or damage occurred. The 1978 Protocol The Protocol to the CMR Convention (Geneva, 1978) replaced the former unit of account (the Poincaré franc) with the SDR . This is why all current limits in the CMR are expressed in SDRs. Practical significance: for international road carriage between CMR states, a contract of carriage under the Convention and a CMR consignment note are mandatory. The CMR does not apply to domestic carriage within Russia — there a consignment note (TN/ETrN) under the Rules for the Carriage of Goods by Road applies. ← Back to the road regulatory framework'},
  {t:'Contact — International Logistics', u:'contact.html', text:'Home → Contact Contact me Have a question about international logistics or foreign trade, or a proposal for cooperation? Write to me — I will answer personally. Your name (optional) Your email (for a reply) Message Send'},
  {t:'Reviewers and market contributions — International Logistics', u:'contributors.html', text:'Home → Reviewers and market contributions Reviewers and market contributions Dear colleagues! The materials on this site are the result of years of practice and constant verification against primary sources, but the market and legislation change quickly. I will be sincerely grateful for your comments, corrections and remarks about errors or outdated information. The names of reviewers who have contributed to the development of the site are published below, in the "Reviewers" section. Accepting market contributions I welcome materials from market participants — notes, articles and posts on international logistics and foreign trade. Please send your materials with attribution — the best will be published on the site. You can send a comment, correction or material through the "Contact" section. Reviewers A list of reviewers who have contributed to the development of the site. Published with the consent of the authors of the comments. The list is updated as feedback is received.'},
  {t:'Currency control — International Logistics', u:'currency-control.html', text:'Home → Customs clearance → Currency control Currency control Currency control is the state\'s supervision over settlements between residents and non-residents under foreign-trade contracts. Its purpose is to prevent unjustified capital outflows and to ensure the repatriation of foreign-currency earnings. Transaction passport → UCN The transaction passport (TP) is a historical currency-control document that has been abolished. It was replaced by the registration of the contract with an authorised bank and the assignment of a unique contract number (UCN) . UCN — a number that the bank assigns to a contract upon registration (Instruction of the Bank of Russia No. 181-I). Who completes it: the resident does not "fill in" any forms himself — he submits to the bank the details of the contract and a copy of it , and the bank registers the contract within one working day and issues the UCN. A separate "passport" in the form of a paper form is no longer required — registration is kept in the bank\'s system. Contract registration thresholds The threshold is determined by the amount of obligations converted at the CBR exchange rate on the date the contract is signed (effective from 2026): Type of contract Mandatory registration threshold Export from RUB 10m Import from RUB 3m Loan agreement (borrowing) from RUB 3m Below the threshold the contract is not registered and no UCN is required — but settlements still pass through the bank with the indication of operation-type codes. How the currency-control system is structured The system is two-tier: Currency-control bodies — the Government of the Russian Federation and the Bank of Russia (CBR) . They set the rules and exercise supervision. Currency-control agents — directly check operations and documents: authorised banks — the principal link: they register the contract, assign the UCN and control payments; the FCS (customs authorities) — reconcile data with the goods declarations; the FTS (tax authorities); VEB.RF and professional securities-market participants. Documents and reporting SPD — a certificate of supporting documents: submitted to the bank for each delivery/payment within the established time limits. Bank control statement (VBK) — generated by the bank for a registered contract. Liability — art. 15.25 of the Russian Code of Administrative Offences: a breach of currency legislation entails heavy fines (up to 100% of the amount of the unlawful operation). Practical advice: watch the deadlines for submitting the SPD and the correspondence between the payment amount and the value of the goods in the declaration. A discrepancy is a frequent cause of fines in currency control.'},
  {t:'Calculation of customs payments — International Logistics', u:'customs-calculation.html', text:'Home → Customs clearance → Calculation of customs payments Calculation of customs payments Below is the algorithm for calculating customs duty and VAT on the importation of goods into the customs territory of Russia, with a real example. Calculation algorithm Determine the customs value (CV) = value of the goods + cost of carriage to the EAEU border + insurance. Calculate the import duty = CV × duty rate (by TN VED code). Calculate VAT = (CV + duty) × VAT rate (usually 20%). Total payments = duty + VAT (+ customs fees for operations). An important nuance: the customs value includes carriage only up to the EAEU border . Carriage within the customs territory (for example, Vladivostok → Moscow) is not included in the CV where it is itemised separately in the documents and not included in the price of the goods. Including the full freight to the final destination in the CV is a frequent mistake leading to overpayment of VAT. A real example Product: Deye SUN-6K-SG05LP1-EU-SM2-P hybrid solar inverter, 6 kW. Delivery: Shanghai → Moscow, one 20-foot container. Initial data Parameter Value TN VED code 8504 40 840 0 (static converters ≤ 7.5 kVA) Import duty rate 0% VAT rate 20% Value of the goods in the container RUB 3,453,750 Freight Shanghai → Vladivostok USD 3,500 CBR exchange rate (on the settlement date) 82.9803 RUB/USD Step 1. Freight in rubles 3,500 × 82.9803 = RUB 290,431.05 Step 2. Insurance premium Insurance base = freight + value of the goods = 290,431.05 + 3,453,750 = RUB 3,744,181.05 . Insured amount = base × 110% = 3,744,181.05 × 1.10 = RUB 4,118,599.16 . Insurance premium = insured amount × 0.5% = 4,118,599.16 × 0.005 = RUB 20,593.00 . Step 3. Customs value CV = goods + freight to the border + insurance = 3,453,750 + 290,431.05 + 20,593.00 = RUB 3,764,774.05 . Step 4. Import duty CV × 0% = RUB 0 . Step 5. VAT (CV + duty) × 20% = 3,764,774.05 × 0.20 = RUB 752,954.81 . Result Payment Amount Import duty RUB 0 VAT 20% RUB 752,954.81 Total customs payments RUB 752,954.81 Customs operations fee (for reference) ~RUB 7,500 The importer\'s full cost (goods + freight + insurance + VAT) ≈ RUB 4,517,729 — excluding the Vladivostok → Moscow carriage. Note: the calculation is given for educational purposes. Rates, exchange rates and thresholds may change — before a shipment, verify them against current data (EAEU TN VED, the Common Customs Tariff, the CBR exchange rate).'},
  {t:'Customs clearance — International Logistics', u:'customs-clearance.html', text:'Home → Customs clearance Customs clearance Customs clearance is the placing of goods under one of the customs procedures provided for by the Customs Code of the EAEU. The chosen procedure determines which payments are made, which documents are submitted, and what may be done with the goods after release. TN VED / HS code The structure of the 10-digit code, how it is formed and how to classify a product correctly. Currency control The UCN (unique contract number) instead of the transaction passport, registration thresholds, who and how controls. Calculation of customs payments The algorithm for calculating duty and VAT on import + a real example on an inverter. 1. Main EAEU customs procedures The customs procedure code in the goods declaration (DT) consists of the direction of movement ( EK — export, IM — import) and a two-digit procedure code . The classifier is approved by Decision No. 378 of the Customs Union Commission of 20.09.2010. DT code Customs procedure EU equivalent Essence EK-10 Export EX-1 export of EAEU goods abroad without an obligation to re-import EK-21 Outward processing EX-2 (outward processing) export for processing, return of processed products EK-23 Temporary export EX-6 (temporary export) export with an obligation to re-import (exhibitions, equipment) EK-31 Re-export — export of previously imported foreign goods IM-40 Release for home use IM-4 (free circulation) the main import — the goods remain in the country, all duties paid IM-51 Inward processing IM-5 (inward processing) import of raw materials for processing and subsequent export of the product IM-53 Temporary admission IM-9 (temporary admission) import with an obligation to export (rental, equipment) IM-60 Re-import IM-6 (re-import) return of previously exported goods without payment of duties IM-70 Customs warehouse IM-7 (warehousing) storage of foreign goods without payment of duties IM-77 / EK-77 Customs transit T1 / T2 carriage of goods under customs control through the territory IM-90 Destruction — destruction of goods under customs control IM-91 Abandonment to the state — transfer of goods to the state IM-93 Duty-free trade — duty-free shops IM-94 Special customs procedure — diplomatic cargo, military property IM-96 Inward processing for home use — processing with payment of duties on the product Note: there are also the codes 00 (no preceding procedure), 78 (free customs zone) and 80 (free warehouse). 2. Customs transit: T1 / T2 — clarification The correspondence of the transit codes is determined by the nature of the goods , not by the "import/export" direction: EU Nature of the goods EAEU T1 — external transit non-Union (foreign) goods IM-77 T2 — internal transit Union goods EK-77 3. EX-1 / IM-4 — where they are used, where there are separate forms EX-1 and IM-4 are codes of the European Union\'s Single Administrative Document (SAD). They are used: in all 27 EU member states — the single SAD form (Union Customs Code, UCC); in Turkey — a customs union with the EU, a similar system of codes; in the United Kingdom (after Brexit) — its own CDS system, but the codes are inherited from the SAD. Separate declaration forms in countries outside the EU: Russia and the EAEU — the goods declaration (DT) with EK/IM codes; the USA — Entry Summary (CBP Form 7501), the ACE system; China — a customs declaration of its own format; Switzerland, Norway, Iceland (EFTA) — not part of the EU customs union; national forms. The correspondence EK-10 ↔ EX-1 and IM-40 ↔ IM-4 is correct in substance (export / free circulation), but these are different documents in different legal systems .'},
  {t:'Air carriage of dangerous goods — International Logistics', u:'dangerous-goods-air.html', text:'Home → Dangerous goods → Air transport Air carriage of dangerous goods Air carriage of dangerous goods is governed by the ICAO Technical Instructions for the Safe Transport of Dangerous Goods by Air (Doc 9284) and the IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations (DGR) . Key particulars of air carriage Air transport imposes the strictest requirements on dangerous goods because of the risks in flight (pressure differential, temperature, vibration): Many goods are forbidden for air carriage or permitted only on cargo aircraft (CAO — Cargo Aircraft Only). The shipper\'s declaration for dangerous goods (DGD) is mandatory. Strict limits on packaging and the maximum quantity per package apply. Forbidden and restricted goods Completely forbidden for air carriage (under any conditions): substances capable of igniting or exploding under normal conditions of carriage; many radioactive and infectious materials (except for certain exceptions); spontaneously combustible and oxidising substances in a number of combinations. Some goods are permitted only on cargo aircraft (CAO) , without passengers. Shipper\'s declaration (DGD) The consignor completes the DGD — a document confirming that the goods are correctly classified, packed, marked and fit for air carriage. Without a correct DGD, the airline will not accept the goods for carriage. Marking and hazard labels Each package with dangerous goods must bear hazard labels, the UN number and the proper shipping name. For lithium batteries, special marking applies (UN 3480/3481). Practical tip: for air carriage of dangerous goods, always involve an agent or service certified by IATA for dangerous goods (DGR). Lithium batteries are the most common "trap": their rules change, and incorrect packing/marking leads to refusal of acceptance.'},
  {t:'Rail carriage of dangerous goods — International Logistics', u:'dangerous-goods-rail.html', text:'Home → Dangerous goods → Rail transport Rail carriage of dangerous goods The carriage of dangerous goods by rail is governed by the Rules for the Carriage of Dangerous Goods by Rail (PPOG; approved by the Council for Railway Transport of the CIS member states, protocol No. 15 of 05.04.1996, as amended). The main provisions are set out below. General provisions (Chapter 1) Application of the rules The Rules apply to the carriage of dangerous goods on the railways of the member states of the Commonwealth and are mandatory for railway transport workers, consignors and consignees of dangerous goods, ports, and freight-forwarding enterprises. In matters not provided for by the Rules, the general rules for the carriage of goods by rail apply. Carriage of dangerous goods in bulk is performed in accordance with the Rules for the Carriage of Liquid Goods in Bulk in Tank Wagons. Carriage between SMGS member states that are not members of the Commonwealth is governed by the Rules for the Carriage of Dangerous Goods — Annex 2 to the SMGS ; between Commonwealth states and states not participating in the SMGS — on the basis of special agreements. Classification of dangerous goods Dangerous goods include substances, materials, articles and waste which, during transport, loading/unloading operations and storage, may harm the environment or cause an explosion, fire, damage to vehicles, or the death, injury, poisoning, burns or illness of people and animals. By the nature of the hazard they are divided into 9 classes (explosive substances, gases, flammable liquids, flammable solids, oxidising, toxic, radioactive, corrosive, miscellaneous) — in accordance with the UN Model Regulations. The assignment of a good to a specific class, category and compatibility group is made by the consignor . Goods admitted to carriage Goods listed in the Alphabetical List of Dangerous Goods (Annex 2) and the List of Class 1 Dangerous Goods (Annex 10) are admitted to carriage. Goods similar in properties to those listed but not named are assigned to the relevant UN number (generic name or "N.O.S.") and carried under the conditions of that number. For goods that cannot be assigned to the list, special conditions of carriage may be established (under national legislation in domestic traffic, or under the Agreement on the Carriage of Goods under Special Conditions — in international traffic). Completion of documents For each consignment the consignor submits to the station a consignment note completed under the SMGS and these Rules. In the "Description of goods" column the following are stated in sequence: the hazard code, after a slash — the UN number, the proper shipping name of the dangerous goods, the number of the main (and, in brackets, the subsidiary) hazard label, the classification code (for gases — the code), the emergency card number. Example: "336/UN 1230 METHANOL, 3 (6.1), 3022, EC 319" "268/UN 1005 AMMONIA, ANHYDROUS, 2.3 (8), 2TC, EC 208" In the upper part of the consignment note the consignor affixes the red stamps provided for the goods. Escort of dangerous goods Goods marked in the Alphabetical List with special conditions are carried escorted by conductors (specialists) of the consignor/consignee; without escort the wagons are not accepted for carriage. Conductors must know the service instructions, the dangerous properties of the goods, first-aid measures and safety in emergency situations. The consignor supplies the conductors with personal protective equipment, a first-aid kit, tools, fire-fighting and decontamination means. If a wagon is defective en route, it is detached and kept under the guard of a conductor until the defect is rectified or a representative of the consignor arrives. Carriage in covered wagons and containers (Chapter 2) Packaging, packing and marking Dangerous goods are presented for carriage in packaging provided for by standards or technical specifications, as well as the requirements of the UN Model Regulations or GOST 26319-2020 "Dangerous goods. Packing". The packaging must be strong, sound, exclude leakage and spillage, and be inert to the contents. Goods emitting dangerous gases/vapours, explosive on drying or reacting with air/moisture, and oxidising goods are packed hermetically . Glass packaging is packed in strong boxes (wooden, polymeric, metal) with non-combustible cushioning materials; the walls of the boxes must be at least 50 mm higher than the sealed bottles. Each package bears marking characterising the type and degree of danger: hazard labels , the name of the goods, the classification code and the UN number. Hazard labels are divided into the main one (by class/division) and a subsidiary one (for the type of subsidiary danger). Requirements for wagons and containers For the carriage of dangerous goods, universal or specialised covered wagons and containers are used (depending on the designation in the Alphabetical List: KV/UK, SKV/SKVa, SK/SKa); for liquid goods — tank wagons (VTs) or tank containers (KTs). Wagons and containers must bear hazard labels and the UN number; an orange plate with the hazard code and the UN number, a white plate with the emergency card number. Only sound and cleaned wagons/containers are presented for loading; fitness in commercial terms is determined by the consignor, the technical inspection by wagon-depot workers. After unloading, the consignee must clean the wagon/container of residues, wash and decontaminate it if necessary, and remove the hazard labels and plates. Acceptance and delivery Dangerous goods are presented for carriage at non-public places; acceptance/delivery at public places is by the direct "road vehicle–wagon" method under the control of station workers. If the packaging, marking or mass does not conform (a small consignment), the goods are not accepted for carriage and a general-form report is drawn up. Arrived dangerous goods must be collected by the consignee within 24 hours ; the consignee is not entitled to refuse acceptance. Joint carriage It is forbidden to load into one wagon/container dangerous goods with different classification codes not permitted for joint carriage (Annex 4). Joint carriage of dangerous goods with non-dangerous ones — under Annex 5. The return of empty cleaned packaging — on general grounds as non-dangerous goods with a note in the consignment note; empty uncleaned packaging from specialised goods is carried under the conditions of the goods carried in it. SMGS requirements for the carriage of dangerous goods In international rail traffic, Annex 2 to the SMGS — "Rules for the Carriage of Dangerous Goods" applies. Key requirements: Dangerous goods are carried only in compliance with Annex 2 to the SMGS — as to classification, packaging, marking and documents, harmonised with the UN Model Regulations (RID/ADR). Goods listed in the SMGS List of Dangerous Goods, with the UN number and proper shipping name, are admitted to carriage. Each package and vehicle must bear hazard labels and orange plates with the hazard identification number and the UN number. Transport documents are completed under the SMGS requirements; an emergency card for the dangerous goods is attached to the consignment note. The conditions of joint loading, stowage and securing, as well as the requirements for rolling stock (wagons and containers), are observed. Emergency response (TsM-407) The Safety Rules and Procedure for the Elimination of Emergency Situations with Dangerous Goods during their Carriage by Rail (approved by the Russian Emergencies Ministry on 31.10.1996 No. 9/733/3-2 and the Russian Ministry of Railways on 25.11.1996 No. TsM-407 ) establish a uniform procedure for action in the event of accidents with dangerous goods on rail transport. Purpose: to regulate the actions of railways, consignors/consignees, emergency rescue services and authorities in the elimination of emergency situations (leakage, spillage, fire, damage to packaging) with dangerous goods, including radioactive materials. Structure: general provisions; the procedure for eliminating emergency situations; the procedure for emergency restoration work; the organisation of fire-fighting; medical and preventive measures; localisation of contamination, neutralisation and decontamination; elimination of accidents with radioactive substances. Emergency card (EC) A key element of the emergency response system is the emergency card (EC). This is a reference document attached to the transport documents for dangerous goods and used first of all in an accident for the rapid identification of the danger and correct action. Each dangerous good has its own card with a unique number (e.g., EC 208 for ammonia, EC 319 for methanol). What the card consists of The emergency card consists of four main sections: Main properties and types of danger — the physical and chemical properties of the goods (state, odour, solubility, toxicity, fire and explosion hazard, danger to humans). Personal protective equipment — which gas mask, protective suit, gloves, footwear and other PPE to use when working with the goods. Necessary actions — general actions (evacuation, cordoning off, calling services), actions on leakage/spillage/scatter and actions on fire. First-aid measures — what to do with the injured person (take to fresh air, rinse eyes, artificial respiration, etc.). What information it contains Card number (EC No.) — a unique identifier linked to the goods; Name of the goods and UN number ; Hazard class and the hazard label number; a description of the dangerous properties, PPE, the order of action and first aid. How to read the card In an accident, the responder finds the relevant EC by the name of the goods (or the UN number in the documents) and reads the sections in sequence: first identifies the danger (section 1), then selects protection (section 2), then performs the actions (section 3) and gives first aid (section 4). The order of the sections is deliberately built "from hazard assessment to action" so that in a stressful situation decisions are made sequentially and without errors. Example of an emergency card: EC No. 208 for anhydrous ammonia (UN 1005). Note that the emergency cards were later updated by a separate document ("Emergency cards for dangerous goods carried by the CIS railways…"), to which the PPOG also refers. Important: the rules for the carriage of dangerous goods on the CIS railways and Annex 2 to the SMGS are interrelated. For international carriage, Annex 2 to the SMGS applies; in domestic traffic, national rules built on the same principles of the UN Model Regulations apply.'},
  {t:'Road carriage of dangerous goods (ADR) — International Logistics', u:'dangerous-goods-road.html', text:'Home → Dangerous goods → Road transport Road carriage of dangerous goods (ADR) International road carriage of dangerous goods is governed by the European Agreement concerning the International Carriage of Dangerous Goods by Road (ADR) of 30.09.1957. Russia is a party to the ADR; the rules are also mandatory for domestic carriage (in accordance with Russian legislation). Structure of the ADR The ADR consists of two annexes: Annex A — requirements for the goods: classification, the dangerous goods list, packaging, tanks, marking, documentation; Annex B — requirements for carriage: vehicles, crew, equipment, movement, loading/unloading. Classification (9 classes) The classification of dangerous goods in the ADR coincides with the UN Model Regulations: 9 hazard classes (explosives, gases, flammable liquids, flammable solids, oxidising, toxic, radioactive, corrosive, miscellaneous). Requirements for participants Driver — a special ADR certificate (after training and examination). Vehicle — approval for the carriage of certain classes (vehicle approval certificate). Special permit — for high-consequence dangerous goods. Equipment — fire extinguishers, wheel chocks, emergency equipment, PPE. Documentation During carriage there must be: a transport document stating the information on the dangerous goods (UN number, proper shipping name, class), instructions in writing for the driver, and, where necessary, a special permit and other documents. Marking and hazard labels Packages and the vehicle are marked with hazard labels and, for the vehicle, with orange plates (the hazard identification number and the UN number). Practical tip: check that the driver holds a valid ADR certificate and that the vehicle is approved for carriage of the specific class. For high-consequence dangerous goods the special permit must be obtained in advance — without it carriage is impossible.'},
  {t:'Sea carriage of dangerous goods — International Logistics', u:'dangerous-goods-sea.html', text:'Home → Dangerous goods → Sea transport Sea carriage of dangerous goods Sea carriage of dangerous goods is governed by the International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code (IMDG Code) , adopted within the framework of the SOLAS Convention, as well as Chapter VII of the SOLAS-74 Convention and MARPOL (as regards marine pollution). The IMDG Code The IMDG Code is a set of rules for the safe sea carriage of dangerous goods in packaged form. It is mandatory for SOLAS contracting states and applied by carriers worldwide. It defines: the classification of dangerous goods (9 classes, harmonised with the UN Model Regulations); requirements for packing and packaging (packing groups I, II, III by degree of danger); marking and hazard labels; stowage and segregation rules (separation of incompatible goods on board); documentation (dangerous goods declaration, transport document). Packing groups By degree of danger, goods are divided into three packing groups : Group Degree of danger I high danger (X) II medium danger (Y) III low danger (Z) Dangerous goods declaration The consignor must provide the carrier with a dangerous goods declaration (DGD) , which contains: the proper shipping name of the goods and the UN number; the class (division) of danger; the packing group; the quantity and type of packaging; a confirmation that the goods are properly classified, packed, marked and fit for carriage. Container and marking A freight container with dangerous goods must bear hazard labels, the UN number, and, for bulk goods, an orange plate. For containers in sea traffic, the VGM rules (verified gross mass) and sealing also apply. Practical tip: for a sea shipment of dangerous goods, agree in advance with the line on the possibility of carriage (not all lines accept all classes) and prepare the DGD before the container is placed. An error in the DGD is a common cause of refusal to load and container downtime.'},
  {t:'Carriage of dangerous goods — International Logistics', u:'dangerous-goods.html', text:'Home → Dangerous goods Carriage of dangerous goods Dangerous goods are carried by all modes of transport under special rules. Below is general information (classification, admission to carriage, obligations of the parties) and materials for each mode of transport. What counts as dangerous goods Dangerous goods are substances, materials, articles and production waste which, owing to their inherent properties and characteristics, may during carriage endanger human life and health, harm the environment, or cause damage to or destruction of material assets and vehicles. Classification of dangerous goods Dangerous goods are divided into 9 classes according to their physical and chemical properties and the nature of the hazard: Class Hazard category Examples of goods Class 1 Explosive substances and articles industrial explosives, pyrotechnics, ammunition Class 2 Gases (compressed, liquefied, dissolved) propane, oxygen, chlorine, ammonia Class 3 Flammable liquids petrol, solvents, alcohols Class 4.1–4.3 Flammable solids; substances liable to spontaneous combustion; substances which, in contact with water, emit flammable gases sulphur, coal, calcium carbide Class 5.1–5.2 Oxidising substances and organic peroxides nitrates, hydrogen peroxide Class 6.1–6.2 Toxic and infectious substances pesticides, cyanides Class 7 Radioactive material radioisotopes, nuclear fuel Class 8 Corrosive substances acids, alkalis Class 9 Miscellaneous dangerous substances and articles lithium batteries, asbestos, environmentally hazardous substances Admission to carriage Only dangerous goods listed in the relevant list (the Dangerous Goods List, the ADR/IMDG list, etc.) and assigned a UN number may be accepted for carriage. Each dangerous good must have a proper shipping name and UN number stated in the transport documents. Carriage is permitted only in sound packaging appropriate to the hazard class, with the required marking and hazard labels. Packing different dangerous goods together in one package is permitted only if they are compatible. Obligations of the consignor Correctly classify the goods (determine the class, UN number, proper shipping name). Provide proper packaging, packing, marking and hazard labels. Complete the transport documents and attach the necessary accompanying documents (emergency instructions, dangerous goods declaration, etc.). Confirm that the goods are ready for carriage and comply with the rules. Obligations of the carrier Accept the goods for carriage only if they comply with the rules. Ensure carriage conditions that exclude danger (securing, compatibility, escort). Comply with the requirements for the vehicle and for operations involving dangerous goods. MSDS — Material Safety Data Sheet The MSDS (Material Safety Data Sheet), in Russia — the Safety Data Sheet for chemical products , is the principal document containing full information on the properties and hazards of a chemical substance (product) and the measures for its safe handling. Purpose The safety data sheet ensures safe handling of chemical products at every stage of the life cycle: production, storage, transport, use and disposal. It is needed for correct classification of dangerous goods, selection of packaging and marking, staff training and emergency response. Content (16 sections under GHS) Under the international standard (GHS; in Russia — GOST 30333) the safety data sheet consists of 16 sections : Identification of the substance/mixture and of the supplier — name, use, supplier contacts. Hazards identification — hazard classification, signal word, pictograms, H-statements. Composition / information on ingredients — chemical composition, CAS numbers, concentrations. First-aid measures — actions in case of inhalation, skin/eye contact, ingestion. Fire-fighting measures — suitable and unsuitable extinguishing media. Accidental release measures — containment, clean-up, personnel protection. Handling and storage — safe working and storage conditions. Exposure controls / personal protection — exposure limits, personal protective equipment. Physical and chemical properties — state, temperatures, solubility, etc. Stability and reactivity — conditions to avoid. Toxicological information — routes of exposure, symptoms, acute/chronic toxicity. Ecological information — environmental impact. Disposal considerations — methods of neutralising and disposing of waste. Transport information — UN number, class, packing group, transport requirements. Regulatory information — references to applicable regulations and standards. Other information — date of issue/revision, changes. How to use it The safety data sheet is read "from identification to action": first determine the hazard (sections 1–3), then protection and response (4–8), then properties and effects (9–12), and finally transport and regulatory aspects (13–16). For carriage, the key sections are 2 (classification), 9 (properties) and 14 (transport information): it is on their basis that the consignor classifies the goods, selects packaging and completes the transport documents. Where it is used Classification of dangerous goods — assignment to a class and UN number. Arranging carriage — MSDS data is transferred into the transport documents and the dangerous goods declaration. Selection of packaging, marking and hazard labels. Staff training and emergency preparedness. Customs clearance and compliance with technical regulations (TR CU 019/2011, REACH, etc.). Practical tip: when shipping dangerous goods, the key is accurate classification (class + UN number). Packaging, marking, compatibility and carriage conditions — and ultimately safety and the absence of claims — all depend on it. A classification error is the most common and most costly mistake when handling dangerous goods. Road transport ADR — international carriage of dangerous goods by road: classes, packaging, marking, documents. Rail transport RID (SMGS Annex 2), emergency cards and emergency response (TsM-407). Sea transport IMDG Code: classification, packing, marking, dangerous goods declaration. Air transport ICAO Technical Instructions and IATA DGR: restrictions, packing, shipper\'s declaration.'},
  {t:'Legal information — International Logistics', u:'disclaimer.html', text:'Home → Legal information Disclaimer (legal information) All materials of the site "International Logistics and Foreign Trade" (texts, tables, diagrams, terms, examples and other elements) are the result of the intellectual work of the author, based on the Literature referenced at the bottom of the site, and are protected by copyright legislation. Use of the materials Any copying, reproduction, reprinting, quoting, distribution or other use of the site\'s materials — in whole or in part, in any form and by any means, including publication on other sites, in print publications, presentations and social networks — is permitted only with the mandatory indication of the source : an active hyperlink to the site and the name of the section/page from which the material was taken. When the materials are used on other resources, the link must be placed directly next to the material used, be visible and available for indexing by search engines. Reference nature of the information The site\'s materials are of an informational and educational nature and are not legal, customs, financial or other professional advice. Information may become outdated; its relevance should be checked against primary sources (texts of conventions, ICC rules, regulatory acts). The author bears no responsibility for any consequences of the application of the published information, nor for the content of third-party sites to which the links may lead. Data processing The site uses the Yandex.Metrika web-analytics service, which collects anonymised visit statistics (including technical data and user actions on the pages) to improve the site\'s operation. The data is processed on the terms of the privacy policy of Yandex LLC. Messages sent through the contact form are used exclusively to reply to the enquiry and are not transferred to third parties. On questions of the use of the materials , contact me through the "Contact" section.'},
  {t:'Air Waybill (AWB) — air freight — International Logistics', u:'documents-air.html', text:'Home → Consignment documents → Air transport Air Waybill (AWB) The air waybill (AWB) is a document issued for the carriage of goods by air. Unlike the bill of lading, the AWB is not a document of title : it is not transferred by endorsement, and the goods are released to the consignee named in it without presentation of the original. Functions of the AWB Evidence of the conclusion of the contract of carriage by air. A receipt for the acceptance of the goods by the carrier. A document for settlement, customs clearance and insurance. The basis for invoicing the carriage (rate, weight, volume are stated). Types of AWB Type Description MAWB (Master Air Waybill) The airline\'s cargo waybill. Issued by the carrier (airline) for a consolidated consignment. Links the forwarder-shipper with the airline. HAWB (House Air Waybill) The forwarder\'s waybill. Issued by the forwarder for a specific consignor. Links the consignor with the forwarder. In consolidated carriage there is usually one MAWB (for the whole consignment) and several HAWBs (for each shipper within the consignment). Particulars of the AWB Issued in several copies (usually 3 originals — for the carrier, the consignee and the consignor). The AWB number consists of 11 digits : the first three are the airline prefix. It is valid for the whole route, including transshipment between flights. The electronic e-AWB (IATA standard) is widely used. For dangerous goods, the shipper\'s declaration (Shipper\'s Declaration for Dangerous Goods, DGD) is completed. Use The AWB is used for all commercial air carriage. Unlike the sea B/L, goods under an AWB are released to the consignee immediately upon arrival — so air carriage requires fast clearance and is not used as an instrument for "holding" the goods in settlements. Practical tip: for air carriage the rate is calculated by chargeable weight — the greater of the actual and the volumetric weight. Volumetric weight = volume (cm³) ÷ 6000. Take this into account when calculating cost: light but bulky cargo can "weigh" more. The international conventions governing air carriage (Warsaw, Montreal) are in the "Regulatory framework" section.'},
  {t:'Rail consignment documents (SMGS, GU) — International Logistics', u:'documents-rail.html', text:'Home → Consignment documents → Rail transport Rail consignment documents Two main documents are used for rail carriage: the SMGS — for international carriage between the participating countries, and the GU consignment note — for domestic, export and import carriage within Russia. SMGS — international rail freight traffic The SMGS (Agreement on International Goods Transport by Rail) has been in force since 1951 and applies to direct international traffic between the stations of the participating countries (CIS, the Baltics, China, Mongolia, North Korea, Vietnam, Iran and others). The SMGS consignment note consists of five sheets : Sheet Purpose 1. Original consignment note Travels with the goods to the station of destination and is issued to the consignee. 2. Road statement Accompanies the goods and remains with the railway of destination. 3. Goods delivery sheet Accompanies the goods and remains with the railway of destination. 4. Arrival notification sheet For notifying the consignee, remains with the railway of destination. 5. Duplicate consignment note Issued to the consignor — confirmation of the contract of carriage. Key features of the SMGS: a single document for the whole route, recognition by customs, coding of goods under ETSNG/GNG , joint and several liability of the railways. GU — the consignment note for domestic and export/import carriage in Russia For carriage within Russia, and also in export and import traffic (within the territory of the Russian Federation), the rail consignment note is used. Its uniform forms are approved by Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 191 of 19.06.2019 "On the Approval of Uniform Forms of Transport Documents for the Carriage of Goods by Rail" (registered with the Ministry of Justice on 06.09.2019 No. 55834; entered into force 365 days after publication). This order replaced the previously applicable Rules for the Completion of Transport Documents (Order of the Russian Ministry of Railways No. 39 of 18.06.2003, which lapsed). Uniform form of the consignment note (four sheets) The transport document — the rail consignment note of form GU-27u (GU-27u VTs) — consists of four sheets : Sheet Document Issued to / retained by Sheet 1 Consignment note (original) issued by the carrier to the consignee Sheet 2 Road statement for the carrier and participants in the carriage process (at least two additional copies per participating infrastructure) Sheet 3 Counterfoil of the road statement retained by the carrier Sheet 4 Receipt for acceptance of the goods retained by the consignor Uniform form GU-27u (GU-27u VTs) Instead of the previously used different blanks (GU-27, GU-29-O, GU-27u-VTs, GU-29u-VTs, etc.), Order No. 191 established a single form of the consignment note GU-27u (the machine-printed variant — GU-27u VTs ) with identically arranged columns on all four sheets. All sheets are completed uniformly (by hand or by computer); the sheets differ in purpose (original, road statement, counterfoil, receipt), not in the content of the columns. The GU consignment note is completed in the ETRAN system (Russian Railways) — electronic preparation of transport documents with an electronic signature; this is the de facto standard for domestic rail carriage in Russia. Completion of the consignment note by the consignor (main columns) The consignor completes the following columns of the uniform consignment note form: Column of the note What is stated Speed the speed category (freight or fast) Wagon type in accordance with the request agreed with the carrier Wagon number, load capacity, number of axles, tank type/volume data marked on the wagon; in addition to the number, after a dash — the code of the railway administration-owner (under the SMGS) Oversize index the five-digit index of the degree of oversize (if absent — not completed) Carrier name and OKPO code Station of departure / destination exact name and code under the tariff guide; for import/export — the marks "Import"/"Export" Consignor / Consignee full name, OKPO code (the carrier\'s code in brackets); only one legal/natural person Payer name and code of the payer for the carriage Transfer stations names and codes of the stations for the transfer of loaded/empty wagons between infrastructures Loading into the wagon by cross out the inapplicable ("Carrier" or "Consignor") Number of packages for packaged/general cargo — the number of packages; for pallets — as a fraction (pallets/packages in a pallet); for bulk/loose/liquid — the words "In bulk"/"Loose"/"In liquid" Packaging abbreviated name ("box", "crt", "bbl") or "N/P" for unpackaged Description of goods the name and code under the tariff guide; for dangerous goods — the emergency card number; notes on wagons, escort, guarding Mass of goods in kg, determined cross out the inapplicable ("Carrier", "Jointly", "Consignor") Total net mass / Wagon tare / Gross mass the respective masses Total packages the total number of packages in figures and words Total mass in words, the total mass of the consignment (if the mass was determined by the consignor) Method of determining the mass the method of weighing and the permissible error (e.g., "on wagon scales ±1.5%") Information on seals (ZPU) by whom sealed, the type of seal and the control marks Declared value in words, the value of the goods in rubles I am responsible for the correctness of the information entered legible signature, position, full name of the consignor Under request No. the number of the request agreed with the carrier On the reverse of the consignment note, the consignor completes column 1 (stowage and securing of the goods under the Technical Conditions) and column 2 "Special declarations and remarks of the consignor" (agreement of open carriage, pallets, protection against freezing, attached documents, etc.). Which document when Situation Document Carriage between stations of SMGS member states SMGS Domestic carriage within Russia GU (via ETRAN) Export/import: the Russian leg GU , then re-issuance to the SMGS at the border Carriage to CIM countries (Western Europe) Combined CIM/SMGS consignment note Practical tip: carefully check the cargo code (ETSNG/GNG) and the mass in the consignment note — the freight charge and customs clearance depend on them. Errors in the description or number of packages are a common cause of delays and claims.'},
  {t:'Road transport documents: CMR, TN, ETrN — International Logistics', u:'documents-road.html', text:'Home → Consignment documents → Road transport Road transport documents For road carriage the following are used: the CMR — for international carriage, the TN (consignment note) — for domestic carriage, and the ETrN — the electronic consignment note (mandatory in Russia from 2026). CMR — the international consignment note The CMR is the consignment note used for international road carriage between the countries party to the CMR Convention (1956). It is completed for goods in international road carriage. Confirms the conclusion of the contract of international road carriage. Is not a document of title (unlike the B/L). Is usually issued in 3–4 copies (for the consignor, consignee, carrier, for customs/settlement). Contains: the consignor, consignee, carrier, goods, weight, number of packages, loading/unloading points, conditions. TN — the consignment note (domestic carriage in Russia) The TN (consignment note) is the principal document for the carriage of goods by road within the Russian Federation. Its form is approved by the Rules for the Carriage of Goods by Road (a Russian Government resolution). Issued for each domestic road carriage. Contains information on the consignor, consignee, carrier, goods and route. Confirms the fact of concluding the contract of carriage and acceptance of the goods. Serves as the basis for settlement and for writing off carriage expenses. ETrN — the electronic consignment note The ETrN is a consignment note generated in the form of an electronic document. From 1 September 2026 it is mandatory for all participants in commercial road freight carriage in Russia: the paper note is prohibited, except for the exceptions established by law. Key concepts (art. 2 of the Charter) Electronic transport documents (EPD) — the electronic consignment note (ETrN), the electronic waybill, the electronic work order, the electronic accompanying statement, the electronic chartering contract, the electronic order, the electronic request, electronic freight-forwarding documents. GIS EPD — the state information system for electronic transport documents (a federal GIS). It receives EPD from operators, processes and stores them, and provides them to state authorities (the Federal Tax Service, the traffic police, Rostransnadzor, etc.). IS EPD — an information system for electronic transport documents ensuring the exchange of EPD between participants. IS EPD operator — a legal entity included in the register of operators of information systems for electronic transport documents. Participants in information exchange — the consignor, the carrier, the consignee, the charterer, the charterer\'s counterparty and other persons who have concluded an electronic document exchange agreement with an operator. How the electronic document flow works The ETrN scheme looks like this: Participants (consignor, carrier, consignee) connect to any IS EPD operator and conclude with it an electronic document exchange agreement (a public contract). Each party generates its own title (section) of the note in its IS EPD and signs it with an electronic signature. The IS EPD operator ensures the exchange of documents between participants (including through roaming between different operators). The operator submits the generated ETrN and the information from it to the GIS EPD . From the GIS EPD, the data is available to state authorities (the Federal Tax Service — for VAT and expense control; the traffic police/Rostransnadzor — during roadside checks). Participants may use different operators : the document passes through each one\'s system and is joined at the GIS EPD level. An IS EPD operator cannot refuse to exchange documents merely because the counterparty uses another operator. Who the operator is The GIS EPD operator — the Federal Tax Service (FNS of Russia): administers the state system and maintains the register of IS EPD operators. IS EPD operators — commercial electronic document exchange (EDO) operators included in the FNS register. The largest: "Tensor" (SBIS), "Kontur" (Diadoc), "Takskom", "Kaluga Astral", "L-Trans"/"Logistika" and others. Participants choose an operator freely; for the ETrN one can use both the operators\' specialised transport solutions and integration through one\'s own accounting system (ERP/TMS). Electronic signature and titles Signature: an enhanced qualified electronic signature (UKEP) — for legal entities and individual entrepreneurs; for natural persons an enhanced non-qualified signature (UNEP) via "Gosuslugi" is possible. MChD (machine-readable power of attorney) — for signing by employees on behalf of an organisation. ETrN titles: the consignor completes and signs its title, the carrier its own (including notes on acceptance and delivery of the goods), the consignee its own (confirmation of receipt). The document is "assembled" from the titles as the carriage progresses. Obligation From 01.09.2026 the consignment note is generated as an ETrN (art. 8 of the Road Transport Charter as amended by 140-FZ). No transition period is provided; the period until 1 March 2027 is not a deferral. The paper note is permissible only in the cases established by Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 262 of 01.06.2026 . When the note is issued on paper (Order of the Ministry of Transport No. 262 of 01.06.2026) The paper form is permissible if two conditions are met simultaneously (part 1 of art. 8 of the Road Transport Charter): the situation falls under one of the cases listed below; the contract of carriage does not provide for an ETrN, and special rules do not establish the obligation to draw one up. Cases of issuing on paper: military goods are carried by road, where the consignor or consignee is the Russian Ministry of Defence, the Ministry of Internal Affairs, the Russian National Guard, the FSB, the Emergencies Ministry, the Foreign Intelligence Service or the Federal Guard Service; the consignor or consignee is a foreign legal entity whose information is not included in the state register of accredited branches and representative offices (art. 21 of Federal Law No. 160-FZ of 09.07.1999); cabotage carriage of goods is performed by foreign carriers registered in an EAEU member state; there is no internet access — the fact of the lack of connection must be recorded by an act (date, time, place, persons present with their positions, how the lack of connection manifested itself). When the connection is restored — return to EPD: the IS EPD operator will transfer the accumulated EPD to the GIS EPD; the consignor and the consignee are the same person ; the consignor or consignee is a natural person and the carriage is not related to their business activity; precious metals and precious stones are carried; there is no technical possibility to send electronic documents to the GIS EPD due to a failure recorded by the state system for detecting, preventing and eliminating the consequences of computer attacks ; carriage in a territory where high-alert, emergency, martial law or counter-terrorist operation regimes are in force; special cargo is carried; periodicals are carried; military and dual-use products are carried within military-technical cooperation and export control. Roadside checks: in the absence of internet or a failure, the driver presents the paper transport document; in an abnormal situation in the GIS EPD — the EPD through software integrated with the IS EPD, or a paper copy of the EPD with an electronic signature stamp. Liability For the absence of a consignment note (including an electronic one), when issuance is mandatory — a fine on the driver of RUB 500 (cl. 2 of art. 12.3 of the Russian Code of Administrative Offences). There are currently no separate fines for non-compliance specifically with the electronic form. The fact of issuing the note on paper does not in itself entail a risk of the tax authority refusing to recognise profit tax expenses and VAT deductions, if the other conditions are met (Letter of the Federal Tax Service of Russia No. SD-36-3/9573@ of 03.11.2026). The format of the electronic consignment note is approved by Order of the Federal Tax Service of Russia No. ED-7-26/1065@ of 09.12.2021. For the information support of the management of the transport and logistics complex, the "GosLog" platform has been created. Which document when Situation Document International road carriage (CMR countries) CMR Domestic carriage within Russia (until 09.2026 / exceptions) TN (paper) Domestic carriage within Russia (from 09.2026) ETrN (electronic)'},
  {t:'Bill of Lading (B/L) — sea freight — International Logistics', u:'documents-sea.html', text:'Home → Consignment documents → Sea transport Bill of Lading (B/L) The bill of lading is the principal document of carriage by sea. It performs three functions : Receipt — confirms that the carrier has received the goods in the stated quantity and condition. Evidence of the contract of carriage — records the terms on which the goods were accepted for carriage. Document of title — gives the right to dispose of the goods (it is transferred by endorsement). Classification of bills of lading Classification Type Description By issue Direct B/L Covers direct carriage between ports without transshipment. Transshipment B/L Provides for transshipment of the cargo to another vessel en route. By content Clean B/L Without remarks on the condition of the goods and packaging. Required by a bank under a letter of credit. Claused / Foul B/L With reservations about packaging defects or visible damage. Conditional B/L Rarely used, contains a reservation on the conditions of issue. By consignee Order B/L Issued "to the order" of the shipper or bank; transferred by endorsement. Straight B/L Issued to a named consignee, not transferable. Bearer B/L Transferred by mere delivery; rare, only by agreement of the parties. By form Ocean B/L Covers carriage between ports. Inland B/L Covers the inland (land) leg of delivery. Through B/L Covers carriage with transshipment to another mode of transport. Combined Transport B/L A single document for multimodal carriage under the responsibility of one operator. e-B/L A bill of lading in electronic form with a digital signature. Telex Release Waiver of the paper original: the goods are released on electronic confirmation. Characteristics of the main types Clean B/L Parameter Characteristic Definition A bill of lading without remarks on the condition of the goods and their packaging. When issued The goods are received in good condition, the packaging is intact. Issued by The master or the port agent of the vessel. Financial consequences The bank is ready to finance under a letter of credit. Applicability All types of cargo, the standard procedure. Example "Received in good order and condition". Practical use: for documentary credits (L/C), for insurance purposes, as evidence of receipt of the goods. Claused B/L Parameter Characteristic Definition A bill of lading with notes on packaging defects and visible damage. When issued Packaging defects are found, seals are broken, damage is visible. Examples of reservations "Packaging in poor condition", "2 bales damaged", "Container dented". Financial consequences The bank may refuse to finance under the L/C. Applicability When visible defects are proven. Risk for the recipient The consignee cannot conceal claims later. Practical use: protecting the interests of the vessel and the sea operator, documenting visible damage, grounds for insurance claims. Order B/L Parameter Characteristic Definition A bill of lading transferable by endorsement to a third party. Wording "To the order of [Shipper name]". Transfer By the shipper\'s signature on the reverse of the document. Use For documentary credits and deferred payment. Financial instruments Often used when discounting a bill of lading at a bank. Applicability Export of goods with conditional payment. Practical use: financing export operations, collateral, transfer of title to the goods. Straight B/L Parameter Characteristic Definition A bill of lading issued to a named consignee of the goods. Wording "Consigned to [Consignee name]". Transfer Not transferable — only the named consignee can receive the goods. Risk of loss Minimal: only one person can receive the goods. Financial instruments Not used under an L/C, as it is non-transferable. Applicability Deliveries to "established" buyers, domestic carriage. Practical use: with a fixed consignee, domestic and adjacent carriage, maximum security for the consignee. Electronic B/L (e-B/L) Parameter Characteristic Definition A bill of lading in electronic form with a digital signature. Platforms TradeLens (Maersk), essDOCS, Bolero. Advantages Faster document flow, lower costs. Legal force Recognised in most countries as equivalent to a paper one. Requirements Both parties must agree to an e-B/L. Applicability Modern logistics operations, containers. Practical use: faster cargo clearance, reduced paper document flow, integration with supply chain management systems. Through B/L Parameter Characteristic Definition A bill of lading covering multimodal delivery. Route From the initial point of dispatch to the final point of delivery. Liability The carrier is liable for the whole route or assigns liability. Transshipment May include transshipment between vessels. Applicability Multimodal carriage (sea–rail, sea–road). Practical use: container carriage from inland points, door-to-door schemes, simplified settlement between carriers. Comparison of bills of lading Criterion Clean Claused Order Straight Through Financing under L/C ✅ ❌ ✅ ❌ ✅ Transferability ✅ ✅ ✅ ❌ ✅ Visible defects ❌ ✅ ✅ ✅ ✅ Speed of turnover Slow Slow Fast Fast Fast Risk of loss Medium Medium Medium Low Medium Use Frequent With defects With financing With trust With multimodal Practical tip: when working under a documentary credit, the bank requires a clean order bill of lading in a full set. A discrepancy between the bill of lading and the terms of the credit is the most common reason for a bank refusing payment. B/L originals are documents of title: send them only via reliable channels (courier, bank). The rules governing the carrier\'s liability under a bill of lading (Hague Rules, Hague-Visby Rules, etc.) are in the "Regulatory framework" section.'},
  {t:'Glossary — International Logistics', u:'glossary.html', text:'Home → Glossary Glossary of terms Key terms of international logistics, sea transport and chartering. Freight-correspondence abbreviations are collected separately — in the section "Chartering abbreviations" . Term Definition Adjustment (ADJ) A correction of the freight rate depending on changing parameters (parcel size, season, conditions). Used in long-term contracts. Chartering The transfer of a vessel by the shipowner into the temporary possession or use of the charterer. See also "Bareboat charter", "Time charter", "Voyage charter". Delivery basis (Incoterms) Delivery terms that define the point at which risk and costs pass from seller to buyer. See the "Incoterms 2020" section for details. Barter An exchange of goods without monetary settlement. Rarely used in sea transport. Bareboat / Demise Charter A charter of the vessel without crew: the charterer takes the vessel into full possession and control, mans it itself and bears all operating costs. Broker (ship / chartering) An intermediary between shipowner and charterer in concluding a chartering deal. Can be exclusive (under a framework agreement) or market (on a per-order basis). Additional Charges Costs of loading, discharging and handling cargo on top of the base freight. Guarantor A person liable for performance of obligations if the principal debtor is insufficient. General Agency (GA) A representation of a shipping company in ports, servicing its vessels. General Average Losses incurred through deliberate and reasonable expenditure or sacrifice for the common safety of the vessel and cargo; apportioned between all participants in the voyage in proportion to value. Deadweight (DWT) The full carrying capacity of a vessel — the total weight of cargo, fuel, stores and crew that the vessel can take at its load-line draught. Deviation A departure of the vessel from the contractual route. Unjustified deviation may deprive the carrier of the right to limit liability. Demurrage A charge for the vessel\'s delay beyond the agreed laytime during loading or discharging. Dispatch / Despatch A premium paid to the charterer for completing cargo operations ahead of the laytime. Usually 50% of the demurrage rate. Additional Freight A surcharge on freight for special conditions (loading at an extra port, etc.). Principal / Customer A legal entity ordering carriage — the charterer or shipper. Endorsement A transfer inscription on the back of the bill of lading by which an order bill of lading is transferred to another party. Cabotage Carriage of goods and passengers between ports of one state. As a rule, permitted only to vessels under the national flag. Cancelling date The latest date by which the vessel must be tendered for loading. If the vessel has not arrived by that date, the charterer may cancel the contract. Commercial Agent (CA) A representative of the shipowner on commercial matters, negotiating freight. Confident A trusted person; an intermediate intermediary in informal negotiations. Bill of Lading (B/L) The main document of sea carriage: a receipt for the goods, evidence of the contract of carriage and a document of title to the goods. Laytime / Lay days The agreed period for loading or discharging the vessel, included in the freight. Once it expires, demurrage begins. The standard modern English term is laytime ; "lay days" is the historical form, and in modern usage "laydays" usually means the start date of the window (in the laycan pair). Liner service Regular carriage on a fixed schedule and route with published tariffs. Lump sum freight A fixed amount of freight for the whole vessel or voyage regardless of the quantity of cargo actually loaded. Dead freight A charge the charterer must pay for cargo space it reserved but did not tender. Mobilization The positioning of the vessel to the loading place under a voyage charter. Multimodal transport Carriage of goods by several modes of transport under a single document on the responsibility of one operator. Sea-air Combined carriage using sea and air transport — combining the low cost of sea and the speed of air. Night Surcharge An additional charge for cargo operations at night. Notice of Readiness (NOR) A written notice by the master that the vessel is ready to load/discharge. The laytime count usually starts from its tender. Encumbered vessel A vessel encumbered by a mortgage in connection with financing its construction or purchase. Offer / Firm offer A firm proposal to conclude a chartering deal on the stated terms, binding on the offeror for the duration of its validity. Particular Average Losses that do not qualify as general average; borne by the party that suffered them, or its insurer. Sub-charter The transfer of the vessel to a third party (sub-charter) under a voyage or time charter. Port charges Fees for the use of port facilities and services, included in the cost of carriage. Advanced Freight Payment of freight (in full or in part) before delivery of the cargo. Recap / Fixture recap A written confirmation of the main terms of a concluded chartering deal, exchanged by the parties before signing the full charter party. Recap Correction A clarification of the terms after the recap when errors are found. Voyage Charter A charter of the vessel for one or several voyages; the shipowner bears operating costs, freight is paid for carrying the cargo. Seasonal Rate A freight rate depending on the season (summer/winter period). Certificate of Registry A document certifying the vessel\'s registration and national affiliation. Special Class A vessel class for carrying heavy/special cargoes. Urgent Voyage A voyage of an urgent nature, for which a speed premium is provided. Standard Terms Unified terms of a charter party — GENCON, NYPE, BALTIME and others. Stevedore A company or person performing loading/unloading work on the vessel and in port. Shipowner / Owner The party to a charter party that provides the vessel for carriage and bears its operating costs (under a voyage charter). Surveyor An independent expert who inspects cargo and vessel, determining the quantity, condition and causes of cargo damage. Time Charter A charter of the vessel for a defined period (months, years) with crew; the charterer pays fuel and port charges, the shipowner pays crew and vessel upkeep. Time Charter Equivalent (TCE) The shipowner\'s daily revenue from operating the vessel, net of variable costs (fuel, port charges). Used to compare voyage performance. Tallyman A cargo counter who keeps a tally of packages during loading and discharging. Tonnage A vessel\'s capacity expressed in register tons; used to calculate charges. Tramp shipping Irregular carriage without a schedule at freely agreed market rates. Fixture The fact of concluding a chartering deal, when the parties have confirmed all terms (clear fixture). Freight The charge for carrying cargo by sea, set by the charter party. Ad valorem freight Freight calculated as a percentage of the value of the cargo carried. Charterer The party to a charter party that hires the vessel to carry cargo. Forwarder An organiser of cargo delivery acting as intermediary between the cargo owner and carriers, not owning transport. House broker An in-house chartering department of a large company (oil, timber, etc.) working without external intermediaries. Home Port The port of the vessel\'s registration, determining its jurisdiction. Charter Party (C/P) The contract for chartering a vessel between shipowner and charterer. Drawn up on standard forms (GENCON, NYPE, BALTIME and others). Exclusive broker A broker with a framework agreement with the principal, holding the exclusive right to find partners for its fixtures. Express Rate An increased charge for urgent delivery under tight deadlines. TEU Twenty-foot Equivalent Unit — a conventional unit equal to the volume of a standard 20-foot container. FEU Forty-foot Equivalent Unit — a conventional unit equal to the volume of a standard 40-foot container (2 TEU). GRT / NRT Gross / net register tonnage — volumetric units of a vessel (1 register ton = 100 cubic feet).'},
  {t:'Incoterms 2020 — all 11 delivery terms', u:'incoterms.html', text:'Home → Incoterms 2020 Incoterms 2020 Incoterms (International Commercial Terms) are international rules for interpreting trade terms that define which party (seller or buyer) bears the costs and risks at each stage of delivery. The current edition — Incoterms 2020 — came into force on 1 January 2020. It is important to understand: Incoterms govern the transfer of risk and allocation of costs , but they do NOT determine the transfer of ownership and do NOT replace the contract of sale. 11 terms — two classes For any mode of transport (7 terms) Term Full name Meaning EXW Ex Works The seller only makes the goods available at its own premises. The buyer bears maximum obligations. FCA Free Carrier The seller delivers the goods to the carrier nominated by the buyer at the agreed place. CPT Carriage Paid To The seller pays carriage to the destination; risk passes when the goods are handed to the first carrier. CIP Carriage and Insurance Paid To Like CPT, but the seller also insures the goods (ICC (A) level of cover). DAP Delivered at Place Delivered at destination. The seller delivers to the agreed place; the buyer unloads. DPU Delivered at Place Unloaded Delivered and unloaded. The only term where the seller must unload the goods. DDP Delivered Duty Paid Delivered with duties paid. The seller bears maximum obligations, including import clearance. For sea and inland waterway transport only (4 terms) Term Full name Meaning FAS Free Alongside Ship Free alongside ship. The seller delivers the goods alongside the vessel at the port of shipment. FOB Free On Board Free on board. Risk passes when the goods are loaded on board the vessel. CFR Cost and Freight Cost and freight. The seller pays freight to the port of destination; risk passes on loading on board. CIF Cost, Insurance and Freight Like CFR, but the seller also insures the goods (minimum cover ICC (C)). The key logic of choosing a term The closer a term is to the start of the alphabet (E, F) — the more obligations the buyer has. The closer to the end (C, D) — the more obligations the seller has. E/F-terms — the buyer arranges the main carriage. C-terms — the seller arranges and pays carriage, but risk passes early (on handing over to the carrier). D-terms — the seller bears risk and cost all the way to the destination. Practical tip: for containerised shipments, FCA is often used instead of FOB — the container is handed over to the carrier at the terminal rather than "on board the vessel". This better reflects the moment of risk transfer in modern container logistics. What else to keep in mind Under CIP (unlike CIF) insurance must be at the ICC (A) level — maximum cover. DPU is a new term in the 2020 edition (replaced DAT) and now allows delivery at any place, not only at a terminal. Incoterms are always stated together with a place : for example, "FOB Shanghai, Incoterms 2020".'},
  {t:'International Logistics and Foreign Trade — knowledge and practice', u:'index.html', text:'Incoterms 2020 All 11 delivery terms: how risks and costs are allocated between seller and buyer. 🌊 Sea freight Vessel types, bill of lading, chartering, chartering operations technique and maritime conventions. 🚂 Rail freight SMGS and consignment notes, railway conventions and rules. ✈️ Air freight Air waybill (AWB), aviation conventions and IATA rules. 🚚 Road freight CMR, consignment note, electronic consignment note, CMR and TIR conventions. Dangerous goods Classification, IATA DGR, DGD, MSDS, ADR, RID, SMGS dangerous-goods rules, emergency cards. 🛃 Customs clearance Customs procedures, TN VED / HS codes, currency control, payment calculation. UCP-600 Documentary credit rules: terms, types and key provisions. Glossary Dictionary of key terms in international logistics and foreign trade. 📈 Market indices — change SCFI — Shanghai Containerized Freight Index A composite container freight rate index published weekly (on Fridays) by the Shanghai Shipping Exchange. It reflects average spot rates for container shipments out of Shanghai on major routes (Europe, Mediterranean, the Americas and others). The key benchmark for export rates from China. Drewry WCI — World Container Index A composite container rate index by Drewry, published weekly (on Thursdays). It is calculated as the average rate (in USD) for a 40-foot container (FEU) across 8 key trade lanes. Widely used in index-linked freight contracts. BDI — Baltic Exchange Dry Index An index of dry-bulk freight rates published daily by the Baltic Exchange (London). It reflects rates for carrying dry bulk cargoes (ore, coal, grain) on major routes and consists of the Capesize, Panamax and Supramax sub-indices. A key indicator of the dry-bulk market. Updated: . Sources: sse.net.cn, drewry.co.uk, balticexchange.com.'},
  {t:'References and sources — International Logistics', u:'literature.html', text:'Home → References and sources References and sources Main literature Limonov E. L. "Foreign trade operations of sea transport and multimodal carriage". Third edition, revised and supplemented. — St. Petersburg: Modul LLC, 2006. Regulatory documents and rules International trade rules Incoterms 2020 — International Chamber of Commerce (ICC), Publication No. 723E. UCP 600 — Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits, ICC, Publication No. 600. ISBP 745 — International Standard Banking Practice for the examination of documents under documentary credits, ICC, Publication No. 745. Sea transport Hague Rules (1924) — the International Convention for the unification of certain rules relating to bills of lading. Hague-Visby Rules (1968) — the Protocol amending the Hague Rules. Hamburg Rules (1978) — the UN Convention on the Carriage of Goods by Sea. Rotterdam Rules (2008) — the UN Convention on Contracts for the International Carriage of Goods Wholly or Partly by Sea. SOLAS-74 Convention — the International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea. IMDG Code — the International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code. Merchant Shipping Code of the Russian Federation (KTM RF) — Federal Law No. 81-FZ of 30.04.1999. Rail transport SMGS — the Agreement on International Goods Transport by Rail (in force since 1951; the CIS, the Baltics, China, Mongolia, North Korea, Vietnam, Iran and others). CIM / COTIF — the CIM Uniform Rules concerning the Contract of International Carriage of Goods by Rail (the COTIF Convention). Russian Railways Charter — Federal Law No. 18-FZ of 10.01.2003. Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 191 of 19.06.2019 — Uniform forms of transport documents for the carriage of goods by rail (the GU-27u/GU-27u VTs consignment note). Registered with the Ministry of Justice on 06.09.2019 No. 55834; entered into force 365 days after publication. Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 256 of 27.07.2020 — Rules for the carriage of goods and empty freight wagons by rail (the procedure for re-consignment, drawing up reports, completing the consignment note, storage periods for goods). Registered with the Ministry of Justice on 16.10.2020 No. 60411. Instruction DCh-1835 — on the carriage of oversized and heavy cargo on the railways of the CIS member states and the Republics of Latvia, Lithuania and Estonia. Approved by the protocol of the XXX session of the Council for Railway Transport on 19.10.2001; amendments — the XLIV session of 13.10.2006 (as amended, in force since 01.01.2007). Rules for the Carriage of Dangerous Goods by Rail (PPOG) — approved by the Council for Railway Transport of the CIS, protocol No. 15 of 05.04.1996, as amended (rev. of 06.11.2024). Safety Rules and Procedure for the Elimination of Emergency Situations with Dangerous Goods (TsM-407) — approved by the Russian Emergencies Ministry on 31.10.1996 No. 9/733/3-2 and the Russian Ministry of Railways on 25.11.1996 No. TsM-407. Air transport Warsaw Convention (1929) — for the unification of certain rules relating to international carriage by air. Montreal Convention (1999) — the modern convention that replaced the Warsaw system for most states. Chicago Convention (1944) — on international civil aviation (ICAO). IATA rules — the standards of the International Air Transport Association (AWB forms, e-freight, cargo handling). Road transport CMR Convention (1956) — on the contract for the international carriage of goods by road. TIR Convention (1975) — the Customs Convention on the International Transport of Goods under Cover of TIR Carnets. Road Transport and Urban Ground Electric Transport Charter — Federal Law No. 259-FZ of 08.11.2007. Federal Law No. 140-FZ of 07.06.2025 (as amended 04.08.2026) — the transition to electronic transport documents and freight-forwarding documents. Rules for the carriage of goods by road — Russian Government Resolution No. 2200 of 21.12.2020. Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 115 of 03.04.2026 (as amended 31.08.2026) — the cases of issuing freight-forwarding documents on paper (registered with the Ministry of Justice on 26.05.2026 No. 86642). Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 389 of 31.08.2026 — amendments to Order No. 115. Standard charter-party forms BIMCO (Baltic and International Maritime Council) — the developer of standard charter-party forms and clauses. GENCON, NYPE, BALTIME, SHELLTIME, ASBATANKVOY and others — the standard forms of chartering contracts. International organisations IMO — the International Maritime Organization. ICC — the International Chamber of Commerce. Note: the site\'s materials are prepared on the basis of the literature cited and the author\'s practical experience in international logistics. The information is of a reference and educational nature and does not replace the advice of a specialised professional or the current text of a regulatory document.'},
  {t:'Market news — International Logistics', u:'news.html', text:'Home → Market news Market news Current logistics and shipping market news: ports and fleets, rail freight, freight rates, warehousing and foreign trade. The feed is refreshed automatically from RSS sources. 📰 Latest news Updated: . Sources: PortNews, InfraNews, Logirus, gCaptain, Splash 247, The Loadstar, РЖД-Партнер, SeaNews (RSS).'},
  {t:'Rail freight — International Logistics', u:'rail-freight.html', text:'Home → Rail freight Rail freight Rail transport is a reliable way to deliver bulk cargo over medium and long distances, especially within the former Soviet space and on the China–Europe corridor. Below are materials on consignment documents and the regulatory framework for rail carriage. Consignment documents SMGS (international carriage) and GU consignment notes (domestic and export/import carriage within Russia), processing via the ETRAN system. Regulatory framework SMGS Agreement, CIM/COTIF, OTIF, OSJD, Russian Railways Charter. Commercial report and general-form report Cases of drawing up, by whom and where they are made, the signing procedure and the content of the reports. Oversized and heavy cargo Instruction DCh-1835: loading gauge, zones and degrees of oversized cargo, the H index, approval procedure.'},
  {t:'Commercial report and general-form report — International Logistics', u:'railway-acts.html', text:'Home → Rail freight → Commercial report and general-form report Commercial report and general-form report The procedure for drawing up reports in the carriage of goods is established by the Rules for the Carriage of Goods and Empty Freight Wagons by Rail (approved by Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 256 of 27.07.2020, registered with the Ministry of Justice on 16.10.2020 No. 60411) — the chapter on drawing up reports. Commercial report Purpose and cases of drawing up The commercial report is drawn up to certify the circumstances provided for by Article 119 of the Russian Railways Charter (shortage, damage to (spoilage of) goods, discrepancy of the description/mass/number of packages with the data in the transport documents, etc.): on unloading at public places — on the day of unloading the goods, and where necessary — on the day of delivery of the goods to the consignee; on unloading at non-public places — on the day of unloading, with the goods being inspected in the course of unloading or immediately afterwards; en route — on the day the circumstances to be certified by a commercial report are discovered. If it is impossible to draw up the report within the stated time limits, it is drawn up within the following 24 hours . By whom and where it is drawn up, completion procedure The commercial report is drawn up by the carrier (at the station of departure, en route or at the station of destination). The report is made in three copies and completed without erasures, deletions or any corrections . Distribution of copies: at the station of destination: 1st — to the carrier for investigation; 2nd — to the consignee (recipient); 3rd — remains with the carrier at the station where it was drawn up; at the station of departure or en route: 1st — to the carrier for investigation; 2nd — attached to the transport document and kept until the station of destination; 3rd — remains with the carrier at the station where it was drawn up. The carrier makes a note in the consignment note on the drawing up of the commercial report at the station of departure, en route or at the station of destination, stating the reasons for drawing it up. What is stated in the report (content) The commercial report must contain, for each consignment: the wagon number and type; the number of seals (ZPU) and the seal markings; the number of packages and mass of goods stated in the transport documents; the number of packages and mass of goods actually present. When the mass is determined on wagon scales, the gross mass, the tare mass of the wagon (from the stencil or verified on scales) and the net mass are stated. Information on overloading of wagons is stated in an annex to the commercial report. Who signs it The commercial report at the station of destination is signed by the consignee (recipient) or their authorised representative (under a power of attorney), if they take part in the inspection of the goods, and by the carrier or its authorised representative. If they disagree with the content of the report, the carrier and the user of railway transport services have the right to state a dissenting opinion in it. When wagons are unloaded by the carrier at public places (where the goods are delivered after unloading), the report is signed only by the carrier or its authorised representative. Issuance of the report At the request of the consignee, the carrier must issue the commercial report within three days . Issuance to a representative — upon presentation of a power of attorney ; to a natural person — upon presentation of an identity document. If the carrier refuses to draw up the report, the consignee must file a written application within three calendar days (for foodstuffs and perishable goods — within one day); the carrier must reply within three days (for perishable goods — within one day). General-form report Purpose and cases of drawing up The general-form report is drawn up by the carrier (or the infrastructure owner) at stations to certify the facts and circumstances provided for by the Charter: detention of wagons en route, presence of wagons on public tracks, a defective seal, signs of access to the cargo, a technical defect of the wagon, etc. The time limit for drawing it up is on the day the circumstances are discovered , unless the chapter on reports provides otherwise. By whom it is drawn up and signed The general-form report is drawn up and signed by one authorised representative of the carrier who drew it up (except for the cases in clause 65 of the Rules). Reports related to the provision of public tracks and the use of the carrier\'s wagons are drawn up by the infrastructure owner (separately for the beginning and end of the detention) and signed by authorised representatives of the infrastructure owner and the carrier. For carriage with escort and guarding by the consignor/consignee, the report is also signed by the person escorting and guarding the goods (if authorised). If the user refuses to sign the report, a note of refusal is made in the report. Number of copies En route — two copies: the 1st is attached to the transport document, the 2nd remains with the carrier (a note is made in the consignment note). For the collection of fines/charges/fees — three copies: 1st — to the document on the assessment of charges; 2nd — issued to the user of the services; 3rd — remains with the party that drew up the report. On the discovery of a technical defect of a wagon/container en route — five copies: 1st — to the consignment note; 2nd — to the road statement; 3rd — to the consignor (or an authorised person); 4th — to the person repairing the defect; 5th — remains at the station where it was drawn up. On a technical defect of an empty wagon presented for loading — three copies (to the wagon owner/consignor, the carrier and the recipient). What is stated in the report (content) The general-form report sets out the circumstances that gave rise to its being drawn up, including: the presentation of uncleaned empty wagons/containers for loading (with the consignor\'s consent) — wagon numbers, the number and date of the contract; detention of wagons en route — the reason and time of detention; the discovery of a defective seal, signs of access to the cargo, defects threatening safety — information on the defects, the condition of the cargo, the seal and the loading; damage to carried cars, road trains, self-propelled machinery, shortage of parts and assemblies; the reasons and time of detention of wagons for the collection of charges for the use of wagons and public tracks; the time of notification of the presentation of wagons, the time of presentation, the time of beginning/end of the detention. Practical tip: the commercial report is the key document for filing a claim against the carrier for shortage of/damage to goods. Insist on it being drawn up immediately upon discovering discrepancies and check the accuracy of the information before signing — corrections in the report are not permitted.'},
  {t:'Carriage of oversized and heavy cargo (DCh-1835) — International Logistics', u:'railway-oversized.html', text:'Home → Rail freight → Oversized and heavy cargo Carriage of oversized and heavy cargo The Instruction on the Carriage of Oversized and Heavy Cargo on the Railways of the CIS Member States, the Republic of Latvia, the Republic of Lithuania and the Republic of Estonia (Instruction DCh-1835) was approved at the XXX session of the Council for Railway Transport on 19 October 2001 , with amendments at the XLIV session on 13 October 2006 (as amended, in force since 1 January 2007). It replaced Instruction No. TsD/4172 of 14.12.1983. The Instruction establishes the procedure and conditions for the carriage of oversized cargo and of all cargo on special wagons (transporters) on 1520 mm gauge railways, as well as the procedure for agreeing the carriage of oversized, heavy and long cargo in traffic with third countries. Loading gauge and oversized cargo Cargo on open rolling stock within the 1520 mm gauge network must not exceed the basic loading gauge ; for flat wagons and 4-axle gondola wagons a preferential loading gauge applies. The minimum permissible distance from the top of rail (ToR) level to the lower parts of the cargo (clearance) is not less than 150 mm . Cargo is oversized if, when placed on open rolling stock, it exceeds the outline of the basic loading gauge, or if its geometric projections in curves exceed those of the reference wagon (frame length 24 m, wheelbase 17 m). Zones and degrees of oversized cargo Oversize zone Height from ToR Degrees Lower 480–1229 mm (1626–1760 mm from the axis) and 1230–1399 mm (1626–2240 mm) 6 degrees Side 1400–4000 mm (inclusive) 6 degrees Upper 4001–5300 mm 3 degrees Cargo exceeding the limiting outline of the oversize zones (below 480 mm and above 5300 mm from ToR) is called super-oversized . Super-oversize above 5300 mm is vertical . Super-oversized cargo and cargo of the 6th degree of lower/side oversize is carried with a control frame . Oversize index (5 characters) For indication in the transport documents, an oversize index of 5 characters is introduced: 1st character — always the letter "N" (oversize); 2nd character — the degree of lower oversize (1–6); 3rd character — the degree of side oversize (1–6); 4th character — the degree of upper oversize (1–3); 5th character — vertical super-oversize (8). The absence of oversize in a zone is "0". Super-oversize in any zone is "8". Example: the index N8480 means lower and upper super-oversize, 4th-degree side oversize, no vertical super-oversize. Heavy cargo Heavy cargo is cargo whose mass or length (including in combination) or loads on the wagon frame (floor) exceed those permissible for carriage on universal rolling stock (Chapter 1 of the Rules for the Stowage and Securing of Cargo, Annex 14 to the SMGS). Procedure for agreeing carriage (3 stages) First stage — preliminary agreement of the possibility, method and conditions of carriage on the basis of the consignor\'s application with the supporting materials. Second stage — agreement by the railway administrations of the technical documentation (drawings and calculations of the stowage and securing of the cargo). Third stage — final agreement of the documentation for cargo of high degrees of oversize and super-oversized cargo, as well as all cargo on transporters (the Special Carriage Department of the Central Directorate for Traffic Management of JSC "Russian Railways"). Practical significance: for the carriage of oversized or heavy cargo, the key points are the oversize index (N0000–N8888) and the completed stages of agreement . Without agreement with the railways and, where necessary, with JSC "Russian Railways", such carriage is not permitted. Special passage conditions (shunting operations, placing in trains) are observed at stations and on sections.'},
  {t:'Air conventions — regulatory framework — International Logistics', u:'regulations-air.html', text:'Home → Air freight → Air conventions Air freight: conventions and rules Warsaw Convention (1929) The Convention for the Unification of Certain Rules relating to International Carriage by Air. It established the basis of the air carrier\'s liability, uniform requirements for the air waybill (AWB) and the presumption of the carrier\'s fault in the event of loss of or damage to cargo. Montreal Convention (1999) The modern convention that replaced the Warsaw system for most states. Key provisions: Liability limit for cargo — 22 SDR per kilogram (unless a value is declared). Stricter carrier liability and simplified rules for filing claims. The electronic air waybill (e-AWB) is permitted. Russia is a party to the Montreal Convention. IATA rules The International Air Transport Association (IATA) develops standards for cargo carriage: uniform AWB forms, cargo handling rules, e-freight and e-AWB standards, and requirements for dangerous goods (jointly with ICAO). ICAO — Chicago Convention (1944) The Convention on International Civil Aviation. It sets out the general principles of international air carriage. The ICAO annexes operate on its basis, including the rules for the carriage of dangerous goods by air (ICAO-TI, jointly with the IATA DGR). Practical significance: for air carriage the carrier\'s liability limit is 22 SDR/kg (Montreal Convention). If the cargo is more valuable, the cargo owner may declare its value in the waybill — the liability then corresponds to the declared value.'},
  {t:'Rail agreements — regulatory framework — International Logistics', u:'regulations-rail.html', text:'Home → Rail freight → Rail agreements Rail freight: agreements and rules SMGS (since 1951) The SMGS is the Agreement on International Goods Transport by Rail. It establishes uniform rules for the carriage of goods between the participating countries (CIS, the Baltics, China, Mongolia, North Korea, Vietnam, Iran and others). It defines the SMGS consignment note, the procedure for accepting/delivering goods, the liability of the railways and the claims procedure. CIM / COTIF (CIM Convention) CIM are the Uniform Rules concerning the Contract of International Carriage of Goods by Rail, part of the COTIF Convention. They are the equivalent of the SMGS for the countries of Western and Central Europe. For carriage between different legal systems, the combined CIM/SMGS consignment note is used. OTIF The Intergovernmental Organisation for International Carriage by Rail — the administrator of the COTIF Convention and the CIM rules. The equivalent of the role that the OSJD plays for the SMGS. OSJD The Organisation for Cooperation between Railways — it administers the SMGS and other agreements within the framework of the CIS/Asian railways. Russian Railways Charter The federal law governing rail carriage within Russia: the contract of carriage, the rights and obligations of the parties, the carrier\'s liability and the claims procedure. It is supplemented by the Rules for the Carriage of Goods by Rail. Practical significance: for export from Russia to Europe, the route often runs under two legal systems: within Russia — the SMGS, and after the bogie change at the border — CIM/COTIF. The combined CIM/SMGS consignment note allows the whole carriage to be documented by a single document.'},
  {t:'Road transport conventions — regulatory framework — International Logistics', u:'regulations-road.html', text:'Home → Road freight → Road conventions Road freight: conventions and rules International and domestic road carriage are governed by different legal regimes. Below are detailed reviews of the key conventions and rules, together with the regulatory framework for electronic transport documents. CMR Convention (1956) The contract for the international carriage of goods by road: the CMR consignment note, carrier liability, the 8.33 SDR/kg limit, limitation periods. TIR Convention (1975) The TIR Carnet and customs transit: distribution of sheets, the guarantee chain (up to €100,000), vehicle approval, eTIR. Rules for the carriage of goods (Government Resolution No. 2200) Domestic road carriage: the consignment note, loading/unloading, delivery times, reports, claims, liability. ETrN — regulatory framework Document What it establishes Federal Law No. 140-FZ of 07.06.2025 (as amended 04.08.2026 by Federal Law No. 302-FZ) the transition to electronic transport documents for all modes of transport (road, rail, air) and freight-forwarding documents; introduces the "GosLog" platform and the register of freight forwarders Road Transport Charter (259-FZ) basic concepts: GIS EPD, IS EPD, operator, electronic consignment note, etc.; art. 8 — the contract of carriage is confirmed by a consignment note generated as an ETrN Rules for the carriage of goods by road (Government Resolution No. 2200 of 21.12.2020) the form of the consignment note and the procedure for its completion/generation From 01.09.2026 the consignment note is generated as an ETrN. The cases where it is issued on paper are defined by a separate order of the Russian Ministry of Transport. Freight-forwarding documents (electronic) From 01.09.2026 freight-forwarding documents are also generated in electronic form (art. 2 of Federal Law No. 87-FZ as amended by 140-FZ) and submitted to the GIS EPD. The cases where they are issued on paper are defined by Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 115 of 03.04.2026 (as amended 31.08.2026; registered with the Ministry of Justice on 26.05.2026 No. 86642; valid until 01.09.2032). 7 cases in which freight-forwarding documents are issued on paper: goods for the needs of state authorities, national defence, state security and law and order, special and military cargo; in territories under high-alert or emergency regimes, a state of emergency, martial law, or the legal regime of a counter-terrorist operation; the client has no technical possibility to transmit an electronic document due to force majeure; the freight forwarder has no technical possibility to transmit an electronic document due to force majeure; services to a natural person for personal, family or household needs (not business activity); the client is a foreign legal or natural person; carriage of military and dual-use products within military-technical cooperation and export control (item 7 introduced by Order of the Russian Ministry of Transport No. 389 of 31.08.2026). The "GosLog" platform and the register of freight forwarders 140-FZ also introduced new mechanisms for regulating freight-forwarding activities: Register of freight forwarders — information on legal entities and individual entrepreneurs engaged in freight-forwarding activities is placed in a register of notifications. The right to operate arises from the day of inclusion in the register. Existing forwarders must file a notification within 60 days (from 01.03.2026). The "GosLog" platform — the national digital transport and logistics platform of Russia (a federal GIS, a "single window" principle). The operator is the federal executive authority in the transport sphere (the Russian Ministry of Transport) or a subordinate organisation. Data storage — a freight forwarder must store in the Russian Federation information on contracts, cargoes and routes for three years. Practical significance: international road carriage requires a CMR (plus a TIR Carnet for transit), while domestic carriage requires a TN/ETrN. Do not confuse them: the CMR applies to international carriage, the TN only within Russia.'},
  {t:'Maritime conventions — regulatory framework — International Logistics', u:'regulations-sea.html', text:'Home → Sea freight → Maritime conventions Sea freight: conventions and rules Hague Rules (1924) The International Convention for the Unification of Certain Rules of Law relating to Bills of Lading (Brussels, 1924). The first universal instrument that established the minimum liability of the sea carrier : the obligation to exercise due diligence to make the ship seaworthy, a list of 17 exceptions from liability, a limit of liability and a one-year time-bar. Hague-Visby Rules (1968) The 1968 Protocol that modernised the Hague Rules: the limit of liability was raised (in SDR per package or per kilogram) and the scope was extended to containerised carriage. These are the fundamental rules referenced by most modern charter-party forms and bills of lading. Hamburg Rules (1978) The UN Convention on the Carriage of Goods by Sea. They strengthened the carrier\'s liability: the "nautical fault" defence was removed, the limits were increased and the time-bar extended to two years. They apply to a limited extent. Rotterdam Rules (2008) The UN Convention on Contracts for the International Carriage of Goods Wholly or Partly by Sea. A modern regime covering door-to-door multimodal carriage and electronic documents. It has not yet entered into force. SOLAS Convention (1974) The International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea. Requirements for ship construction and safety. It includes the VGM requirement — the mandatory verification of the gross mass of a container before loading. IMDG Code The International Maritime Dangerous Goods Code: classification, packing, marking and carriage of dangerous goods by sea. Load Line Convention (1966) The International Convention on Load Lines defines the permissible draught and load capacity of a ship: the load line (Plimsoll mark) fixes the maximum loading, the permitted loads and the stability requirements. Responsibility rests with the shipowner. UN Convention on International Multimodal Transport of Goods (1980) The Convention on International Multimodal Transport of Goods. It defines the liability of the multimodal transport operator and a single document (CTD) for carriage by several modes of transport. It has been ratified by a limited number of states, but its principles are widely applied in practice. Merchant Shipping Code of the Russian Federation (KTM RF) The main national act in the field of Russian maritime transport (in force since 1999). It defines contracts of carriage by sea and chartering, the status of the bill of lading and the liability of the parties. Federal Law "On Seaports in the Russian Federation" (No. 261-FZ, 2007) It regulates the operation of Russian seaports, the procedure for port operations and tariffs for port services. EAEU Customs Code It defines the customs procedures for the import/export of goods, document flow and the procedure for paying customs duties. Since 2018 the EAEU Customs Code has been in force, replacing the Customs Code of the Russian Federation. Standard charter-party forms Standard (model) terms of chartering contracts developed by BIMCO and other organisations: Form Year Scope Particulars GENCON 1994 Voyage charter A universal form for all types of cargo. BALTIME 1939, rev. 1974 Time charter Hire of a vessel for a period, originally for Baltic trade. NYPE (New York Produce Exchange) 1946, rev. 2015 Time charter for dry cargo The industry standard for the dry-bulk fleet. ASBATIME — Time charter A form for Baltic Sea conditions. SHELLTIME 4 2003 Tanker time charter Developed by Shell, the standard for oil tankers. ASBATANKVOY — Tanker voyage charter For tanker carriage. CONTINOLOAD — Container voyage charter For container ships. Practical significance: in practice it is the Hague-Visby Rules that most often govern the carrier\'s liability under a bill of lading. Check which revision of the rules your B/L or charter-party refers to — the limits of liability and the time limits for claims depend on it.'},
  {t:'Road freight — International Logistics', u:'road-freight.html', text:'Home → Road freight Road freight Road transport is the most flexible way to deliver cargo "door to door", indispensable over short and medium distances. Below are materials on consignment documents and the regulatory framework for road carriage. Consignment documents CMR (international), the TN consignment note (domestic) and the electronic consignment note (e-CMR / ETrN). Regulatory framework CMR Convention, TIR Convention, Russian rules for road carriage of goods and the e-CMR regulatory framework.'},
  {t:'Rules for the Carriage of Goods by Road (Government Resolution No. 2200) — International Logistics', u:'road-transport-rules.html', text:'Home → Road freight → Rules for the carriage of goods by road Rules for the Carriage of Goods by Road (Government Resolution No. 2200) The Rules for the Carriage of Goods by Road are approved by Government Resolution No. 2200 of 21 December 2020 (in force since 1 January 2021). This is the principal subordinate act detailing the procedure for domestic carriage of goods by road in Russia: from the conclusion of the contract of carriage to the delivery of the goods and the filing of claims. The Rules are issued in pursuance of the Road Transport Charter (Federal Law No. 259-FZ of 08.11.2007). What the Rules govern Resolution No. 2200 establishes the procedure for organising the carriage of goods by road, including: conclusion of a contract of carriage of goods and a charter contract (freight contract) ; the procedure for providing vehicles and containers; tendering and acceptance of goods , loading and unloading; determination of the weight of the goods and sealing; delivery times and delivery of the goods ; specifics of carrying particular types of goods; the procedure for drawing up reports and filing claims . The consignment note (TN) Carriage of goods under a contract of carriage is formalised by a consignment note (TN) . Its form is approved by the Rules (Annex No. 4). Key rules: The TN is drawn up by the consignor (unless the contract of carriage provides otherwise) for each carriage. The note is made out in three copies : for the consignor, the consignee and the carrier. The TN is signed by the consignor and the carrier; the signatures record the taking over of the goods for carriage. It contains particulars of the consignor, consignee and carrier, the goods (description, number of packages, weight, declared value), the route, times and conditions of carriage. Link with the ETrN: from 01.09.2026 the consignment note is generated as an electronic consignment note (ETrN) . A paper TN is permitted only in the cases established by law. For details, see the road transport documents section. Acceptance, loading and unloading Loading of the goods onto the vehicle is, as a general rule, performed by the consignor , and unloading by the consignee , unless the contract provides otherwise. The carrier must provide a sound vehicle suitable for carrying the relevant goods within the agreed time. The weight of the goods is determined by the consignor (by weighing or calculation); the parties record it in the note. Goods requiring containers/packing are tendered in sound containers ensuring their preservation during carriage. After loading, the vehicle may be sealed — the seals are recorded in the note. Delivery times The time for delivery of the goods is determined by the contract of carriage . Where no time is fixed by the contract, the goods must be delivered within a reasonable time . For breach of the delivery time the carrier bears liability (a penalty for delay under the Road Transport Charter). Delivery of the goods The goods are delivered to the consignee named in the note at the destination. On delivery, the weight, number of packages and condition of the goods are checked. Where loss, shortage or damage to the goods, or a breach of the seals, is found on delivery, a report is drawn up — this is the basis for a subsequent claim and action. Reports: the procedure for drawing them up Reports are drawn up in circumstances that may give rise to the liability of the parties, in particular: loss, shortage, or damage to (spoilage of) the goods; failure to provide a vehicle or container; delay in delivery of the goods; delay in loading/unloading (excessive demurrage). A report is drawn up in the presence of the parties\' representatives and signed by them; where a party evades signing, a note to that effect is made. The report is attached to the claim. Claims and actions As a rule, a claim is presented to the carrier before an action is brought. The procedure and time limits are established by the Road Transport Charter (259-FZ): a claim against the carrier is presented within the established time limits (for example, for loss of goods — after the delivery time); the carrier must consider the claim and reply within 30 days ; the limitation period for claims arising from the carriage of goods by road is one year . Liability of the parties The liability of the carrier, consignor and consignee is established by the Road Transport Charter (259-FZ). The main provisions: For loss/shortage of the goods , the carrier compensates the value of the lost (missing) goods. For damage to the goods — the amount by which their value has declined. For delay in delivery — a penalty, as a rule, as a percentage of the carriage charge for each day of delay, but not exceeding the amount of the carriage charge. The consignor/consignee are liable for vehicle demurrage and other breaches provided for by the contract and the law. Practical significance: Resolution No. 2200 applies to domestic carriage by road within Russia. For international carriage, the CMR Convention applies (plus a TIR Carnet for customs transit). Do not confuse them: the TN/ETrN is for domestic carriage within Russia, the CMR for international carriage. ← Back to the road regulatory framework'},
  {t:'Sea freight — International Logistics', u:'sea-freight.html', text:'Home → Sea freight International sea freight Maritime transport is the backbone of international trade: about 90% of world cargo volume (by weight) moves by sea. It is the most cost-efficient way to deliver large consignments over long distances. Bill of lading (B/L) Functions, types and practical use of the bill of lading in sea carriage. Maritime conventions Hague Rules, Hague-Visby Rules, Hamburg Rules, Rotterdam Rules, SOLAS, IMDG Code, merchant shipping code. Chartering Types of contracts of carriage by sea, standard charter-party forms and commercial terms. Chartering operations technique Stages of a deal, correspondence, recaps and freight calculation examples. Chartering abbreviations A dictionary of 356 abbreviations used in chartering. Vessel types Container ships They carry standard containers and are classified by capacity (in TEU — twenty-foot equivalent units): Class Capacity Notes Feeder up to ~3,000 TEU short-haul, feeder services to major hubs Panamax ~3,000–4,500 TEU fits the old locks of the Panama Canal Post-Panamax ~5,000–9,000 TEU too wide for the old locks New Panamax ~10,000–14,000 TEU the new (expanded) locks of the Panama Canal ULCV / ULCS 18,000–24,000+ TEU ultra-large, only on the Asia–Europe trade Example: the largest ULCV-class container ship — MSC Irina MSC Irina (2023) is one of the largest container ships in the world, the lead vessel in a series of 24,000+ TEU ships built for Mediterranean Shipping Company (MSC). It operates on the main Asia–Europe route. Parameter Value Capacity 24,346 TEU Length 399.9 m Beam 61.3 m Draught ~16.5 m Deadweight ~228,700 t Speed ~22.5 knots Built Hudong-Zhonghua Shipyard (China), 2023 Flag Liberia Because of their dimensions (a 61 m beam) and draught, such vessels can only call at a limited number of deep-water hub ports with modern terminals and heavy-duty crane equipment. The 24,000+ TEU capacity is achieved by stacking containers up to 10–11 tiers in the hold and up to 12–13 tiers on deck. How to read a container\'s stowage position on board The position of each container on a container ship is defined by three coordinates — Bay / Row / Tier : Coordinate Meaning Numbering rule Bay Cross-section of the vessel, counted from bow to stern odd numbers (01, 03, 05…) — for 20-foot containers; even numbers (02, 04, 06…) — for 40-foot containers Row Transverse row from the centreline towards the sides even numbers (02, 04, 06…) — starboard; odd numbers (01, 03, 05…) — port Tier Vertical level from the bottom of the hold upwards even numbers (02, 04, 06…) — in the hold; 82, 84, 86… — on deck (numbering continues upward) Example of a stowage position: the container 034282 means — Bay 03 (a 20-foot bay), Row 42 (starboard), Tier 82 (first tier on deck). This code is used in cargo plans, terminal documents and load/discharge planning. Bulk carriers They carry dry-bulk cargoes (ore, coal, grain, fertilisers) and are classified by deadweight (DWT): Class Deadweight (DWT) Use Handysize 15,000–35,000 t small ports, versatility Supramax 35,000–60,000 t medium parcels, with deck cranes Panamax 60,000–80,000 t sized for the Panama Canal Capesize 100,000–400,000 t ore/coal, routes around the Cape of Good Hope Example: a Capesize bulk carrier — Valemax Valemax is a series of the world\'s largest ore carriers with a deadweight of ~400,000 t, built for the Brazilian mining company Vale to carry iron ore from Brazil to China. The lead vessel is Vale Brasil (2011). Parameter Value Deadweight ~400,000 t Length ~362 m Beam ~65 m Draught ~23 m Use iron ore, Brazil → China route Built 2011–2016 (China, South Korea, Philippines) Valemax vessels pass through neither the Panama nor the Suez Canal (the latter is too small for them when fully laden) and sail around the Cape of Good Hope. The huge size of a single voyage (up to 400,000 t of ore) gives the lowest cost per tonne, so such ships are only economically justified on stable, high-volume ore routes between the largest deep-water terminals. Other vessel types Tanker — liquid cargoes: crude oil and petroleum products, chemicals (chemical tanker), liquefied gas LNG/LPG (gas carrier). Ro-Ro — wheeled cargo: cars, trailers, special equipment (driven on board under their own power). General cargo / multipurpose vessel — break-bulk and general cargo in packaging, often with its own cranes. Reefer vessel — perishable cargo under controlled temperature. Heavy-lift vessel — oversized and extra-heavy equipment (project cargo). Container types ISO standard. The main sizes are 20 and 40 feet (TEU and FEU). Below are the most common types. Type Dimensions (L×W×H) Volume Payload 20\' DC (standard) 6.06 × 2.44 × 2.59 m ~33 m³ up to ~28 t 40\' DC (standard) 12.19 × 2.44 × 2.59 m ~67 m³ up to ~26.8 t 40\' HC (High Cube) 12.19 × 2.44 × 2.90 m ~76 m³ up to ~26.6 t 45\' HC (High Cube) 13.72 × 2.44 × 2.90 m ~86 m³ up to ~28 t Specialised containers Reefer — with a built-in refrigeration unit, for temperature-controlled cargo (from −35 °C to +25 °C). Open Top — opening top (tarpaulin), for cargo loaded from above by crane or over-height cargo. Flat Rack — a platform without walls or roof, for heavy and oversized cargo (machinery, equipment). Tank container — a tank within a container frame, for liquid cargoes. Open Side — opening side walls, for cargo that cannot be loaded through the doors. Practical tip: when choosing a container, check the weight limits — the payload depends on the type, as well as the carrier\'s and port\'s limits on the gross container weight. For heavy cargo (ore, metal), the volume is often not fully used — the weight is the limiting factor.'},
  {t:'TIR Convention (1975) — International Logistics', u:'tir-convention.html', text:'Home → Road freight → TIR Convention TIR Convention (1975) The TIR Convention (from French Transports Internationaux Routiers ) is the Customs Convention on the International Transport of Goods under Cover of TIR Carnets, concluded in Geneva on 14 November 1975 (entered into force on 20 March 1978). Russia is a party to the Convention; the national guaranteeing association is ASMAP (the Association of International Road Carriers). How it works The TIR Convention allows goods to be carried across several borders without intermediate customs inspection : the goods move in a sealed approved vehicle from the customs office of departure to the customs office of destination. Duties and charges are neither paid nor deposited en route — instead an international guarantee system operates. The goods are carried under customs seals and marks and, as a rule, are not inspected at intermediate customs offices. Import/export duties and charges are not paid or deposited at customs offices en route. It is used for transit carriage (goods move through the territory of states without being released into free circulation). The TIR Carnet The TIR Carnet is the customs transit document under which goods are carried under the TIR procedure. It is issued for a specific carriage (one vehicle/combination, one route) and consists of a cover, a set of detached vouchers and counterfoils. The carnet is issued to the carrier by the guaranteeing association (in Russia, ASMAP) after the carrier has been approved for the TIR system. Distribution of sheets under paper processing A standard TIR Carnet consists of a cover , sets of sheets (a detached voucher + a counterfoil) and an accident report . Each "voucher + counterfoil" pair has its own colour and purpose. As the carnet passes through customs offices, the sheets are distributed as follows: Sheet Colour Where it goes Cover yellow stays in the carnet; contains the holder\'s data, validity and the guaranteeing association Voucher No. 1 (goods) white remains at the customs office of departure (customs record of the goods) Voucher No. 1 (guarantee) green remains at the customs office of departure and is sent to the guaranteeing association (starts the guarantee chain) Counterfoil No. 1 — stays in the carnet Voucher No. 2 (goods) white remains at the customs office of destination Voucher No. 2 (guarantee) green remains at the customs office of destination and is sent to the guaranteeing association (closes the guarantee chain) Counterfoil No. 2 — stays in the carnet Additional sheets (where needed) yellow used by intermediate customs offices in the event of transloading/transfer Accident report — completed in the event of a road accident or irregularity The logic of distribution: the white vouchers are for the customs record (they remain at the customs offices of departure and destination); the green vouchers are for the guarantee chain (the customs office sends them to the guaranteeing association, which uses them to verify that the TIR operation has been properly terminated and that the guarantee may be discharged). The counterfoils stay in the carnet and, after the operation, are returned to the holder — proof that the TIR carriage has been completed. Guarantee chain and the guarantee limit Each TIR Carnet is covered by a guarantee of the national guaranteeing association (in Russia, ASMAP), acting in conjunction with the international union IRU and insurance companies. Where obligations to pay duties/charges arise during a TIR carriage and the carrier does not settle them, the guaranteeing association is answerable to the customs authorities. The international TIR guarantee limit is up to €100,000 per carnet (raised from the former €60,000). This amount is the ceiling of the guarantee chain\'s liability for a single TIR operation; the actual guarantee for a given carriage depends on the goods, the route and the amount of security. Approval of the carrier and the vehicle Carrier approval for the TIR system: the carrier must meet the Convention\'s requirements (reliability, no serious customs offences) and obtain a certificate of approval through the national guaranteeing association (ASMAP). Vehicle approval : the load compartment (container) must be constructed so that goods cannot be removed from or introduced into it without visible breaking of the seals. An approved vehicle receives a certificate of approval of a road vehicle for the carriage of goods under customs seals. eTIR — the electronic TIR Carnet eTIR is the digital version of the TIR Carnet, in which customs offices and guaranteeing associations exchange carriage data electronically (instead of paper vouchers). Its legal basis is Annex 11 to the TIR Convention (in force since 2021). As of 2026, eTIR is at the stage of pilot application and gradual expansion rather than full replacement of paper carnets: countries (including the members of the Organization of Turkic States) are extending the use of eTIR on individual routes, and pilot runs with the electronic e-CMR consignment note into Russia are planned for late 2026. The paper TIR Carnet remains the primary document on most routes. Russia\'s participation The Russian Federation is a party to the TIR Convention. The national guaranteeing association is ASMAP , which issues TIR Carnets, approves carriers for the system and liaises with the customs authorities (the Federal Customs Service of Russia) on the guarantee chain. Practical significance: a TIR Carnet is needed for the international transit of goods across borders under customs seals. It does not replace the CMR consignment note — these are different documents: the CMR records the contract of carriage, while the TIR Carnet covers customs transit. In practice, an international carriage of goods often uses both documents together. ← Back to the road regulatory framework'},
  {t:'TN VED and the HS code — International Logistics', u:'tnved-hs-code.html', text:'Home → Customs clearance → TN VED / HS code TN VED and the HS code The HS (Harmonized Commodity Description and Coding System) is an international classification developed by the World Customs Organization (WCO). It is used in more than 200 countries as the basis for customs tariffs. The EAEU TN VED (Commodity Nomenclature of Foreign Economic Activity of the Eurasian Economic Union) is the national (regional) extension of the HS used in Russia, Belarus, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan and Armenia. Structure of the 10-digit code The TN VED code consists of 10 digits divided into meaningful levels: Digits Level Example (8517 12 00 00) 1–2 Chapter 85 — electrical machinery and equipment 3–4 Heading 8517 — telephones, communication apparatus 5–6 Subheading (HS level, worldwide) 8517 12 — for cellular networks 7–8 EAEU subheading 8517 12 00 9–10 EAEU sub-subheading 8517 12 00 00 How the nomenclature is formed The first 6 digits are uniform worldwide — that is the level of the Harmonized System (HS). The 7th–10th digits are refined at the EAEU level and are used to set the rates of duties, VAT, excises and non-tariff regulation measures. The nomenclature contains 21 sections and 97 chapters (chapters 01–97); chapter 77 is reserved, and chapters 98–99 are for national use. The nomenclature is revised approximately every five years (HS 2022 is in force, HS 2027 is in preparation). Why correct classification matters The TN VED code directly determines: the rate of import/export customs duty; the amount of VAT and excises; non-tariff regulation measures — licensing, certification, quotas; prohibitions and restrictions on import/export. ⚠️ A classification error is costly: incorrect classification entails additional charges, fines under art. 16.2 of the Russian Code of Administrative Offences, delay of the cargo, and administrative or even criminal liability. Classification is the most responsible part of declaration, which is why it is performed by experienced declarants or customs representatives. How to classify a product Determine the functional purpose of the product and the material of manufacture. Find the appropriate chapter , then the heading and subheading. Use the Explanatory Notes to the TN VED (the official EEC reference) for accurate classification. In case of doubt, apply for a binding classification decision from the customs authority. Data source for the classifier — Alta-Soft services (alta.ru).'},
  {t:'UCP 600 — International Logistics', u:'ucp600.html', text:'Home → UCP-600 UCP 600 — Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits The UCP 600 (Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits) are the ICC (International Chamber of Commerce) rules for documentary credits, Publication No. 600. The current revision came into force on 1 July 2007 and replaced the previous revision UCP 500 (1993). The UCP 600 contains 39 articles grouped by subject. It is the set of rules that banks worldwide apply when handling documentary credits, provided the credit states that it is subject to the UCP. Nature and purpose A documentary credit (Letter of Credit, L/C) is an undertaking by the issuing bank, at the request of the buyer (applicant), to make payment to the seller (beneficiary) against presentation of documents complying with the terms of the credit. The UCP 600 standardises this procedure: it defines the terms, the document examination procedure, the time limits, the responsibility of banks and the settlement rules. Thanks to uniform rules, the credit becomes a reliable instrument of international settlement, where trust between the parties is replaced by trust in the bank and in the documents. Why and when it is used: a credit is used when the seller and the buyer are not prepared to rely on each other directly (a first transaction, expensive goods, political/currency risk). The buyer instructs its bank to issue the credit, the bank guarantees payment, and the seller receives the money against presentation of the required documents. It is used most often in international trade (in Russia — for import/export), and also domestically for large transactions. Key principles Independence principle — a credit is by its nature separate from the sale contract on which it is based. Banks are in no way concerned with such contract. Banks deal with documents, not with goods (art. 5) — banks examine only the documents, their formal completeness and compliance, not the goods themselves, their quality or the fact of shipment. Strict documentary compliance — the documents must exactly meet the terms of the credit; a discrepancy entitles the bank to refuse payment. Irrevocability — under UCP 600 a credit is irrevocable, even if this is not stated in its text (art. 3). Key terms and abbreviations Term / abbreviation Definition L/C (Letter of Credit) A documentary credit — an undertaking by a bank to make payment against complying documents. UCP Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits. ICC International Chamber of Commerce — the body that drafted the rules. Issuing Bank The bank that issues the credit at the request of the applicant. Applicant The buyer on whose instructions the credit is issued. Beneficiary The seller in whose favour the credit is issued. Advising Bank The bank that advises the credit to the beneficiary (usually the seller\'s bank). Confirming Bank The bank that adds its own undertaking to that of the issuing bank (a confirmed credit). Nominated Bank The bank authorised to honour the credit (payment, acceptance, negotiation). Complying Presentation A presentation of documents that complies with the terms of the credit and the UCP 600. Negotiation The purchase by the nominated bank of drafts and/or documents from the beneficiary against payment. Discrepancy A failure of the documents to comply with the terms of the credit, grounds for refusing payment. ISBP International Standard Banking Practice for the examination of documents under documentary credits. SWIFT MT700 The standard SWIFT message format for the issuance of a credit. At sight Payment on presentation (immediately after examination of the documents). By acceptance An acceptance credit — payment against an accepted draft at a specified date. By deferred payment A credit with deferred payment. Types of credit Criterion Type Brief description By confirmation Confirmed The confirming bank adds its own undertaking — payment is guaranteed even against the risk of the issuing bank. Unconfirmed Only the issuing bank bears the undertaking. By method of settlement At sight / by payment Payment immediately after a complying presentation. By acceptance The bank accepts the draft and pays it at maturity. By deferred payment Payment on a specified future date without a draft. By negotiation The nominated bank purchases documents/drafts from the beneficiary. By transferability Transferable May be transferred to a second beneficiary (art. 38). Non-transferable Cannot be transferred. Other Revolving Renews automatically for an amount/period as it is used. Standby L/C A guarantee-type credit — payment only upon non-performance of obligations. Key rules of the UCP 600 (by article) Examination of documents (art. 14) The bank must examine a presentation to determine whether it complies on its face. Data in the documents must not conflict with one another, with the credit or with the UCP 600. The bank has a maximum of 5 banking days following the day of presentation to examine the documents and decide whether the presentation is complying. Complying presentation and refusal (art. 15–16) Upon a complying presentation the bank must honour the undertaking. If discrepancies are found, the bank may refuse to honour (refusal is at the bank\'s discretion). The bank must give a single notice stating the discrepancies and whether the documents are being held or returned — otherwise it loses the right to claim that the documents are discrepant. Commercial invoice (art. 18) It is issued by the beneficiary and its amount must not exceed the amount of the credit (unless the bank has agreed otherwise). It must be issued in the same currency as the credit. Transport documents (art. 19–25) Bill of lading (art. 20), multimodal transport, air transport document (art. 23), road/rail documents. The document must indicate the carrier, be issued within the required period and correspond to the mode of shipment. A "clean" transport document — one without clauses about defects in the goods or packaging (art. 27). Insurance document (art. 28) The insurance document must cover the risks stated in the credit and be for not less than 110% of the value of the goods (unless otherwise stated). Tolerances in amount and quantity (art. 30) A tolerance of ±5% in amount/quantity is allowed where the quantity is not stated precisely (e.g. "about", "approximately"). Unless the credit states otherwise, partial shipments are allowed (art. 31). Transfer of a credit (art. 38) A transferable credit may be transferred to a second beneficiary (for example, an intermediary). Practical points for a logistics specialist Tip: when shipping under a credit, agree in advance with the buyer on the exact wording in the credit (description of goods, port of shipment, dates, required documents). Any discrepancy in the bill of lading, invoice or certificate is a risk of delay or refusal of payment. Most often, refusal is caused precisely by documentary discrepancies, not by problems with the goods themselves. The full current text of the rules is in the official ICC Publication No. 600, complemented by the International Standard Banking Practice (ISBP 745).'},
  {t:'VAT on export and import to/from Russia — International Logistics', u:'vat-export-import.html', text:'Home → VAT on export and import VAT on export from and import to the Russian Federation Taxation at the 0% rate applies to the following sales (cl. 1 of art. 164 of the Russian Tax Code): 1. Exported goods The 0% rate applies to goods: removed under the export customs procedure; placed under the free customs zone customs procedure; removed under the re-export procedure (including products of processing in the customs territory, waste and residues); removed from a free customs zone / free warehouse; removed from the territory of the Russian Federation to the territory of an EAEU member state (under the EAEU Treaty of 29.05.2014); sold by Russian organisations or individual entrepreneurs to natural persons from abroad (from a warehouse in a foreign state), with delivery to an address outside the EAEU. The condition is the submission to the tax authorities of the documents provided for by art. 165 of the Russian Tax Code . 2. Services for the international carriage of goods International carriage is carriage by sea-going or river vessels, vessels of mixed (river–sea) navigation, aircraft, rail transport and road vehicles , in which the point of departure or the point of destination is located outside the territory of the Russian Federation. The 0% rate extends to the services of Russian organisations and individual entrepreneurs: for the provision of railway rolling stock and/or containers for international carriage; freight-forwarding services (FFS) under a freight-forwarding contract for the organisation of international carriage. What is included in freight-forwarding services Negotiations on sale-and-purchase contracts, completion of documents, acceptance and delivery of goods, organisation and performance of carriage, delivery and removal of goods, loading/unloading and warehouse services, information services, cargo insurance, payment and financial services, customs clearance, forwarding escort, development and agreement of technical conditions for loading and securing of cargo, tracing of cargo, control of the completeness of shipment, re-marking, servicing and repair of containers, servicing of refrigerated containers, and storage of goods. ⚠️ When the 0% rate does NOT apply. The zero rate does not extend to services for the carriage of exported/imported goods, or to FFS for such carriage, if simultaneously : 1) the organisation/individual entrepreneur performs the carriage only between points in the Russian Federation ; 2) the organisation/individual entrepreneur is not indicated in the transport, consignment and/or other documents confirming the export/import of goods as one of the carriers . Particulars of rail carriage The 0% rate does not apply to the services of Russian rail carriers indicated in subcl. 9 of cl. 1 of art. 164 of the Russian Tax Code. It applies to services in the organisation and performance of rail carriage from the place of arrival of the goods in the Russian Federation (from ports or border stations) to the station of destination in the Russian Federation . 3. FFS and railway rolling stock on export (within Russia) The 0% rate applies to FFS and services for the provision of railway rolling stock and containers for the carriage of exported (re-exported) goods by rail, provided that the point of departure and the point of destination are in the Russian Federation . 4. Inland waterway transport The 0% rate applies to the works (services) of inland waterway organisations in respect of goods removed under the export/re-export procedure, for carriage within the Russian Federation from the point of departure to the point of unloading or transshipment onto sea-going vessels or other modes of transport. 5. Air carriage (transit with a landing in Russia) The 0% rate applies to services for the carriage of goods by aircraft, where the point of departure and the point of destination are outside the Russian Federation, with a landing in the Russian Federation, if the place of arrival of the goods in the Russian Federation and the place of departure from the Russian Federation coincide . Summary by mode of transport Type of carriage VAT rate Condition International (rail, road, sea, air) 0% point of departure or destination — outside Russia FFS for international carriage 0% freight-forwarding contract Rail rolling stock and containers (international) 0% provision for international carriage FFS / rail rolling stock on export (within Russia) 0% points of departure and destination — in Russia Domestic carriage (not international) 20% both points in Russia, without a link to export/import Source: cl. 1 of art. 164 of the Russian Tax Code (as amended 04.08.2026, with amendments and additions in force from 01.10.2026). The article is extensive and contains numerous amendments — in the event of questions on taxation at the 0% rate, refer to the full version of art. 164 of the Russian Tax Code. Documents to confirm the 0% rate (art. 165 of the Russian Tax Code) For services for the international carriage of goods (subcl. 2.1 of cl. 1 of art. 164 of the Russian Tax Code) the taxpayer confirms the right to the 0% rate by submitting to the tax authority (cl. 3.1 of art. 165 of the Russian Tax Code): a contract (copy of the contract) for the provision of the services — with a foreign or Russian person; copies of the transport, consignment and/or other documents confirming the export of the goods from the Russian Federation (import into the Russian Federation) — unless otherwise provided by subcl. 3 of cl. 3.1 of art. 165. Which documents exactly — by mode of transport Transport Export Import Sea / river shipping order + bill of lading / sea waybill (port of discharge — outside the EAEU) bill of lading / sea waybill (port of loading — outside the EAEU) Air air waybill (airport of discharge/transshipment — outside the EAEU) air waybill (airport of loading — outside the EAEU) Road copy of the transport document under which the goods were exported copy of the transport document under which the goods were imported Rail electronic register (DT number + information from the consignment document) electronic register (number of the transit declaration / DT + information from the consignment document) Carriage with EAEU countries For export from the Russian Federation to the territory of an EAEU member state, or import from the EAEU territory (by sea, river, aircraft, rail and road transport), copies of transport documents are submitted indicating the place of discharge or loading (station of destination/departure) in the territory of the other EAEU state . Rail carriage from the place of arrival to the station of destination in Russia For the organisation and performance of rail carriage from the place of arrival of the goods in the Russian Federation (from ports or border stations) to the station of destination in the Russian Federation, the following are submitted: a register in electronic form with information from the documents under which the goods arrived at the port / border station, plus information from the consignment document on the carriage to the station of destination; a register with information on the registration number of the transit declaration or the goods declaration. Time limits and nuances: the procedure with electronic registers for rail (subcl. 3 of cl. 3.1 of art. 165) was introduced by Federal Law No. 92-FZ of 22.04.2024 and applies to tax periods beginning from 01.04.2025 . In the event of a discrepancy in (or absence of) information, the tax authority may request copies of documents — they are submitted within 30 calendar days from the date of the request (cl. 1.2 of art. 165 of the Russian Tax Code). Case law on art. 165 of the Russian Tax Code The highest courts consistently state: in deciding on the 0% rate, the economic substance of the transaction (its link to export) is decisive, not the formal conformity of the documents. A formal approach to refusing the 0% rate is impermissible. Key acts of the highest courts Ruling of the Constitutional Court of the Russian Federation No. 31-P of 30.06.2020 (the "Gazpromneft Trading GmbH" case): the 0% rate applies even if the taxpayer provided services only at the domestic stage of international carriage due to unforeseen circumstances beyond the parties\' control (a vessel accident, transshipment, cancellation of the bill of lading) — provided the link to the completed export transaction is confirmed. A cancelled bill of lading does not lose its evidentiary value. Resolution of the Plenum of the Supreme Arbitration Court of the Russian Federation No. 65 of 18.12.2007 : documents with insignificant defects of form or content cannot be regarded as not submitted; the significance of the defects is assessed by the court. This is the basis for confirming the 0% rate through the court. Resolution of the Presidium of the Supreme Arbitration Court of the Russian Federation No. 6031/08 of 11.11.2008 : a taxpayer cannot be fined for non-payment of VAT if the export is confirmed after 180 days — the grounds for a fine under art. 122 of the Russian Tax Code cease to exist. Practical theses Thesis Source Documents certified by facsimile are not accepted Commercial Court of the Far Eastern District No. F03-5752/2014 of 19.01.2015 A 180-day delay can be confirmed (declaration for the period when the time limit expired) Commercial Court of the North Caucasus District No. A32-33491/2014 of 17.09.2015 An incorrect HS code in the declaration is not grounds for refusing 0% Commercial Court of the Volga District No. F06-70344/2020 of 11.03.2021 Provisional declaration: a full one is required (Ministry of Finance position) Letter of the Ministry of Finance No. 03-07-08/60478 of 24.08.2018 Provisional declaration: the type is not set, except pipelines/power lines (FTS position) Letter of the Federal Tax Service No. SD-4-3/9908@ of 24.05.2019 No fine when confirmed after 180 days Presidium of the Supreme Arbitration Court No. 6031/08 of 11.11.2008 Insignificant document defects are not grounds for refusal Plenum of the Supreme Arbitration Court No. 65 of 18.12.2007 Economic substance matters more than form Constitutional Court No. 31-P of 30.06.2020'},
];